\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Becoming Increasingly Popular Funding Option For Gold Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2011\par 23:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Traders increasingly betting on gold in yen terms\par \par \uc2 -Yen-denominated gold interest increasing since earthquake\par \par \uc2 -Trade captures both strength in gold, weakness in yen vs. dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Stephen L. Bernard \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--With gold's allure as a safe haven rising while that of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fades, traders are increasingly seeking a double-whammy from these twin trends by betting on the precious metal in yen terms rather than dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants say the popularity of the trade has grown since last month's devastating earthquake and tsunami in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and following the ensuing globally coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Group of Seven nations to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think it's wiser to be short yen than the dollar," said Dennis Gartman, a trader and publisher of The Gartman Letter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The day after central banks around the world \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Gartman initiated a "several million" dollar trade that is essentially a yen-denominated gold investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "made it abundantly clear you need to own gold in yen terms above all else," Gartman said. "They are telling you to buy gold" because they are flooding the market with yen and will need to continue to print money to help rebuild the devastated country, Gartman added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, William Rhind, strategic director of business development in the U.S. for ETF Securities, which sponsors one of the world's largest physical gold exchange traded funds, said his firm had seen increasing inquiries about buying gold in yen from investors. Mostly the inquiries are coming from sophisticated financial advisors and hedge funds in the U.S. and Europe, he said, describing it as an "opportunistic" strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculative short positions in the yen, or bets the yen will fall, have increased in the past three weeks by 37%, according to weekly data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission. That could be an indication that hedge funds, which are counted among speculative investors, are ramping up their anti-yen bets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen-denominated gold trade provides an added opportunity for investors who are bullish about both gold and the dollar--with latter possibly getting a boost from the prospect of tighter U.S. monetary policy toward year-end. That's because purchasing the precious metal in another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  essentially removes the anti-dollar bet inherent in traditional gold investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A yen-denominated gold trade takes two steps, Gartman said. First, gold is purchased through an ETF or in futures markets. Then an equally large dollar-yen swap is completed to fully wipe out the dollar exposure, giving an investor essentially a yen-denominated gold investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Gartman, the trade has yielded a bigger profit than a traditional dollar-denominated gold investment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June gold futures have risen about 3% against the dollar since March 18, the day central banks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. During that same time, the dollar has gained about 6% against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any investors buying gold in yen terms would have captured that extra 6% \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gain, helping these traders to nearly triple their profits in the past couple of weeks. A traditional buyer of gold in dollars would have missed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  component.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The increased interest in yen-denominated gold is similar to euro-denominated gold buying that twice flared up in 2010 when the 17-nation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc's sovereign debt crisis started to swell, ETF Securities' Rhind said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold consistently rose during that period as well, while the euro dropped against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stephen L. Bernard, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4528; stephen.bernard@dowjones.com [ 04-06-11 1750ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110406e746000s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
