\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: CIMB Keeps Malaysia's 2011 Exports Forecast At 8.5%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,935 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2011\par 02:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] CIMB Economic Research keeps Malaysia's 2011 export growth forecast at 8.5% on-year (vs +15.6% in 2010); this is despite a rebound in electronic shipments propelling Malaysia's export growth to 10.7% on-year in February (vs +4.6% in January), the fastest on-year pace in seven months. "It beat our estimate of 4.3% and consensus forecasts of 5.0%. That said, the 7.5% growth in January-February exports suggests a slowing in the growth of the external sector's contribution to 1Q GDP growth," says CIMB; the house also says export growth in the near term will be dented by Japan's earthquake-tsunami as Japan is Malaysia's third largest trading partner, taking up 11.8% of its exports. "The potential slowdown in global demand due to persistent high oil prices also poses a short-term risk to Malaysia's exports," CIMB says; it adds that the high base in March 2010 could push overall export growth into negative territory. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] The lead JGB futures contract may fall to 139.06 following weakness in U.S. Treasurys and amid continued concerns that the government may be forced to increase supply ahead, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. The contract is currently down 0.09 at 139.18. "The government is doing its best to show that it is trying to avoid additional debt issuance heading into the supplementary budget formation, but the market's distrust isn't going away quickly," Miura says. The benchmark 10-year JGB yield is up 0.5 bp at 1.275% and he tips the yield to trade in a 1.260%-1.285% range. "One must be aware that the winds are blowing against the bond market from various angles now."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Measures introduced by Bank Indonesia in June don't seem to have significantly discouraged fund inflows, the IMF says. "The measures were effective in sharply reducing foreign participation in SBIs initially, as well as dampening market volatility. After a short period, however, foreign ownership of SBIs actually rose above pre-holding period levels," it adds. Foreigners have also increased government bond holdings. The latest data shows foreign ownership in Bank Indonesia's SBI paper rose to IDR77.4 trillion ($8.9 billion) or 34% of the total amount in March vs IDR41.80 trillion (15.5% of total amount) in June. Still, the absolute amount was still lower than IDR82.99 trillion in April 2010 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reducing SBI issuance and shifting to term deposits, which are not accessible to foreigners, to manage liquidity. Analysts expect Bank Indonesia may extend the SBI holding period from one month currently if inflows are still strong. But, such move is unlikely soon as it still needs a stronger rupiah to help contain inflation. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is steady at 3.0250 in quiet trade as investors tread carefully and take time to assess their positions after China's rate hike, says a local brokerage dealer. "I think people are cautious and they will keep a close tab on developments in Europe to see if the ECB will raise rates later this week," says the dealer; he adds, the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not spotted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market yet "but I won't be surprise if they step in later at 3.0250 if selling (on the USD/MYR) intensifies." He tips the pair to trade in a 3.0250-3.0300 range. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 ("MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 May Rise On Materials Strength," at 2349 GMT, misstated the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may rise slightly Wednesday on the back of continued strength in materials stocks, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs rising to equivalent A$47.62 vs A$47.55 locally. Replacement buying of BHP shares during the pricing period for the A$5 billion off-market buyback is expected to remain a feature this week. The main caveat is how China's share market reacts to the People's Bank of China's quarter percent tightening. However, overnight strength in materials suggests the rate hike was anticipated. Some major brokers including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  have taken a more optimistic view on China's monetary policy/inflation/growth outlook recently. "The market is looking through that (Chinese tightening) to some extent, with the view that we're getting close to the end of the tightening," says a senior institutional trader. Otherwise there's focus on Leighton (LEI.AU) after the Age said a A$5.7 billion desalination plant being built in Victoria state by Leighton unit Thiess is falling behind schedule and will be a key factor in an expected profit downgrade for Leighton. February home loans data are due at 0130 GMT and could have implications for banks. S&P/ASX 200 last 4900.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is 0.1% lower at 3466.34 as the market takes its lead from offshore with the Dow closing down 0.1%, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Peter Young. "It's going to be steady-as-it-goes this afternoon," he says. Telecom (TEL.NZ) is down 0.7% at NZ$2.045 after bouncing to NZ$2.065 Monday from a recent low of NZ$1.965. \uc2 The Warehouse Group\uc2  (WHS.NZ) is down 4.5% at NZ$3.39 and \uc2 Ebos Group Ltd\uc2 . (EBO.NZ) is down 2.0% at NZ$7.50 after both went ex-dividend. Young says the market isn't seeing much profit-taking, with Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) remaining very strong, down just 0.1% at NZ$9.40. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0012 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices face mixed leads after U.S. wheat futures settled narrowly mixed overnight as traders assessed whether markets have adequately factored in weather threats. "I think the market is just pausing to reflect on the fact that it's already had a lot of bullish news fed to it," says Dan Manternach, agriculture services director at Doane Advisory Services. CBOT May wheat fell 3.75 cents to US$7.8625/bushel, KCBT May rose 2.0 cents to $9.50 and MGE May rose 0.75 cent to $9.6225. AUD/USD last 1.0333, down a little from this time Tuesday. On Tuesday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January next year in New South Wales jumped A$11 to A$314/metric ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0011 GMT [Dow Jones] PBOC's interest rate hike Tuesday could provide a good test to the HK stock market's current strength--on one hand, it could be viewed as continued tightening efforts by Chinese authorities, but another way to interpret the hike is that China's tightening cycle may be close to an end, and investors may view this with some relief. The second scenario is perhaps more likely with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , for example, expecting one more rate hike with a higher risk of no more hikes, instead of two or more hikes. Separately, Daiwa says its base-case forecasts include only one more 50bp hike in the reserve requirement ratio later this year (possibly in late April or May), and "that should mark the end of monetary tightening for this year." The HSI ends up 1.5% at 24,150.58 Monday, completing a four-day, 1,090 points, or 4.7%, rise. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatile trading is expected in base metals markets as China returns from a two-day holiday, during which the People's Bank of China raised benchmark lending and deposit rates by 0.25 percentage point, the fourth hike since October, in an attempt to slow inflation, according to market participants. "It will be quite interesting when Chinese comes back," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Daniel Briesemann. "A further correction is quite possible." Analysts have shown some concern for demand in China following the interest rate increase there yesterday, says a major market participant who requests anonymity. LME 3-month copper settled $60 higher Tuesday at $9,389/metric ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to extend its consolidation for now after settling down 13 cents Tuesday at $108.34/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bullish, but the stochastic measure has turned bearish at the overbought level. Support is at $107.50 (Tuesday's low); a breach would expose the downside to $106.30 (Friday's low), then to $104.12 (Thursday's low), $103.44 (March 30 low) and $102.70 (March 29 reaction low). Resistance is at $108.60 (Tuesday's high), then at $108.78 (30-month high set Monday); a breach would reinstate the near-term positive bias, exposing the upside to the psychological $110 level, then to $110.45 (the Sept. 22, 2008 reaction high) and $117.56 (the projected top of the ascending channel formed with the Feb. 12, 2009 low of $33.55 and the June 11, 2009 high of $73.23). May crude is down 41 cents at $107.93/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW may decline from 1,090.20 late Tuesday in Seoul on continued foreign net buying of local shares, participants say. China's rate hike on Tuesday for the fourth time since October may have only a limited impact on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as traders focus more on the economic recovery in advanced economies, including the U.S., says Samsung Futures; a China rate hike is usually negative for local stocks and tends to support USD/KRW. A local bank trader tips a 1,082-1,092 band for the session; the pair is likely to open lower following one-month NDFs that ended lower in New York and is expected to move in a tight range amid the lack of fresh catalysts, the trader says. He adds the market will likely watch for any impact on the USD/KRW from USD sales related to short-JPY orders following recent gains in USD/JPY. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com) [ 04-05-11 2034ET ]\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is at 85.02 with resistance tipped at 85.50 for the global day; investors expect some profit-taking orders to come in shortly and the pair will be capped as a result, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. "The dollar is getting close to the high of the post September \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " of Y85.94, which he thinks should prove a strong psychologically resistance. Although a USD/JPY breach above 86.00 won't be easy, conditions for the move are falling in place, especially considering orders from yen-hungry Japanese exporters are falling in the aftermath of the March 11 quake. "The market's dynamics are changing, and we may see the dollar above Y90 next month," he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 04-05-11 2034ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110406e7460000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
