\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lead JGB Futures May Fall To 139.06 - Mizuho\b0\par\par\uc2 1,973 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2011\par 02:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] The lead JGB futures contract may fall to 139.06 following weakness in U.S. Treasurys and amid continued concerns that the government may be forced to increase supply ahead, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. The contract is currently down 0.09 at 139.18. "The government is doing its best to show that it is trying to avoid additional debt issuance heading into the supplementary budget formation, but the market's distrust isn't going away quickly," Miura says. The benchmark 10-year JGB yield is up 0.5 bp at 1.275% and he tips the yield to trade in a 1.260%-1.285% range. "One must be aware that the winds are blowing against the bond market from various angles now."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Measures introduced by Bank Indonesia in June don't seem to have significantly discouraged fund inflows, the IMF says. "The measures were effective in sharply reducing foreign participation in SBIs initially, as well as dampening market volatility. After a short period, however, foreign ownership of SBIs actually rose above pre-holding period levels," it adds. Foreigners have also increased government bond holdings. The latest data shows foreign ownership in Bank Indonesia's SBI paper rose to IDR77.4 trillion ($8.9 billion) or 34% of the total amount in March vs IDR41.80 trillion (15.5% of total amount) in June. Still, the absolute amount was still lower than IDR82.99 trillion in April 2010 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reducing SBI issuance and shifting to term deposits, which are not accessible to foreigners, to manage liquidity. Analysts expect Bank Indonesia may extend the SBI holding period from one month currently if inflows are still strong. But, such move is unlikely soon as it still needs a stronger rupiah to help contain inflation. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is steady at 3.0250 in quiet trade as investors tread carefully and take time to assess their positions after China's rate hike, says a local brokerage dealer. "I think people are cautious and they will keep a close tab on developments in Europe to see if the ECB will raise rates later this week," says the dealer; he adds, the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not spotted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market yet "but I won't be surprise if they step in later at 3.0250 if selling (on the USD/MYR) intensifies." He tips the pair to trade in a 3.0250-3.0300 range. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 ("MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 May Rise On Materials Strength," at 2349 GMT, misstated the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may rise slightly Wednesday on the back of continued strength in materials stocks, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs rising to equivalent A$47.62 vs A$47.55 locally. Replacement buying of BHP shares during the pricing period for the A$5 billion off-market buyback is expected to remain a feature this week. The main caveat is how China's share market reacts to the People's Bank of China's quarter percent tightening. However, overnight strength in materials suggests the rate hike was anticipated. Some major brokers including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  have taken a more optimistic view on China's monetary policy/inflation/growth outlook recently. "The market is looking through that (Chinese tightening) to some extent, with the view 