d provide a good test to the HK stock market's current strength--on one hand, it could be viewed as continued tightening efforts by Chinese authorities, but another way to interpret the hike is that China's tightening cycle may be close to an end, and investors may view this with some relief. The second scenario is perhaps more likely with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , for example, expecting one more rate hike with a higher risk of no more hikes, instead of two or more hikes. Separately, Daiwa says its base-case forecasts include only one more 50bp hike in the reserve requirement ratio later this year (possibly in late April or May), and "that should mark the end of monetary tightening for this year." The HSI ends up 1.5% at 24,150.58 Monday, completing a four-day, 1,090 points, or 4.7%, rise. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatile trading is expected in base metals markets as China returns from a two-day holiday, during which the People's Bank of China raised benchmark lending and deposit rates by 0.25 percentage point, the fourth hike since October, in an attempt to slow inflation, according to market participants. "It will be quite interesting when Chinese comes back," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Daniel Briesemann. "A further correction is quite possible." Analysts have shown some concern for demand in China following the interest rate increase there yesterday, says a major market participant who requests anonymity. LME 3-month copper settled $60 higher Tuesday at $9,389/metric ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2356 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to extend its consolidation for now after settling down 13 cents Tuesday at $108.34/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bullish, but the stochastic measure has turned bearish at the overbought level. Support is at $107.50 (Tuesday's low); a breach would expose the downside to $106.30 (Friday's low), then