ions for the move are falling in place, especially considering orders from yen-hungry Japanese exporters are falling in the aftermath of the March 11 quake. "The market's dynamics are changing, and we may see the dollar above Y90 next month," he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 04-05-11 2023ET ]\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may rise slightly Wednesday on the back of continued strength in materials stocks, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs rising to equivalent A$47.62 vs A$47.55 locally. Replacement buying of BHP shares during the pricing period for the A$5 billion off-market buyback is expected to remain a feature this week. The main caveat is how China's share market reacts to Bank of China's quarter percent tightening. However, overnight strength in materials suggests the rate hike was anticipated. Some major brokers including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  have taken a more optimistic view on China's monetary policy/inflation/growth outlook recently. "The market is looking through that (Chinese tightening) to some extent, with the view that we're getting close to the end of the tightening," says a senior institutional trader. Otherwise there's focus on Leighton (LEI.AU) after the Age said a A$5.7 billion desalination plant being built in Victoria state by Leighton unit Thiess is falling behind schedule and will be a key factor in an expected profit downgrade for Leighton. February home loans data are due at 0130 GMT and could have implications for banks. S&P/ASX 200 last 4900.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 04-05-11 2023ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110406e7460000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
