\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-BOJ set to retain easy monetary policy-IMF\b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2011\par 00:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * IMF sees BOJ maintaining accommodative stance\par \par \uc2 * IMF to cut 2011 growth forecast for Japan\par \par \uc2 * Repatriation flows won't be big driver of yen\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, background throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Lesley Wroughton\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan is likely to maintain its easy monetary policy stance when it meets next week, a senior International Monetary Fund official said Friday ahead of an April 6-7 meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mahmood Pradhan, IMF mission chief to Japan, said Japan's devastating earthquake would hit growth over the short-term, although reconstruction efforts could produce a sharp recovery next year for the country.\par \par \uc2 The IMF is expected to cut its 2011 forecast for Japanese growth when it unveils updated figures on April 11 in its World Economic Outlook, said Pradhan, who is also a senior advisor in the IMF's Asia and Pacific Department.\par \par \uc2 "It would be appropriate of the BOJ to maintain a very accommodative stance," Pradhan told Reuters Insider. "I think that is what they will do."\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to revise down its economic assessment when it meets on Wednesday in the wake of the nuclear crisis and power outages.\par \par \uc2 The dominant market view is that it will stand pat on monetary policy although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expressed its readiness to ease policy if the effects of the disaster threatens Japan's fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 Pradhan said there had been an impressive coordinated effort by Japanese agencies to address the immediate humanitarian needs in the aftermath of the disaster.\par \par \uc2 He said the authorities have injected more than 40 trillion yen into the economy to keep financial markets stable, which is essential for a smooth recovery.\par \par \uc2 "I think they have done that and I think we should see them continuing to do that," said Pradhan.\par \par \uc2 He said short-term movements in the Japanese yen were hard to explain, but dismissed the role of speculators in driving up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think the yen in the longer term sense is where it should be," Pradhan said. "The yen reflects the underlying performance of Japan's economy."\par \par \uc2 He said there was no evidence the repatriation of funds to pay for reconstruction costs was driving up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Our assessment is that the yen repatriation flows over the medium term will not make a material difference to value of the yen," Pradhan said. "Put it another way, we do not see repatriation flows to be the big driver of the yen."\par \par \uc2 Pradhan said the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 18 of the Group of Seven large economies to stem the rise of the yen was "fully appropriate" and said further steps could not be ruled out.\par \par \uc2 "What is clear ... was that the excess volatility in a very short space of time was really unhelpful for Japan's efforts to rebuild," he said. "In that sense, the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fully appropriate, but that's not to say that we can sit here and think about on what criteria similar types of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can take place."\par \par \uc2 Pradhan said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had "ample" resources to finance the rebuilding of the country and may end up costing the government somewhere in the region of about 1 percent to 2 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 "That cost is manageable," he said, noting it was a temporary increase in public sector expenditure.\par \par \uc2 He said the Japanese government was exploring ways to fund the rebuilding, including through changes in spending priorities, changes in the country's tax structures and by borrowing money.\par \par \uc2 "All of these are in play," said Pradhan. "They have not decided exactly what proportion they will use of the three .. but all of these are available and all are within the feasible set of what Japan can do."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Leslie Adler; and Andre Grenon)\par \par \uc2 IMF-JAPAN/ (UPDATE 1, INTERVIEW, TV)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110404e741001ms\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
