\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan CB More Likely To Pause Than Hasten Hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] The Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to continue with moderate 12.5bp hikes, but has a greater likelihood of a pause instead of a more aggressive hike, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "In general, the CBC (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China) thinks current monetary policy is adequate to contain inflation expectation and maintain price stability." The house adds "downside risks from MENA (Middle East and North Africa) geopolitical uncertainty and short-term supply chain impact from Japan earthquake...could also affect Taiwan's exports and industrial production." On Thursday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised key policy rates by 12.5 bps, in its fourth consecutive hike. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are down 0.3% at 459.12 with financial stocks leading losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday said Vietnam is expected to cut public investment by VND50 trillion, or 7.4% of this year's revised budget expense. Dealers say many investors are staying on the sidelines because of weak fund inflows. "Sentiment is weak because of two major factors: the government is reducing spending and banks are charging high interest rates," says a Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Among decliners, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) is down 1.4% at VND13,800, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) is down 2%% at VND30,600 and \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2 (PVF.VH) is down 1% at VND20,400. The trader predict immediate support at 455. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are flat at 8679.04 ahead of a four-day weekend, with losses in Acer (2353.TW) and profit-taking in banking stocks offset by gains in tech heavyweights HTC (2498.TW) and Hon Hai (2317.TW). Andrew Teng, an analyst at Taiwan International Securities, tips the Taiex to meet strong resistance at 8700, while 8600 remains firm support for the day. Acer is down 2.0% at NT$58.80, plauged by the departure of its CEO and lingering concerns on PC market outlook. Banks fall as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 12.5 bps interest rate hike Thursday is already priced in, Teng adds; Cathay Financial (2882.TW) is down 1.3% at NT$47.90, Fubon Financial (2881.TW) is down 0.4% at NT$38.90. HTC continues to soar, adding 2.2% to NT$1,175.00, Teng tips the stock to hit NT$1,200.00 "very soon" on strong handset growth outlook; Hon Hai is up 1.5% at NT$104.50. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are mostly flat, but may fall later as the country's stronger official PMI will ease concerns over a sharp economic slowdown, says a Shanghai-based securities analyst. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.61 vs 127.60. "Investors were uncertain about China's economic prospects, and a rise in PMI will confirm that there is still growth," the analyst says. China's official PMI rose to 53.4 in March from 52.2 in February, the China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing said Friday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE (Dow Jones)--The Australian share market is higher Friday, confounding expectations of profit-taking after a week of gains.\par \par \uc2 "It's a relatively strong market, stronger than expected," said Simon Reed, an advisor at broker RBS Morgans.\par \par \uc2 The S&P/ASX 200 was up 0.6% at 4866.3 points at midday.\par \par \uc2 Reed said attention seemed to be focused in the energy space, with market talk about mergers and acquisitions and inflows of funds heading in that direction.\par \par \uc2 "Often you only need one deal to light up others across the sector," he said.\par \par \uc2 Woodside Petroleum\uc2 , which has been subject to talk about a takeover by BHP Billiton, is up 1.6%, after chief executive Don Voelte denied any knowledge of a speculated potential takeover.\par \par \uc2 But Woodside's share price could also be supported by a stronger oil price and the "ongoing realisation that oil may not be below $100 soon", Reed said.\par \par \uc2 Oil and gas producer Santos is also up 1.5%. BHP shares are up 0.6% while \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  is up 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Reed said money was also flowing into the major banks ahead of three of the banks reporting their first half financial results in May. \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , which won't report, is up 0.4%, \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2  is up 0.6%, \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2 . is up 0.7% and \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  is up 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Regional pay television provider \uc2 Austar United Communications\uc2  saw its shares jump 6.5% in early trade on a report that a bid price had been agreed between its major shareholder \uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  and Foxtel. Austar later said no price has been decided and its shares are at 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 A big mover among the small cap companies is \uc2 Biota Holdings Ltd\uc2 ., whose shares are up 36% after it said it had been awarded a contract worth up to US$231 million for the advanced development of its laninamivar influenza antiviral drug.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Australian dollar was trading a little softer from its fresh post-float high of 1.0373 against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The AUD is still finding good buying support above 1.03 however it has by mid morning slipped half a cent from its overnight high, said Tim Waterer, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at CMC Markets.\par \par \uc2 "With U.S. non farm payrolls looming large tonight, it would not be a surprise to see the AUD take a brief pause from its 'golden run' during the local session today. Traders generally take on a more cautious tone ahead of US jobs numbers as they are a key indicator of economic health," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Gavin Lower, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-3-9292-2095; gavin.lower@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is lower after the central parity was set at a fresh all-time low. The pair is at 6.5472 vs 6.5485 in late Asian trade Thursday, and it may test 6.5450 support, says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. "The market is expecting higher inflation rate for March, at 5%. Also, investors think China's inflationary pressure will further grow until June and that's driving the yuan higher," says the trader. The central parity was set at 6.5527 vs Thursday's 6.5564. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] The rally in crude oil prices has solid foundations, with refiners paying record premiums for high-quality, low-sulphur crudes to make up for lost Libya output as their maintenance season winds down. Many refineries can't process lower-quality Saudi crude, although the kingdom is doing its best to satisfy global demand, Mirae Asset Research says, noting the price premium of some top light, sweet crudes like those from Azerbaijan and Kazakhstan have soared to all-time highs. Nymex May light, sweet crude is at $107.08, up 36 cents after gaining 2.3% overnight. (simon.hall@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to open higher following benign March inflation data, which could inspire the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its key interest rate unchanged at 6.75% next week, traders say. They expect the main index to top 3700 after it ended up 1.0% Thursday at 3678.674. "The stronger rupiah should also help inspire buying but typical Friday trade my limit the main index's gains," says a trader with Valbury Securities. The government says, on-year inflation was at +6.65%, compared with market's +6.94% forecast. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) and Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may lead gains on strong 2010 earnings and a brighter outlook. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is moving higher on USD-buying by some banks who are taking precautionary positions ahead of the U.S. non-farm payrolls data later Friday, says a local bank trader, adding the pair is likely to stay rangebound ahead of the data. The pair is at 43.46 from 43.39 late Thursday in thin volume; next resistance is pegged at 43.60, with support at 43.30. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The smaller-than-expected volume for JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities "could be supportive" for the USD/JPY as well as other non-USD \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the JPY, Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank says. Finance ministry data showed Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y692.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Feb. 25 to Mar. 29. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen on March 18 as a part of an international effort to stem the yen's strength. Yamamoto says the data could lead to positive views toward the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has succeeded in having a bigger impact on the market with a smaller volume. Some traders had estimated two trillion yen for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on that day. Yamamoto says the USD/JPY may head toward the 84 mark but cautions a possible USD drop after the release of the U.S. March jobs data due at 1230 GMT. The pair is at 83.59 from 83.15 late Thursday in New York. (tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.1% at 23,559.54, consolidating after its 2.0% rally in the past 2 sessions; volume stays strong at HK$14.47 billion. Steven Leung, director of institutional sales at UOB KayHian, says the market momentum is not strong enough to break the 24,000 resistance, while 22,500 is very well supported by strong corporate earnings; he adds, investment theme for April include Golden Week-related stocks such as Macau gaming plays, luxury retailers, and Chinese companies reporting 1Q11 earnings. Most blue chips are higher but none is rising by more than 2%; \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2  (2038.HK) is the worst-performing index member for the second straight session; it falls 2.1% to HK$4.57 on top of 4.5% post-results tumble Thursday. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 03-31-11 2235ET ]\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher with metal companies and coal miners leading gains on the back of stronger global commodity prices, analysts say. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2933.75; analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. Also, "the higher March Purchasing Managers Index indicates a rebound in manufacturing activity, which also lends support," says Huatai Securities analyst Zhou Lin. China's official PMI rose to 53.4 in March from 52.2 in February after three consecutive months of slowdowns, official data show Friday. But he adds that concerns over further tightening measures by Beijing may curb further gains in the market. Among leading gainers, coal play \uc2 Beijing Haohua Energy Resource\uc2  (601101.SH) is up 1.5% at CNY49.51, while Anhui Hengyuan Coal Industry & Electricity Power (600971.SH) rises 0.6% to CNY41.98. Metal firm Zijin Mining (601899.SH) adds 0.5% to CNY7.65, and Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) advances 0.5% to CNY39.06. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.1% higher at 1254.26. (amy.li@dowjones.com) [ 03-31-11 2235ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110401e7410001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
