\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:BOJ Low  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Volume Supports USD/JPY-\uc2 Barclays\b0\par\par\uc2 1,838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 04:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] The smaller-than-expected volume for JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities "could be supportive" for the USD/JPY as well as other non-USD \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the JPY, Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank says. Finance ministry data showed Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y692.5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Feb. 25 to Mar. 29. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold yen on March 18 as a part of an international effort to stem the yen's strength. Yamamoto says the data could lead to positive views toward the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has succeeded in having a bigger impact on the market with a smaller volume. Some traders had estimated two trillion yen for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on that day. Yamamoto says the USD/JPY may head toward the 84 mark but cautions a possible USD drop after the release of the U.S. March jobs data due at 1230 GMT. The pair is at 83.59 from 83.15 late Thursday in New York. (tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.1% at 23,559.54, consolidating after its 2.0% rally in the past 2 sessions; volume stays strong at HK$14.47 billion. Steven Leung, director of institutional sales at UOB KayHian, says the market momentum is not strong enough to break the 24,000 resistance, while 22,500 is very well supported by strong corporate earnings; he adds, investment theme for April include Golden Week-related stocks such as Macau gaming plays, luxury retailers, and Chinese companies reporting 1Q11 earnings. Most blue chips are higher but none is rising by more than 2%; \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2  (2038.HK) is the worst-performing index member for the second straight session; it falls 2.1% to HK$4.57 on top of 4.5% post-results tumble Thursday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are higher with metal companies and coal miners leading gains on the back of stronger global commodity prices, analysts say. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.2% at 2933.75; analysts peg immediate resistance at 2950. Also, "the higher March Purchasing Managers Index indicates a rebound in manufacturing activity, which also lends support," says Huatai Securities analyst Zhou Lin. China's official PMI rose to 53.4 in March from 52.2 in February after three consecutive months of slowdowns, official data show Friday. But he adds that concerns over further tightening measures by Beijing may curb further gains in the market. Among leading gainers, coal play \uc2 Beijing Haohua Energy Resource\uc2  (601101.SH) is up 1.5% at CNY49.51, while Anhui Hengyuan Coal Industry & Electricity Power (600971.SH) rises 0.6% to CNY41.98. Metal firm Zijin Mining (601899.SH) adds 0.5% to CNY7.65, and Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) advances 0.5% to CNY39.06. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.1% higher at 1254.26. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds and bond futures are higher following the lower-than-expected March CPI reading; March CPI rose 4.7% on-year compared with February's 4.5% rise, but was lower than the 4.9% gain forecasted by economists polled by Dow Jones. "The data supports the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not hike interest rates in April," says a bond manager at a local asset management firm. But he adds local banks' continued net selling of bond futures will likely cap overall market gains. He tips resistance at 103.20 for June bond futures, which are up 20 ticks at 103.16. The three-year yield is down five bps at 3.68%, the five-year yield is down five bps at 4.06% and the 10-year yield is down three bps at 4.45%. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is down at 7.7794 from 7.7850 late Thursday as a stronger yuan continues to drive demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the fully convertible HKD is often traded as a proxy for the CNY, says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. The USD/CNY hit a new record low on Thursday. The trader tips the pair to trade in a 7.7770-7.7830 band in the near term. "Investors expect the comments out of the G-20 meeting in Nanjing yesterday could fuel speculation of a stronger yuan, lending support to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I've spotted that many European banks keep buying the Hong Kong dollar," the trader says. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are likely to fall on profit taking after their recent gains, but buying by foreigners is expected to continue and may limit downside, says Far East Securities analyst Parin Kitchatornpitak. Foreigners were net buyers of THB4.07 billion worth of shares on Thursday and THB6.29 billion on Wednesday. He tips support at 1040 and resistance at 1056. On Thursday, the SET index ends down 0.3% at 1047.48, after hitting an intraday high of 1056.52, the highest level since October 1996. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.2% at 1548.72 with select blue chips leading gains, says a dealer with a local brokerage; "the blue chips are garnering attention because their valuations are looking attractive amid improving earnings outlook on the back of sustained domestic economic growth." He thinks the index may trade in a 1540-1550 range for the day with intermittent profit-taking ahead of the weekend given the absence of market moving news. HwangDBS Vickers Research reckons that the KLCI "could pull back slightly today" from its morning high of 1551.25 following a cumulative gain of 30.9-point or 2.0% over the past three days. Among gainers, \uc2 Nestle\uc2  (4707.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR47.50, \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) is up 0.6% at MYR7.28 and Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is up 1.6% at MYR3.74. On the downside, Boustead (2771.KU) is off 1.0% at MYR5.80, Paramount (1724.KU) slips 0.6% to MYR5.40 and Tenaga (5347.KU) falls 0.6% to MYR6.21. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is slightly lower at 8,698 vs 8,705 late Thursday as some players are betting on an on-month deflation number for March, says a dealer; but they are mostly reluctant to build large positions until the actual figure is out around 0200 GMT later today. "If inflation is better than expected, the rupiah could strengthen further," the dealer says, tipping a 8,690-8,710 range for today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's March CPI was below market expectations, though still elevated, allowing the BOK to hold rates steady this month before resuming tightening as early as May, according to Daishin Economic Research Institute economist Kim Yoon-gi. "It seems that the government's efforts to keep a tab on services prices are bearing fruit. Rises in education fees and other service charges were much smaller than I expected," he says. He adds strong exports also support the market's view that the BOK won't deliver a back-to-back rate hike at its April 12 policy review. But he says rising global oil prices remain the biggest concern and are likely to be reflected in consumer prices, keeping inflation at similar or higher levels in April. He adds the KRW's recent gains against the USD helped to contain imported inflation as the government takes a wait-and-see approach instead of aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the KRW's rise. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.3% at 3114.74, shrugging off the weak lead from Wall Street as investors continue to see value in blue chips, despite the STI's 6.7% rebound from its March low at 2919. Volume is lower at 154 million shares worth S$181 million, reflecting some caution ahead of U.S non-farm payrolls data later Friday. Phillip Securities says little is changed for STI technicals; "price continues to test the 3110/3120 region, inching higher towards 3120 but not trading into it. Short-term momentum is starting to fizzle a little as price consolidates." The house says "the odds of cutting through 3120 (key resistance) in a single day is not very high, but if NFP exceeds expectations and the U.S. markets surge, it could spill over into the STI and aid in cutting through." It tips support at 3092 (short-term price trigger/breakout level) and 3080. Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG) is down 2.5% at S$1.99 after announcing a discounted and dilutive private placement to property buys at Singapore's science park. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] China's official PMI rises to 53.4 in March from February's 52.2, indicating a rebound in manufacturing activity after three consecutive months of declines in the PMI. The data likely provides reassurance to the market that China's slowdown is limited. "Investment growth remained high in the January-February period, while growth in exports and consumption both declined. Growth in total demand has declined. If stocks of enterprises are adjusted in future, there is still a possibility of economic growth slowing," says CFLP analyst Zhang Liqun in a note. The input price subindex falls to 68.3 from 70.1, showing inflationary pressures are still high and the PBOC is likely to further tighten monetary policy. A Dow Jones poll last week showed that PBOC is likely to raise its benchmark lending and deposit rates by 25 bps each, as well as banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps in 2Q, starting as soon as this month. (stefanie.qi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is a tad higher at 30.27-30.28 from 30.25 late Thursday in thin trade, as some investors take profit on the baht after Thursday's gains, says a dealer at a local bank. But he adds, the pair is still biased down ahead, on continued portfolio inflows though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to slow the baht's rise. Foreigners bought Thai shares with a net amount of THB4.07 billion Thursday and THB6.29 billion Wednesday; month-to-date, foreigners were net buyers of THB19.60 billion of Thai shares. Investors are looking to the U.S. non-farm payrolls due later Friday for further cues. The dealer tips resistance at 30.35 and support at 30.23. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 03-31-11 2215ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110401e7410001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
