\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 01:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    82.70-83.60      82.57-83.98 \par EUR/USD   1.4116-1.4233    1.4045-1.4249 \par AUD/USD   1.0308-1.0373    1.0270-1.0400 \par NZD/USD   0.7578-0.7648    0.7548-0.7661 \par GBP/USD   1.6015-1.6083    1.5978-1.6151 \par USD/CHF   0.9122-0.9216    0.9070-0.9276 \par USD/CAD   0.9681-0.9728    0.9664-0.9753 \par EUR/JPY   117.17-119.00    116.75-119.63 \par EUR/GBP   0.8776-0.8853    0.8753-0.8876 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bullish bias as markets await 1230 GMT U.S. March non-farm payrolls (forecast +195,000 vs +192,000 last) & unemployment rate (forecast unchanged at 8.9%), underpinned by improved USD sentiment after Fed's Kocherlakota said lifting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target for short-term interest rates by more than 0.5 percentage point late this year is "certainly possible". USD/JPY also supported by seasonal yen outflows from Japan as country's new fiscal year starts; loose BOJ monetary policy; expectations for coordinated G-7 JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against excessive yen volatility; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  nuclear crisis; bigger-than-expected 6,000 drop in latest U.S weekly jobless claims (vs minus 2,000 forecast); smaller-than-expected drop in March ISM-Chicago business barometer to 70.6 in March from 71.2 in February (vs 70.0 forecast). But USD sentiment dented by sharp drop in March ISM-NY Current Business Conditions index to 66.4 last month from 77.5 in February, surprise 0.1% drop in U.S. February factory orders (vs forecast for 0.4% rise). USD/JPY gains also tempered by Japan exporter sales, caution before U.S. jobs report, positions adjustment before weekend. No strong cue from Wall Street for JPY-funded carry trades as U.S. stocks closed mixed (DJIA off 0.25%, Nasdaq up 0.15%), but yen crosses vulnerable to 0100 GMT March CFLP China manufacturing PMI data. Other data: 2350 GMT Japan 1Q Tankan survey of enterprises, 1215 GMT Fed's Plosser speaks, 1300 GMT Fed's Dudley speaks, 1400 GMT U.S. March ISM manufacturing PMI (forecast 61.0 vs 61.4 last), February construction spending (forecast +0.2% vs -0.7% last). USD/JPY daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter near overbought, suggesting sideways or higher USD/JPY trading near-term. Resistance at 83.56-83.60 band (Feb. 22 high-200-day moving average); breach would target 83.98 (Feb. 16 reaction high), then 84.51 (Dec. 15 reaction high), psychological 85.00 and 85.94 (Sept. 17 high). Support at 82.70 (hourly chart), then at 82.57 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 82.37 (Wednesday's low), then 81.51 (Tuesday's low), 81.30 (Monday's low), 80.87 (March 25 low) and 80.74 (March 24 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to range-trade before U.S. payrolls report. Underpinned by surprise rise in euro-zone annual CPI to 29-month high of +2.6% in March from +2.4% in February (vs forecast for dip to +2.3%) fuelling expectations ECB will raise interest rates several times this year, starting with its first rise in almost 3 years on Thursday from record low 1.0%. EUR/USD also supported by sovereign euro-buying interest; but gains limited by sovereign debt crisis in Ireland, Greece and Portugal; hawkish comment from Fed's Kocherlakota; caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0755 GMT German March manufacturing PMI, 0800 GMT eurozone March manufacturing PMI, 0900 GMT eurozone February unemployment. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD in bearish mode but stochastics bullish. Support at 1.4116 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.4049-1.4045 band (Wednesday's low-Tuesday's low), then 1.4019 (Monday's low), 1.3980 (March 18 low), 1.3868 (March 17 low), 1.3852 (March 15 low) and 1.3750 (March 11 reaction low). Resistance at 1.4233 (yesterday's high); breach would target 1.4249 (March 22 high), then 1.4283 (Nov. 4 reaction high) and 1.4582 (Jan. 13, 2010 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate after hitting fresh post-float high of 1.0373 yesterday. Underpinned by buoyant commodity prices (CRB spot index ended up 5.72 yesterday at 359.48); but gains tempered by hawkish comment from Fed's Kocherlakota, caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD bullish, while stochastics stay elevated at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher AUD/USD trading near-term. Resistance at 1.0373 (yesterday's high, matching ascending trendline from Nov. 5 high of 1.0182); breach would expose upside to psychological 1.0400 and 1.0500 levels. Support at 1.0308 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 1.0270 (Wednesday's low), then 1.0202 (Tuesday's low, matching previous cap set March 1), 1.0184 (March 25 low), 1.0106 (March 24 low) and 1.0073 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate after hitting one-month high of 0.7648 yesterday. Underpinned by strong commodity prices; but gains tempered by hawkish comments from Fed's Kocherlakota, caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD bullish, while stochastics stay elevated at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher NZD/USD trading near-term. Resistance at 0.7648 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.7661 (Feb. 21 reaction high), then 0.7734 (downtrend line from Nov. 5 high of 0.7974) and 0.7796 (Feb. 8 high). Support at 0.7578 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 0.7548 (Wednesday's low), then 0.7489-0.7478 band (Tuesday's low-March 25 low), 0.7434 (200-day moving average), 0.7417 (March 24 low) and 0.7371 (March 23 low).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by hawkish comments from Fed's Kocherlakota, concerns about adverse impact of UK government spending cuts and tax hikes on economy; GBP sales on buoyant EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD losses tempered by caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0830 GMT UK March CIPS manufacturing PMI (forecast 60.5 vs 61.5 last). Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics bullish at oversold. Support at 1.6015 (yesterday's low), then at 1.5978 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 1.5941-1.5934 band (Tuesday's low-Monday's low), then 1.5905 (100-day moving average), 1.5821 (Jan. 31 low), 1.5765 (200-day moving average) and 1.5750 (Jan. 25 reaction low). Resistance at 1.6083 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.6151 (yesterday's high), then 1.6267 (March 24 high), 1.6382 (March 23 high) and 1.6401 (March 22 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with bullish bias. Underpinned by hawkish comments from Fed's Kocherlakota; but gains tempered by caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0715 GMT Swiss February retail sales, 0730 GMT Swiss March SVME PMI. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode. Resistance at 0.9216 (hourly chart), then at 0.9276 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9316 (March 14 high), then 0.9370 (March 9 high, near 55-day moving average) and 0.9506 (Feb. 22 high). Support at 0.9122 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9070 (March 25 low), then 0.9026 (March 24 low), 0.8976 (March 23 low) and 0.8852 (March 17 record low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with bearish bias. Undermined by stronger commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled up $2.45 yesterday at $106.72/bbl for highest settlement since Sept. 2008); 0.5% growth in Canada January GDP (in line with forecast) encouraging market players to bring forward their expectations for renewed monetary tightening from BOC. But USD/CAD losses tempered by caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold, suggesting sideways or lower USD/CAD trading near-term. Support at 0.9681 (yesterday's low, matching March 10 low); breach would target 0.9664 (March 9 low), then psychological 0.9600 and 0.9500 levels. Resistance at 0.9728 (yesterday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, targeting 0.9753 (Wednesday's high), then 0.9785 (Tuesday's high), 0.9822-0.9826 band (Monday's high-March 25 high), 0.9843 (March 23 high) and 0.9865 (March 18 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting 10-month high of 118.13 on EBS this morning. Buoyed by expectations for April ECB rate hike, Japan importer demand. But EUR/JPY gains tempered by euro-zone sovereign debt worries, Japan exporter sales, caution ahead of U.S. payrolls report, positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher EUR/JPY trading near-term. Resistance at psychological 119 level; breach would target 119.63 (previous base set Feb. 25, 2010), then psychological 120 level. Support at 117.17 (hourly chart), then at 116.75 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 116.22 (Wednesday's low), then 115.56 (previous cap set March 18), 114.82 (Tuesday's low), 114.15 (Monday's low) and 113.52 (March 24 low). [ 31-03-11 2336GMT ]\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting fresh 5-month high of 0.8853 yesterday. Supported by expectations for April ECB rate hike. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, while stochastics stay elevated at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher EUR/GBP trading near-term. Resistance at 0.8853 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.8876 (uptrend resistance line from Feb. 25 high of 0.8593), then 0.8941 (Oct. 25 top) and psychological 0.9000. Support at 0.8776 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 0.8753 (Wednesday's low), then 0.8727 (uptrend support line from Feb. 22 low of 0.8371), 0.8660 (March 24 low), 0.8650 (March 23 low) and 0.8621 (March 15 low).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 31-03-11 2336GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ