\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Best 1Q In More Than A Decade\b0\par\par\uc2 3,239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2011\par 00:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12319.73  -30.88  -0.25% \par Nasdaq     2781.07   +4.28  +0.15% \par S&P 500    1325.83   -2.43  -0.18% \par FTSE 100   5908.76  -39.54  -0.66% \par Xetra DAX  7041.31  -15.84  -0.22% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 27/32 -1/32 0.829% +2.4BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 31/32 -6/32 2.258% +4.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 10/32 -3/32 3.467% +1.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.19-22     +0.39% \par Range         83.22-82.54 \par EUR/USD       1.4155-58    +0.19% \par Range         1.4233-1.4113 \par AUD/USD       1.0323-26    -0.04% \par Range         1.0372-1.0308 \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,439.90   +$15.00 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,421.70-$1,441.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $106.72    +$2.45 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks closed lower after a late slide Thursday but still managed to finish with their best first quarter in more than a decade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 30.88 points, or 0.3%, at 12319.73. Stocks traded in a tight range for much of the session as investors hesitated ahead of key data, but slipped minutes before the market's close after \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\uc2  President Narayana Kocherlakota said key U.S. interest rates might have to rise in late 2011.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Dow finished the year's first three months up 6.4%, its biggest point gain since 1998 and its biggest percentage gain since 1999. The measure has closed up seven of the last eight quarters.\par \par \uc2 Growing optimism that the U.S. economic recovery is strengthening has fueled this quarter's gains despite turmoil overseas.\par \par \uc2 Worries over the destruction in Japan and the conflicts in the Middle East and North Africa briefly sent the Dow down 6.3% this spring, and the benchmark indexes were trading nearly flat for the year just two weeks ago. Since then, the indexes have rebounded vigorously.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 has climbed 5.4% so far this year, its best first quarter since 1998. The measure edged down 2.43 points, or 0.2%, to 1325.83 Thursday. The Nasdaq Composite added 4.28 points, or 0.2%, to 2781.07 Thursday, and is up 4.8% over the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 "It was a struggle--it wasn't easy," Erick Maronak, senior portfolio manager at \uc2 Victory Capital Management\uc2 , said of the gains so far this year. "The positive outcome for the quarter came in a pretty brief period of time."\par \par \uc2 Construction- and mining-equipment maker Caterpillar was the Dow's top performer for the quarter, climbing 19%. Thursday, Caterpillar edged down 18 cents, or 0.2%, to $111.35.\par \par \uc2 Energy companies also fueled the blue-chip measure's gains as oil prices rose amid turmoil in Libya and fears that unrest in North Africa and the Middle East could spread to key oil exporters, including Saudi Arabia. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  were among the Dow's top two performers in the first quarter, surging 18% and 15%, respectively. However, on Thursday, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  lost 52 cents, or 0.5%, to 107.49, while Exxon slid 36 cents, or 0.4%, to 84.13.\par \par \uc2 Cisco Systems was the measure's weakest component, tumbling 15% in the first quarter. Thursday, Cisco shed 18 cents, or 1%, to 17.15.\par \par \uc2 "I'm surprised at the strength we've seen in the markets," said Jeff Morris, head of U.S. equities at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 , noting the array of international concerns. "We had signs of some of the emerging-market economies [that were] supporting the global economic growth starting to slow even before the Japanese twin natural disasters and the nuclear crisis."\par \par \uc2 Others have pointed to recent U.S. economic data as a reason for cautious confidence looking toward the second quarter. On Friday, the government will release its closely watched monthly employment report. A survey of economists by Dow Jones Newswires pointed to an increase of 195,000 jobs in March.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, the Labor Department said initial claims for unemployment benefits fell by 6,000 to a seasonally adjusted 388,000 in the week ended March 26. While economists were expecting a drop of only 2,000, the prior week's figures were revised up to 394,000, from an originally reported 382,000.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Thursday on concerns over further monetary tightening measures from Beijing as economists expect data for March to show the county's inflation rate accelerated from 4.9% in February.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said further declines in the index are likely to be limited despite the inflation concerns because of the inexpensive valuations of heavyweight blue chips including banks. They pegged initial support for the index at 2900.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are beginning to speculate again on the possibility of another hike in interest rates over the coming weekend," said Qian Qimin, an analyst from Shenyin Wanguo Securities. The brokerage expects the rise in the CPI this month to be 5.3%.\par \par \uc2 "The market is staying cautious after it failed several times to hold above the 3000 mark since the beginning of this year," Gold State Securities analyst Li Lei said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares rose for the second consecutive session Thursday, boosted by Wall Street's rise overnight, although a decline for Foxconn following a wider-than-expected net loss last year capped gains.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the benchmark index to find support at 22,800 this week, although investors remained cautious ahead of U.S. non-farm payroll data due Friday.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to predict the market direction at present. Investors are likely to maintain their positions as they wait for Friday's crucial U.S. payroll report," said Peter Lai, director at DBS Vickers.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Thursday as investors took heart from exporter-friendly yen weakness, and the absence of further negative developments on Japan's ongoing nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 Overt pessimism on stocks has eased somewhat after normal manufacturing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were resumed at many firms after interruptions due to the March 11 earthquake, but concerns remain over earnings, said Hideyuki Ishiguro, investment strategy supervisor at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Some overseas manufacturers have realized they need to diversify their sources away from Japanese firms, so even if more firms restore production in coming months, they may face falling demand," he said.\par \par \uc2 While the index may weaken in coming months, for now the weaker yen is supportive, he added, while recent overseas and domestic institutional investor interest will likely remain intact.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures settled at their highest level in 2 1/2 years in U.S. trading Thursday as the dollar weakened and Libyan rebels suffered a setback, raising fears that the conflict could be prolonged.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures on the Comex division of the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2  ended Thursday at a new record settlement on worries about Europe's financial system and forecasts for higher gold demand from India.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed higher on the London Metal Exchange Thursday after the U.S. dollar slipped against the euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A signal that the world's most powerful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may finally be preparing to raise rates from record lows to combat inflation boosted the dollar against its chief rival Thursday, pulling the euro off near five-month highs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minneapolis Federal Reserve Bank President Narayana Kocherlakota said Thursday the Fed may need to increase short-term interest rates by the year end if underlying inflation rises as he anticipates. The surprise remarks jolted short-term interest rate futures, showing traders betting on a nearly even chance of a rate hike by December and enabling the dollar to pare much of its losses against the euro, though it was still trading lower on the global session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His comments came shortly after Richmond Fed President Jeffrey Lacker said it was worth re-examining the Fed's bond-purchasing program, adding that he was concerned about increasing inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It feels to me like particularly the Fed presidents seem now to be taking a more universally hawkish line and are going to be less of a pushover for the more dovish contingent," a view which supported the dollar, said Alan Ruskin, global head of G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the euro clung to broad gains as traders looked ahead to a widely expected ECB rate increase next week, while data showing accelerating euro-zone inflation added to rate-hike hopes.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets have been largely responsive to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speakers and indicators of upcoming rate decisions, as the race for which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will offer higher yields continues to be a primary market driver.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The story here has basically been one of interest rate differentials," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Washington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen suffered especially against both the euro and the dollar, as markets expect rates to remain on hold in earthquake-stricken Japan much longer than any of its Group of 10 leading industrial nations counterparts. The euro posted fresh 10-month highs against the yen, while the dollar crept closer to post-earthquake highs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, investors took caution with their positioning, as they await U.S. nonfarm payrolls on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued their bull run as global equities' performance signaled strong risk sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.4155 from $1.4128 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.15 from Y82.88, while the euro was at Y117.70 from Y117.09. The U.K. pound was at $1.6036 from $1.6075. The dollar was at CHF0.9195 from CHF0.9184.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 76.057 from 76.118.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Results of Ireland's bank stress tests failed to move markets, as they came in line with expectations, analysts said. The tests indicated a EUR24 billion shortfall for Irish banks, which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com analysts deemed at the low end of estimates. [ 31-03-11 2208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro's been remarkably resilient when you consider all the information we had out today about Ireland," said John McCarthy, manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at ING Capital Markets in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro hit its highest levels since May against the Japanese yen, which has been broadly weaker since the Group of Seven coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier this month. Though fiscal-year-end flows supported the yen earlier in the session, interest-rate differentials soon came back into play, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among commodity-related \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Australian dollar continued to shine against the U.S. dollar, hitting fresh highs since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was floated in 1983.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys ended the first quarter trading on a weak note Thursday as comments from a key Federal Reserve official added to anxiety about a shift to tightening monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Minneapolis\f28 \uc2 \f2  President Narayana Kocherlakota, who votes on monetary policy this year, said higher short-term rates, perhaps a rise of 75 basis points, were "certainly possible" in late 2011 to tame the threat of inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kocherlakota was the latest Fed official over the past week to call for a shift in monetary policy. Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia President Charles Plosser, another strong advocate for vigilance on inflation, said recently that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will need to tighten monetary policy in the "not-too-distant future."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far, Fed officials who advocated for accommodative measures to support an economic recovery have yet to signal a change of stance, such as New York Fed President William Dudley and Federal Reserve Vice Chairman Janet Yellen. Dudley is scheduled to speak Friday, about 30 minutes after the release of the March non-farm payrolls report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Thursday afternoon trading in New York, the 10-year note was 3/32 lower to yield 3.467%. Bond prices and yields move in opposite directions. The two-year note was 1/32 lower to yield 0.829%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year yield has risen from 3.294% at the end of December. Demand for safe-haven assets has faded, reflecting an improving economy and budding price pressure in the U.S., which may require the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to start withdrawing monetary stimulus earlier than some investors expect. Debate on the Fed's policy outlook is likely to intensify as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's $600 billion Treasury bond-buying program is approaching the finish line, with a scheduled completion by the end of June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond President Jeffrey Lacker said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should reconsider its $600 billion bond-buying program even as it nears an end. Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis President James Bullard said Tuesday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could stop $100 billion short of the $600 billion target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The non-farm payrolls report will shed light on the health of the labor market and shape market expectations on the Fed's monetary policy outlook. Economists in a Dow Jones Newswires survey expect the report to show U.S. employers added 195,000 new jobs in March, following 192,000 new hires in February. The unemployment rate is forecast to stay unchanged, at 8.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A strong number will reinforce the recent comments from some Fed officials about the need to withdraw liquidity sooner rather than later and the bond market could begin to price in a change in the easy monetary policy," said Christian Cooper, head of U.S. dollar derivatives trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 James Combias, head of U.S. Treasury trading at Mizuho Securities USA Inc. in New York, said the 10-year yield is likely to fall about six to 10 basis points if the jobs number Friday is below 150,000. On the other hand, a number above 250,000 will push the 10-year yield higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures edged down Thursday, giving up earlier gains after a late afternoon rally in share prices dented demand for the safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But JGB prices may remain firm in coming weeks as the economic toll of the massive earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 becomes clearer, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Makoto Yamashita, chief strategist at Deutsche Securities, said that the house sees "no guarantee that the slowdown in the Japanese economy will be only temporary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While JGBs may face some selling pressure in April, which marks the start of Japan's new fiscal year, that may not be enough to push prices down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, the weakening of the yen in recent days, which helps the country's key export sector, may still prove temporary, Deutsche Securities' Yamashita said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We do not expect an environment conducive to selling JGBs and buying Japanese stocks because a clear trend of yen depreciation has not begun," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Thursday after the PBOC drained a net CNY153 billion ($23.3 billion) from the money market this week through its open-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the fourth weekly withdrawal in a row, and also the largest weekly withdrawal in twelve months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is likely to stay in a 127.50-127.65 band Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among key data releases Friday in Asia, the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey will likely show the headline diffusion index, which measures sentiment among large manufacturers, rose to 7 in March from 5 in December, according to the median forecast of 14 Tokyo-based economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists expect a moderate recovery in the index due to improvements in exports and industrial production in the past few months. While the March 11 earthquake is expected to have a large economic and business impact, the latest tankan is unlikely to take all of it in because the deadline for survey responses was the very day of the earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's inflation likely rose in March due to surging crude oil prices and the cost pass-through by manufacturers. However, the uptick in prices will be kept in check somewhat by the government's diesel subsidy and other price controls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March CPI likely rose 3.1% on the year and 0.52% on the month, compared with rises of 2.87% and 0.40% in February, respectively, according to the median forecasts from a Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. March core CPI likely climbed 1.5% on the year and 0.175% on the month, according to the poll.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia may report higher on-year inflation in March due to a low base of comparison, though on the month it will likely report deflation, which may leave the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more room to delay hiking rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The median forecast of 12 regional economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires is for CPI to rise 6.94% on the year compared with a 6.84% rise in February; the median forecast of eight economists who gave on-month comparisons is for the index to contract 0.07% compared with a 0.13% increase a month earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CFLP and HSBC China manufacturing purchasing managers' indexes are also due.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Kocherlakota Says Higher Rates Possible In Late 2011 \par Fed Unveils Discount-Window Borrowing Details \par Ireland To Put More Money Into Banks, Restructure Sector \par US Jobless Claims, Factory Orders Fall \par Ally Financial IPO Expected To Raise About $5B \par Portugal Misses Budget-Deficit Goal, Sets Elections June 5 \par Galleon Founder's Lawyers Press Ex-Employee \par Toyota To Raise US Vehicle Prices \par BOJ Said To Mull Emergency Loans To Quake-Hit Firms -Bloomberg \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sold Y692.5 Billion In March \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par TEPCO: Radioactivity 10,000 Times Limit Detected From Undergroud Water-Kyodo \par Tepco To Build Temporary Water Storage Tanks, Ponds By Mid-April \par WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Losing Ground In China A Year After Moving Search Services To Hong Kong \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2230  Australia    Mar Australian PMI \par 2300  S Korea      Mar CPI \par 2350  Japan        1Q  Tankan Survey of Enterprises in Japan \par 0000  S Korea      Mar Trade data \par 0000  S Korea      Mar South Korea Manufacturing PMI \par 0100  China        Mar CFLP China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers'Index (PMI) \par 0200  Taiwan       Mar Taiwan Manufacturing PMI \par 0230  China        Mar HSBC China Manufacturing PMI \par 0400  Indonesia    Mar CPI \par 0400  Indonesia    Feb Trade Balance \par 0500  India        Mar India Manufacturing PMI \par 0500  Japan        Feb Steel Imports & Exports Statistics \par 0515  Japan        Mar Auto sales \par 0530  Australia    Mar Commodity Price Index \par 0700  Thailand     Mar CPI \par 0730  Thailand     Weekly International Reserves \par 0750  France       Mar Manufacturing PMI \par 0755  Germany      Mar Manufacturing PMI \par 0800  EU           Mar Eurozone Manufacturing PMI \par 0830  UK           Mar CIPS Manufacturing PMI \par 0900  EU           Feb Unemployment \par 1000  UK           CBI & PwC Financial Services Survey press briefing \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1215  US           Philadelphia Fed President Charles Plosser speech in Harrisburg \par 1230  US           Mar Unemployment rate, Non-Farm Payrolls \par 1300  US           New York Fed Pres Dudley speaks in San Juan, P.R. \par 1300  US           Dallas Fed Pres Fisher speaks in Dallas \par 1400  US           Mar ISM Manufacturing Report \par 1400  US           Feb Construction Spending - Construction Put in Place \par N/A   US           Mar Domestic Auto Industry Sales \par N/A   US           Credit Markets Symposium continues, hosted by Richmond Fed President Jeffrey Lacker \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 31-03-11 2208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110331e73v001jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
