\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/ \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 : When the G-7 Stood By  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Japan Real Time Report blog at blogs.wsj.com/japanrealtime.)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi\par \par \uc2 It\u194\'00\'C2 was around 9 a.m., Tokyo time, on March 18, when the Group of Seven leading industrial nations launched an extremely rare joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the yen's steep appreciation in spite of the March 11 disaster. At that stage, a dollar was worth just 79.20 yen.\par \par \uc2 Now, nearly two weeks on from the G-7's first concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a decade, the dollar's worth is close to 83 yen. And that 5% change means a great deal, and a great deal of relief, to Japanese finance officials like Fumihiko\u194\'00\'C2 Igarashi, a senior vice finance minister.\par \par \uc2 "There are clear signs that the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is having effects" on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Mr. Igarashi said, settled in a sofa in his office in the finance ministry. "That is my assessment."\par \par \uc2 The yen's upward trend, which had brought it to a record high of\u194\'00\'C2 76.25 yen to the dollar the day before the G-7 action, threatened to batter an export-reliant Japanese economy whose northern regions have been ravaged by the massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11. Rises in the yen make Japanese products less competitive abroad and usually drive Japanese stock markets lower.\par \par \uc2 Whether the dollar's stability is sustainable is hard to\u194\'00\'C2 gauge with certainty. But with some analysts now expecting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to recover versus the yen, as the U.S. Federal Reserve looks more eager to tighten monetary policy, Japanese officials may be able to relax for now---after the hectic moments preceding the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The mood surrounding the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was much different from a unilateral move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September, which didn't go down well in some G-7 quarters.\u194\'00\'C2 Rintaro Tamaki, Japan's\u194\'00\'C2 top\u194\'00\'C2 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bureaucrat, initiated full-fledged G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks\u194\'00\'C2 on March 17, after the dollar tumbled around\u194\'00\'C2 3 yen to the record\u194\'00\'C2 low in early Asian trading and then failed to rebound as sharply as some Japanese officials expected in following hours, according to a person familiar with the group's discussions.\par \par \uc2 When the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  was first discussed by the G-7, "nobody had major objections or strong reasons why it should not be done," a European official said, again speaking on condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 As the G-7 deputies formed agreement on joint action, the main agenda for a planned teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs for the morning in Tokyo on\u194\'00\'C2 March 18, Friday changed.\par \par \uc2 The original purpose of the meeting, which was a French initiative, was to\u194\'00\'C2 reaffirm\u194\'00\'C2 the G-7 support for Japan and to learn how the country was coping, Japanese officials said. After the yen's jump and the G-7 deputy negotiations, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon became the biggest agenda item.\par \par \uc2 In the G-7 meeting, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, who had been constantly updated on the group's mood, asked his counterparts to conduct joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it didn't take much time to seal a deal, the first person said. The members didn't preset the amounts of the yen they should sell or the levels to which they should lift the dollar versus the yen, the person said.\par \par \uc2 Back at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Noda read the G-7 agreement out to reporters, sided by Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa. Some had the impression he might be biting back tears. Japanese government officials said Mr. Noda might have been touched by the true signs of genuine G-7 solidarity.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 31-03-11 0915GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110331e73v000t1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
