\par\par \b \uc2 CHARTING MARKETS: Japan's MOF Has The Time And The Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Cox, CMT   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar/yen pair is going where the Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted it to go after the earthquake: up, that is.\par \par \uc2 But only a relatively higher dollar move would nail down the MOF's intention for a relatively weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, if it opens the Friday session above Thursday's high of Y83.24, would be going for weekly resistance at Y84.89, at least. Such a move would test Y84.57.\par \par \uc2 It's a monthly level and it would be convenient for the MOF to help the dollar over that level and cement relative dollar strength.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the dollar may instead open lower Friday, and that prospect has a lot going for it.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's Thursday intraday high is Y83.24, the highest level since EUR/JPY touched its record low at 76.25 on March 16. Wednesday's high was Y83.21, and the Y83.25-area intraday resistance is evident. Decisive trading below Y82.91, if it comes to that, would break the uptrend. The result may be a dollar dip to Y81-area support as the yen flexes. I estimate that widest corrective move would take the dollar down to Y79.13 at most.\par \par \uc2 The signal for the move would be decisive dollar trading below Y80.69. At that point, talk of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would universal, I guess. Of course \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't a given, but it might be effective.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday it came out that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y692.5 billion in the March \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Observers note that the figure was in line with Y660 billion which was based on revised data flows from the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 That, as the saying goes, isn't hay.\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Cox, a chartered market technician, is chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires. He was formerly a senior analyst for the Commodity Research Bureau. He can be reached at stephen.cox@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (Data by CQG)\par \par \uc2 (This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.) [ 03-31-11 1610ET ]\par \par \uc2 1081 4247   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110331e73v000bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
