\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Higher, Led By Telecom, As Job Hopes Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 3,049 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12350.61  +71.60  +0.58% \par Nasdaq     2776.79  +19.90  +0.72% \par S&P 500    1328.26   +8.82  +0.67% \par FTSE 100   5948.30  +16.13  +0.27% \par Xetra DAX  7057.15 +122.71  +1.77% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 29/32  +1/32 0.801% -2.4BP \par 5Y Tsy   100 5/32  +7/32 2.213% -5.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 14/32 +10/32 3.450% -3.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       82.84-89     +0.49% \par Range         83.19-82.35 \par EUR/USD       1.4127-33    +0.15% \par Range         1.4147-1.4049 \par AUD/USD       1.0324-29    +0.32% \par Range         1.0337-1.0268 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    9.6%/11.4% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    9.5%/10.4% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol  10.85%/11.45% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,424.90    +$7.40 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,413.10-$1,431.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $104.27    -$0.52 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Telecommunications stocks led U.S. indexes higher Wednesday, as optimism over deal activity added to confidence that jobs data would continue pointing toward a strengthening labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 71.60 points, or 0.58%, to finish at 12350.61, its eighth gain in 10 trading days and highest close in nearly six weeks. The measure was led by \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , which gained 2.2% after Chief Executive \uc2 Randall Stephenson\uc2  said he expects some divestitures as his company pursues a $39 billion buyout of \uc2 T-Mobile USA\uc2  from \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index closed 8.82 higher, or 0.67%, at 1328.26, also posting its eighth gain in 10 days. The Nasdaq Composite gained 19.90, or 0.72%, to 2776.79.\par \par \uc2 The Russell 2000 small-capitalization index finished at its highest close of the year and its highest since Oct. 12, 2007, as it gained 10.88, or 1.31%, to 840.37.\par \par \uc2 "The evidence continues to mount that the economy is in recovery mode," said Jack McPherson, portfolio manager at Eagle Small Cap Core Value Fund, noting that small-cap stocks tend to outperform their larger counterparts coming out of a recession.\par \par \uc2 Investors taking their money out of fixed income to wade into stocks may also be boosting small-caps, he said. Still, at a time of global fragility, it is somewhat surprising that people are willing to invest in riskier assets, he said. With their lower cash reserves and higher volatility, small-cap stocks are generally considered riskier than their larger counterparts.\par \par \uc2 "Given the geopolitical backdrop, given what's going on in Europe, you'd think aversion to risk might be higher," McPherson said.\par \par \uc2 In other deal news, \uc2 Valeant Pharmaceuticals\uc2  made a $5.7 billion bid for biopharmaceutical group \uc2 Cephalon\uc2 . \uc2 Cephalon\uc2 's stock surged 28% and Valeant's rose 13%.\par \par \uc2 Investors said the market was also looking ahead with more confidence to Friday's key government jobs report, following encouraging data Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 Automatic Data Processing\uc2  said U.S. private-sector payrolls increased by 201,000 jobs in March, just below the expected 205,000. Half the gains in the ADP report came from small businesses. The figure was "consistent with a gradual if uneven decline in the employment rate," the report said.\par \par \uc2 "The market's showing its hand very clearly here: They want this number Friday to be through the roof," plus revisions to previous figures, Todd Colvin, vice president at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  said. "The jobs number is really what's driving the trade here."\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks ended at their highest levels since the devastating March 11 earthquake, as a weaker yen lifted shares of exporters and of firms which restarted factories shuttered after the disaster.\par \par \uc2 "The mood is improving with companies resuming their plant \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  faster than expected," said Hisatsune Kobayashi, a senior market analyst at Nikko Cordial Securities. He added that the external environment was also favorable vis-a-vis the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, on the hopes for a U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Electric Co. continues to be dragged by mounting fears it will be nationalized\par \par \uc2 The virtual nationalization of Tepco "is an effective and fitting option" which would give the public greater reassurance and would prevent the company's credit strength from being excessively damaged, Nomura analysts Toshihiro Uomoto and Kazuma Ogino said in a client report.\par \par \uc2 "It would be best if there is more progress on the nuclear power plant crisis," said Cosmo Securities strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi. He added, however, that it was unrealistic to expect shares to rise too much amid the uncertain corporate earnings outlook.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly lower Wednesday led by brokerages, with the market seemingly in correction mode for the time being, and property developers after some cities announced property price control targets.\par \par \uc2 "Property stocks will be under pressure as there is no sign of any relief in the government's tightening efforts," said Jacky Zhang, an analyst at Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the index to hover below 3000 as investors await the release of major economic data for March for potential cues about changes in policy. China is due to release its March Purchasing Managers Index on April 1 and other major economic data, such as the inflation rate, around April 15.\par \par \uc2 "The market may need to have a rest before testing psychological resistance at 3000," said Li Lei, an analyst at Gold State Securities.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher Wednesday, boosted by Wall Street's rise overnight and gains in Cheung Kong and its affiliate \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  because of stronger-than-expected full-year results.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the benchmark index to continue to trade in a narrow range this week, with concerns over the Middle East and Japan likely to cap any further gains.\par \par \uc2 Prudential Brokerage Associate Director Alvin Cheung said he expects the index to remain in a 22,800-23,500 range this week.\par \par \uc2 "It's still too early to say if the index has reversed to an uptrend, as the current gains are mainly supported by individual companies," Cheung said.\par \par \uc2 Oil inventories rose to a new high at a key U.S. delivery depot, government data showed Wednesday, causing crude futures to settle lower in U.S. trading, despite continued fighting in Libya.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices settled modestly higher in U.S. trading Wednesday as a weaker dollar led to a turnaround from a mid-morning dip on record-high U.S. crude oil inventories.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed lower on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday after weaker-than-expected U.S. data and a lack of buying activity from China, the world's top metals consumer, weighed heavily on the markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro edged up against the dollar Wednesday as the effect of recent hawkish rhetoric from Federal Reserve officials wore off, paring the dollar's recent gains.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy has been the main catalyst for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets of late, as the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to raise interest rates in April and as several Fed officials have signaled their support for a more aggressive approach to inflation in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier this week, the dollar advanced on heightened hopes for an end to the Fed's accommodative policy stance, but the euro gained the upper hand as ECB board member Lorenzo Bini Smaghi signaled that there will be a number of rate rises to come in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When European central bankers are talking about rates, the euro benefits, when you have FOMC members talking about rates, the dollar benefits. It's kind of a tug of war right now," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York-Mellon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets have been more sensitive to official commentary in recent days as they await key U.S. economic data at the end of the week, along with additional Fed speakers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the dollar rose Wednesday to a foothold above Y83, well above its immediate postintervention high of Y82, with some market participants betting on a turning point higher for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in concert with other Group of Seven leading nations, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier this month in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to force the yen lower after the dollar weakened to an all-time low of Y76.25, it seems the dollar is now on a steady path higher against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fund managers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, riskier, commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to perform well on the back of gains in U.S. equities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was at $1.4127 from $1.4114 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y82.89 from Y82.48, while the euro was at Y117.09 from Y116.39. The U.K. pound was at $1.6075 from $1.6008. The dollar was at CHF0.9184 from CHF0.9203.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 76.088 from 76.249.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro gained ground against the dollar after the ECB's Bini Smaghi said that euro-zone interest rates will be raised in a gradual way. With one ECB rate increase priced in for April, some market participants have expressed doubts whether rate increases would extend beyond that.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone rate increases "are priced in but to hear from an ECB official that they could do more than just a one-off rate hike is giving (the euro) support," said Mark McCormick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City President Thomas Hoenig's comments that the Federal Reserve needs to get on with the process of tightening policy largely went unnoticed by markets. Despite this week's slew of hawkish Fed speakers, their comments have been overplayed in markets, as it is too early for the Fed to shift its position on monetary policy, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, St. Louis Fed President James Bullard said that the Fed must eventually start policy tightening, though policy will still be accommodative early in the tightening cycle.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Within the FOMC, the dominating school of thought...remains very dovish," said Alvise Marino, a strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 . [ 30-03-11 2202GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market's focus on yields also drove both the euro and the dollar higher against the yen Wednesday. While the dollar briefly hurdled Y83, to its highest since March 11, the euro hit 10-month highs above Y117.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, continuing risk sentiment helped commodity-related \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Australian dollar touched fresh all-time highs against the U.S. dollar since it was floated in 1983.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys broke with the longest losing streak in more than two decades Wednesday, with prices rising after weaker U.S. data and as the government wrapped up its latest round of Treasury note auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The week's auctions weren't stellar--the government had to pay up to find homes for its two-, five- and seven-year notes--but the auctions' passing allowed bond prices to rise leading into Friday's key U.S. nonfarm payrolls report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kevin Walter\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, said many market participants are reluctant to head into Friday's employment report low on Treasurys, having been burned in the past when payrolls gained by less than expected. Walter said there was buying interest out of Japan Wednesday and he believes Asian investors' interest in Treasurys could pick up even more next week after their fiscal year-end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Wednesday afternoon trading in New York, the 10-year note was up 10/32 to yield 3.450% and the two-year note was up 1/32 to yield 0.801%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains Wednesday put an end to a U.S. government-bond market selloff that was substantial. Treasurys prices had been under pressure for the last nine sessions amid worries about the Federal Reserve outlook and that policymakers could end their easy monetary policy sooner than expected as the U.S. economy improves. It was the longest decline since 1990.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With payrolls and quarter end, it was only a matter of time before the market was going to clear itself after a pretty big selloff," said George Goncalves, managing director and head of U.S. interest-rates strategy in the Americas at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities International Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Wednesday as a weaker yen and a rebound in share prices dented the appeal of safe-haven assets, while a continued slump in U.S. Treasurys also weighed on demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But as the economic impact of the massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11 becomes increasingly clear, demand for low risk JGBs could rise in the weeks ahead, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prolonged leaks of radioactive materials from the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors northeast of Tokyo are adding to the country's economic woes, which will benefit JGBs so long as the government doesn't sharply increase issuance, analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The risk of a prolonged nuclear crisis is especially troublesome for the economy," said Jun Ishii, chief fixed-income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "As long as the authorities seem unable to bring the situation under control, corporate and consumer sentiment will almost surely continue to wither," exerting downward pressure on long-term yields, Ishii said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Wednesday as demand for safe-haven bond investments increased.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, a Shanghai-based local fund manager noted that bonds have limited upside due to tighter liquidity ahead of the Tomb Sweeping Holiday which starts Sunday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Thursday, South Korea's February factory output likely fell on the month for the first time in four months because of longer national holidays. The median forecast of nine economists in a Dow Jones Newswires poll is for industrial output to contract 2.5% on the month while maintaining its on-year expansionary trend with an 8.5% rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong retail sales likely grew 16.0% on the year in value terms in February, slower than January's 28.2% rise, according to a median forecast of seven economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. Economists say the earlier timing of the Lunar New Year this year frontloaded spending to January, dragging on last month's retail sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese housing starts are forecast to have risen 7.3% on the year in February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Taiwan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to raise benchmark interest rates 12.5 basis points in its quarterly meeting Thursday, as growing--albeit benign--inflationary pressure outweighs concern about the impact of rising oil prices and the Japanese earthquake on Taiwan's economic growth. All 17 economists polled expect the benchmark discount rate to rise to 1.750% from 1.625%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par ADP March Private-Sector Jobs Up 201K \par Obama Outlines US Energy Plan \par Valeant Bids $5.7B For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cephalon\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Galleon Founder Shared Goldman Tip On 2008 Wiretap \par BofA CEO '10 Compensation Valued At $1.9M \par NY Fed: Declines AIG Offer For Maiden Lane II Assets \par Treasury Says Financial Rescues Will Turn Profit \par Investors Await Results Of Irish Bank Stress Tests \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Johnson & Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Reorganize Mcneil Unit \par Qihoo IPO Steals Limelight From Apollo's Offering \par Fed's Hoenig Warns Of 'Inflationary Impulses' \par Tarp Watchdog To Warn Of Risks From Big Firms \par S Korea Manufacturers Outlook Index 95 In April Vs 96 In March \par WSJ: AMR Suspends Daily Flights To Japan From Dallas, NY JFK \par WSJ: EPA Says Traces Of Radiation Found In US Milk \par US Still Concerned About Fukushima, But 'Slow Recovery' Taking Place \par WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 McDonald's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Boosts Imports To Japan Amid Supply-Chain Problems \par WSJ: Edano Says Japan To Step Up Acceptance Of Foreign Help \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2300  S Korea      Feb Industrial Production Index \par 2300  S Korea      Feb Service Industry Activity Index \par 2315  Japan        Mar Japan Manufacturing PMI \par 0030  Australia    Feb Financial Aggregates, incl Private Sector Credit \par 0030  Australia    Feb Building Approvals \par 0030  Australia    Feb Retail Trade \par 0200  New Zealand  Mar NBNZ Business Outlook \par 0200  Singapore    Feb Bank Loans \par 0200  Singapore    Feb Money Supply \par 0430  Japan        Feb Preliminary Report on Petroleum Statistics \par 0500  Japan        Feb Housing Starts \par 0500  Japan        Feb Construction Orders \par 0530  Australia    Feb International Reserves & Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Liquidity \par 0600  UK           Mar Nationwide House Price Index \par 0600  Germany      Feb Retail Trade \par 0630  India        Weekly WPI (for fuel products and primary articles) \par 0645  France       Feb PPI \par 0730  EU           Feb EuroCOIN indicator of euro area economic activity \par 0800  Germany      Mar Labor market statistics \par 0815  Hong Kong    Feb Money Supply \par 0830  Hong Kong    Feb Retail Sales \par 0830  UK           1Q  Bank of England Credit Conditions Survey \par 0900  EU           Mar Flash Estimate euro area inflation \par 1030  Malaysia     Feb Money Supply \par 1230  US           Mar 26 Unemployment Insurance Claims Report - Initial Claims \par 1230  US           Mar ISM-NY Report \par 1345  US           Mar 27 Bloomberg Consumer Comfort Index \par 1345  US           Mar ISM-Chicago Business Survey - Chicago PMI \par 1400  US           Mar 19 DJ-BTMU U.S. Business Barometer \par 1400  US           Feb Manufacturers' Shipments, Inventories & Orders (M3) \par 14