\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par PROPOSAL TO GUT DOE LOAN GUARANTEE PROGRAM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 About $41 billion in loan guarantees for renewable energy projects are caught up in the bipartisan wrangling over the federal budget, which could derail dozens of projects and eliminate tens of thousands of jobs.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MINISTER WARNS OF FURTHER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior Japanese finance ministry official says "it does not make sense" for the yen to rise against the dollar as Japan is still in the middle of a crisis, in comments that maintain the threat of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER AFTER RALLY IN TOKYO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are up, buoyed by gains for overseas markets with a rally in Tokyo, and the economic focus falling on ADP employment data due later.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US JOB CUT PROJECTIONS DROP TO 1995 LEVELS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March layoffs in the private sector are down 39% from the prior year to 41,528, according to consultancy \f28 \uc2 \f2 Challenger, Gray & Christmas\f28 \uc2 \f2 , moving the 1Q tally to 130,749 job cuts, which is 28% lower than the same period in 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE ECONOMIC CONFIDENCE WEAKENS, INFLATION BUILDS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic sentiment in the euro zone deteriorates as unrest across unrest across the Arab world drove up oil prices and Japan was hit by a devastating earthquake, tsunami and fears of a nuclear disaster, official data shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPE'S DEBT CONTAGION RISK SIGNIFICANT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The risk that Europe's sovereign debt troubles could spread to other euro-zone countries beyond the periphery is "not insignificant," European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Executive Board member Lorenzo Bini Smaghi says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US MORTGAGE APPLICATION VOLUME DOWN IN WEEK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mortgage Bankers Association says the volume of mortgage applications filed in the U.S. last week fell a seasonally adjusted 7.5% from the previous week as interest rates rose. Refinance activity declines 10%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN COMPANIES EXPECT EURO TO TUMBLE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German companies swing to the opinion that the euro is set to fall, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says, citing its March poll of German companies oriented towards foreign trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TULLOW SELLS UGANDA OIL STAKES TO TOTAL, CNOOC FOR $2.9B \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tullow Oil\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it will sell stakes in three Ugandan oil blocks to China's Cnooc and France's Total for a combined $2.9 billion, pushing forward a development that's set to transform Uganda into a major oil producer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRELAND SAYS STRESS TESTS WON'T BE ROSE-TINTED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Irish government won't present an artificially optimistic view of the country's largest banks when it announces the results of new stress tests, Economy Minister Richard Bruton tells German daily Handelsblatt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Morning Briefing \par STOCKS SEEN UP DESPITE JOBS CAUTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks are poised for opening gains as index futures push higher, consolidating above significant technical levels, said David Morrison at GFT. Traders warn that investors are bound to feel slightly nervous ahead of Friday's non farm payroll numbers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Heard on the Street \par RATINGS WOE FOR PORTUGAL, EUROPE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European governments are complaining loudly about the role of ratings agencies in escalating the euro zone sovereign crisis. While much of this is just bluster, S&P's downgrade of Portugal may give critics some ammunition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par OBAMA TO SPEAK ON ENERGY POLICY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama, under pressure to respond to rising gas prices, will outline a series of initiatives to cut the nation's reliance on foreign oil, including new initiatives to expand oil production, increase the use of natural gas to power vehicles and increase production of ethanol.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Top Stories \par PAYROLLS AREN'T THE ONLY GAME IN TOWN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Equity markets all over the world are showing remarkable resilience, quite a feat in the face of Japan's catastrophe, the involvement of Western countries in yet another Middle East conflict and the euro-zone debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER AFTER RALLY IN TOKYO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock futures higher after rally in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US TREASURYS FALL AS EQUITIES PUSH AHEAD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasurys fall against stronger equity markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO TRADES LOWER, YEN CONTINUES TO WEAKEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro trades off its Asian highs against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE DOWN SLIGHTLY AS US SUPPLIES RISE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures are down slightly as traders anticipate a large gain in U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPOT GOLD A TOUCH HIGHER, BUYING SUBDUED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold is trading slightly higher in Europe, remains caught in a range. [ 30-03-11 1200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110330e73u001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
