\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Set For Tentative Try Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 4,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen higher, govt debt mixed; euro, spot gold, oil futures lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Japan Mulls New Radiation Containment Steps\par \par \uc2 -Air Strikes Rock Tripoli As World Mulls Arming Rebels\par \par \uc2 -Allies Intensify Call for Gadhafi to Step Down\par \par \uc2 -EnBW Cuts Borrowing Limit By 20% With New EUR2B Syndicated Loan\par \par \uc2 -EU Open To Fair Tax Competition But Wants Fast Deal -Report\par \par \uc2 -ECB Stark: Time To Normalize Rates "Step By Step" - Report\par \par \uc2 -Fed Fisher Says Will Vote Against Further Liquidity To US Economy\par \par \uc2 -NY Atty General To Review Proposed \uc2 AT&T\uc2 -T-Mobile Merger\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Statoil\uc2  To Freeze Investment Over UK Oil Tax -Report\par \par \uc2 -UK Deputy PM Casts Doubt On New Nuclear Plants -Report\par \par \uc2 -US Appeals Court Rules Against Pernod, Cuban Firm On Trademark\par \par \uc2 -Valeant Offers $5.7 Billion for \uc2 Cephalon\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  profit up 47%\par \par \uc2 -Japan Industrial Output Posts Surprise Gain In February\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0810 EU Mar Eurozone Retail PMI; 0900 EU Mar Business & Consumer Surveys; 1100 UK Mar CBI Distributive Trades Survey; 1215 US Mar ADP National Employment Report; 1430 US Mar 25 EIA Petroleum Status Report\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks rose Wednesday after a strong performance on Wall Street while Japanese exporters led Tokyo up thanks to a weaker yen, but fears over the nuclear radiation crisis and the Libyan standoff continued to weigh.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses are likely to follow suit in a precarious try higher, with government debt mixed. The euro, spot gold and oil futures are all lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses will attempt a tentative step higher Wednesday after Wall Street's gains, although the backdrop remains overloaded with potential pitfalls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Wednesday's opening, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BGC Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  is calling the FTSE up 12 at 5944, the DAX up 45 at 6980, and the CAC up 20 at 4007.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said the continuing crisis at Japan's Fukushima nuclear power plant, damaged by the massive March 11 earthquake and tsunami, is depressing sentiment in Asia and having a knock-on effect in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday's sovereign debt rating downgrades for Greece and Portugal will likely keep bulls on the cautious side Wednesday though the euro-zone economic sentiment survey at 0900 GMT will provide some direction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We predict more flailing and more downgrades ahead. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market has become largely immune to the downgrades, with the euro hardly reacting at all to recent downgrade news. This is likely to hold as long as markets have confidence in Spain," observed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said investors appeared to be adjusting positions ahead of key U.S. jobs data due Friday, which will set the tone and put recent weaker figures in perspective.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IG Index\f28 \uc2 \f2  trader Yusuf Heusen in London said trade was subdued ahead of the closing of books for the first quarter at the end of the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The situation in Japan clearly remains critical, so traders will be keeping a watchful eye on further developments...(while) oil prices are easing on news that the rebels in Libya are making further progress."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Paris, Renaud Murail at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bourse said prices were holding up well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is difficult to find rational reasons for this latest rise...dealers are being prudent," he said, noting that volumes were thin which usually makes for wider price swings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. Deputy Prime Minister Nick Clegg cast doubt on the future for nuclear power by predicting that a review of existing plants--ordered after the explosion at the Fukushima power station--would recommend higher and more costly safety standards, The Daily Telegraph in the U.K. reported Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also in the U.K., Norwegian oil and gas firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (STO, STL.OS) said that it was freezing investment decisions on the Mariner and Bressay fields as it reviewed its spending plans in light of the U.K.'s GBP2 billion windfall tax on the sector, The Times said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street futures are higher Wednesday after energy and telecommunications companies led U.S. stocks up Tuesday as investors looked to push the market higher for the seventh session in nine, ahead of critical data later this week on unemployment and manufacturing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The gains were largely powered by telecom stocks, which surged on continued optimism about a less competitive industry after \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 's bid for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 's \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2 . A deal would create the country's largest phone carrier. \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 1.8%, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Verizon Communications\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 1.7%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sprint Nextel\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the purported loser in an \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  merger with T-Mobile, tumbled 4.6%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are only a dozen companies in telecoms, and it looks like \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  would be the monster," said Scott Armiger, vice president and portfolio manager at Christiana Trust. "With all the swings up and down we've seen in March, some of the telecom advance could also be defensiveness--it's a good place to hide."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Armiger pointed to high dividend yields that are attractive to investors looking to switch out of their bond positions. "It's the late money moving out of bonds--the run in corporate credits is over, and it's time to short Treasurys.... People are just exiting bonds, looking at money-market rates and realizing they have no alternatives, so they go into stocks."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Asia, despite the gains, traders remained cautious as crews continued their struggle to avoid a meltdown at the crippled Fukushima Daiichi plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It would be best if there was more progress on the nuclear (power plant crisis) issue, but it's unrealistic to expect shares to rise further when corporate earnings (outlook) is uncertain," said Cosmo Securities strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is under pressure from the dollar after a drumbeat of Federal Reserve members threw their support behind an end to quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A gradual move from easing toward tighter monetary policy would make dollar assets relatively attractive. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants view the recent comments by St. Louis Fed President James Bullard, as well as Philadelphia Fed President Charles Plosser as a signal to prepare for an end to loose U.S. monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The markets are now finally contemplating life without [quantitative easing]," said Paresh Upadhyaya, head of Americas G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's all about interest-rate differentials."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As worries about Japan's nuclear situation and upheaval in the Middle East and North Africa appear to have momentarily subsided, investors have shifted their focus back to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy and interest-rate differentials, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After recent mixed U.S. economic data, a strong payrolls number "could be enough for the markets to start moving closer to contemplating the next big move in U.S. monetary policy," Upadhyaya said. An acceleration in inflation would also be pivotal, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and the euro, however, will likely remain rangebound ahead of the payrolls number and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate-setting meeting in April, analysts said. In the near term, the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely find support at $1.40, though it could trade as high as $1.4250, said Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York-Mellon. Still there is greater risk to the downside for the euro as markets have pinned their bets on more than one ECB rate hike this year, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are going to start to wonder is this a one-off hike or a potential series of hikes," said Mark McCormick, strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, however, has largely shrugged off its periphery's sovereign debt concerns, seeing muted reaction to Standard and Poor's ratings downgrades for Greece and Portugal's government debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling, now a bit lower against the dollar and euro, is likely to hold in narrow ranges as conflicting influences continue to contain the pound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro, dollar and sterling rallied against the yen Wednesday. "It's significant that the dollar broke above the high of 82 yen it had hit after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (by the G7 amid the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  atomic crisis) on March 18, and it seems there is basically a more bullish sentiment forming," Osao Iizuka, a senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\f28 \uc2 \f2 , told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed Wednesday while investors wait out the stock market's tentative gain to see if it lasts with monetary policies in focus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday, participants will be eyeing the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's three-month liquidity tender and an Italian bond sale, while casting a view to monetary policy implications.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ECB's monetary policy stance has become "very accommodative" and it is the time to bring interest rates "step by step" back to normal, a key member of the ECB's executive board said in a newspaper interview published Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That indicates that the ECB could raise its benchmark interest rate to 1.25% from 1.0% at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's next rate-setting meeting on April 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking with German daily \f28 \uc2 \f2 Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Juergen Stark also said that reports that the ECB is working on a plan to keep struggling Irish banks afloat in the medium term "are just rumors, nothing more."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he acknowledged that the results of a new round of "stress tests" on Irish banks "will have consequences for the liquidity provisions by the Eurosystem [of central banks] and for ELA [Emergency Liquidity Assistance] by the Irish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The extra yield demanded by investors to buy gilts instead of safe-haven German bunds has narrowed to 30 basis points, its tightest level in 18 months, with the U.K. increasingly seen as a safe harbour amid trouble in highly indebted countries in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We should also continue to see support from overseas given interest rates are likely to be on hold for longer than the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Anthony O'Brien, interest rate strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.K. is also becoming a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  diversification trade, with overseas central banks accumulating reserves that need to be deployed," he said. [ 30-03-11 0503GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are lower Wednesday after benchmark Treasurys fell on Tuesday, posing the longest losing streak since 1990 as anxiety grew that the Federal Reserve could end its easy-money policy before many investors expect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comments from two Federal Reserve officials over the past few sessions added to debate in the bond market about a potential shift in monetary policy. Without support from the largest buyer in the Treasury market, many investors were worried that bond yields could rise significantly in coming months, raising borrowing costs for U.S. consumers, businesses and the federal government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A third stage of government asset purchases is unnecessary, and fiscal prudence and job creation is now needed to propel economic growth in the U.S., \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\f28 \uc2 \f2  President Richard Fisher said Tuesday in an interview on Fox Business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current $600 billion round of "quantitative easing" is set to end in June, and Fisher said he would vote against an extension or any tapering off of the government bond-buying program beyond June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo Wednesday, lead June JGB futures were lower on weakness in U.S. Treasurys Tuesday and the rebound in the Nikkei.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude fell in Asian trade on Wednesday as investors weighed in the impact of a jump in U.S. oil stockpiles, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in May, lost 55 cents to $104.24 per barrel while Brent North Sea crude for May dipped 21 cents to $114.95.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The American Petroleum Institute (API), an industry body, said Tuesday that crude inventories rose by 5.7 million barrels last week with the increase seen as an indication of sluggish U.S. demand. The U.S. is the world's biggest oil consumer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unrest in Libya and the region along with a weekly U.S. Department of Energy inventory report due later Wednesday will be keenly watched by traders, said Ong Yi Ling, investment analyst for Phillip Futures in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At an international summit on Libya in London on Tuesday, British Foreign Minister William Hague said that Qatar had agreed to arrange the sale of Libyan oil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said that "the hopes of Libyan oil shipments normalising soon are exaggerated."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME 3-month copper futures still look trapped in the range of $9,400-$9,800 a metric ton they have occupied since March 18. The May Comex contract has eased since the close of LME trade, last at $4.3476 a pound, or $9,586/ton, against a $9,580/ton close for LME 3-month futures yesterday. Broker Triland argues that while sentiment is generally negative, "the general news gives no clear signal to traders." Standard Bank is taking a very bearish view of Chinese copper stockpiles, following a visit to the country earlier this month, but \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  points to research from Brook Hunt suggesting Chinese demand will remain strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold's outlook is being clouded by fears of monetary tightening in major economies, but investors should be mindful of real, rather than nominal, interest rates, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a report. It says low nominal interest rates and actual and projected inflation rates imply negative real rates through 2012 in the U.S., the euro zone and China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In addition, central banks will be mindful of jeopardizing economic growth, and as such may compromise a little on inflation in certain cases. Such an environment will tend to be supportive of the gold price." The bank tips gold to average $1,600 a troy ounce in 2012, with safe-haven demand likely to continue given geopolitical uncertainty and worries over the level of sovereign debt in major economies. Spot gold is at $1,415.50/oz, down $3.20 since Tuesday's New York close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 30-03-11 0503GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12279.01  +81.13  +0.67% \par Nasdaq     2756.89  +26.21  +0.96% \par S&P 500    1319.44   +9.25  +0.71% \par FTSE 100   5932.17  +27.68  +0.47% \par Xetra DAX  6934.44   -4.19  -0.06% \par CAC40      3987.80  +10.85  +0.27% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9655.69  +196.61 +2.08% \par Hang Seng    23453.60 +393.24 +1.71% \par S&P/ASX 200   4825.50 +69.69  +1.47% \par Taiwan Index  8664.66 +68.09  +0.79% \par S.Korea Kospi 2091.47 +19.34  +0.93% \par Shanghai Comp 2943.85 -14.23  -0.48% \par Bombay SenSex 19307.68 +186.88 +0.98% \par Dow Future    12247.50 +17.50  +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 2328.75  +6.25   +0.3% \par S&P Future    1319