\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Edges Up Vs Dollar On Rate Rise Expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erin McCarthy Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro edged up against the dollar Wednesday as the effect of recent hawkish rhetoric from Federal Reserve officials wore off, paring the dollar's recent gains.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy has been the main catalyst for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets of late, as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to raise interest rates in April and as several Fed officials have signaled their support for a more aggressive approach to inflation in coming months.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the dollar advanced on heightened hopes for an end to the Fed's accommodative policy stance, but the euro gained the upper hand as ECB board member Lorenzo Bini Smaghi signaled that there will be a number of rate rises to come in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 "When European central bankers are talking about rates, the euro benefits, when you have FOMC members talking about rates, the dollar benefits. It's kind of a tug of war right now," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York-Mellon.\par \par \uc2 Markets have been more sensitive to official commentary in recent days as they await key U.S. economic data at the end of the week, along with additional Fed speakers.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar rose Wednesday to a foothold above Y83, well above its immediate postintervention high of Y82, with some market participants betting on a turning point higher for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in concert with other Group of Seven leading nations, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to force the yen lower after the dollar weakened to an all-time low of Y76.25, it seems the dollar is now on a steady path higher against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fund managers said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, riskier, commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to perform well on the back of gains in U.S. equities.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the euro was at $1.4127 from $1.4114 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y82.89 from Y82.48, while the euro was at Y117.09 from Y116.39. The U.K. pound was at $1.6075 from $1.6008. The dollar was at CHF0.9184 from CHF0.9203.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 76.088 from about 76.249.\par \par \uc2 The euro gained ground against the dollar after the ECB's Bini Smaghi said that euro-zone interest rates will be raised in a gradual way. With one ECB rate increase priced in for April, some market participants have expressed doubts whether rate increases would extend beyond that.\par \par \uc2 Euro-zone rate increases "are priced in but to hear from an ECB official that they could do more than just a one-off rate hike is giving [the euro] support," said Mark McCormick, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City President Thomas Hoenig's comments that the Federal Reserve needs to get on with the process of tightening policy largely went unnoticed by markets. Despite this week's slew of hawkish Fed speakers, their comments have been overplayed in markets, as it is too early for the Fed to shift its position on monetary policy, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, St. Louis Fed President James Bullard said that the Fed must eventually start policy tightening, though policy will still be accommodative early in the tightening cycle.\par \par \uc2 "Within the FOMC, the dominating school of thought...remains very dovish," said Alvise Marino, a strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The market's focus on yields also drove both the euro and the dollar higher against the yen on Wednesday. While the dollar briefly hurdled Y83, to its highest since March 11, the euro hit 10-month highs above Y117.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, continuing risk sentiment helped commodity-related \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the Australian, Canadian and New Zealand dollars rose against the U.S. dollar. The Australian dollar touched fresh all-time highs against the U.S. dollar since it was floated in 1983.\par \par \uc2 Investor appetite for carry-trades also gave riskier, higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost, Marino said.\par \par \uc2 Still, with a steady string of Fed speakers approaching and with U.S. nonfarm payrolls data on Friday, investors will be cautious about their positioning, analysts said. But a strong payrolls number could help the dollar significantly reverse its losses against the euro, analysts said. -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Javier David and Bradley Davis contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 - -\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110331e73u00jah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
