\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen at post- \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  low, euro/dollar steady\b0\par\par\uc2 701 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Yen at post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lows, euro/yen at 10-mo high\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  repatriation expectations seen overstated\par \par \uc2 * Fed seen lagging ECB in rate hike\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Julie Haviv\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, March 29 (Reuters) - The yen slid to post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lows on Tuesday and analysts anticipate more losses if the spread between U.S. and Japanese yields continues to widen and if repatriation flows into Japan fail to emerge.\par \par \uc2 The two-year U.S. Treasury yield rose to 0.81 percent, eight basis points above Friday's close and up 18 basis points in six days, widening its gap over comparable Japanese yields.\par \par \uc2 The wider spread helped the dollar reached its highest levels against the yen since March 18, when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other major central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop runaway yen gains.\par \par \uc2 "While there are no obvious catalysts for the yen's moves, we suspect that the combination of recent equity market resilience and higher U.S. Treasury yields is weighing on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Wells Fargo Bank in New York.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to 82.48 yen <JPY=> while the euro was last at 116.08 yen <EURJPY=>, a 10-month high.\par \par \uc2 "There are a lot of yen negatives right now," said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at CitiFX in New York.\par \par \uc2 "First, the rate differential between U.S. and Japanese yields has widened, working in the dollar's favor," he said. "Second, it is becoming increasingly clear that repatriation flows (to Japan) are not panning out and if anything will be limited."\par \par \uc2 The yen hit a record high against the dollar during the week following Japan's massive earthquake and tsunami, largely due to expectations investors would repatriate funds from overseas back into Japan.\par \par \uc2 CitiFX said there are several reasons why repatriation flows should be limited, one of which is that Japanese households are still accumulating foreign assets.\par \par \uc2 Japanese firms also appear to have enough funds to deal with the costs of rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 "Life insurance companies can cope with payments because of their cash holdings. Casualty insurance companies may encounter some difficulty but government reinsurance should cover the payments," the firm said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Also, pension funds and retail investors need not raise yen cash and repatriate.\par \par \uc2 "Japanese banks will meet huge loan demand in the area, whereas the government is to increase JGB issuance. The BOJ (Bank of Japan) will be obliged to embark upon further monetary easing," the firm said.\par \par \uc2 CitiFX said this policy mix could be negative for the yen in the medium and long run.\par \par \uc2 FED SEEN LAGGING ECB\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against the euro early on Tuesday after St. Louis Federal Reserve bank President James Bullard told an audience in Prague the U.S. economy was strong enough to curtail the Fed's $600 billion bond-buying program by $100 billion [ID:nLDE72S0RJ].\par \par \uc2 The program, aimed at keeping interest rates low to bolster the economy, has been widely deemed a bane for the dollar on views it is tantamount to printing money.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar's gains versus the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were short lived, as many expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten policy well before the Fed.\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> hit a session low of $1.4060 on the EBS trading platform after falling through reported bids at $1.4080. It last traded nearly unchanged at $1.4084.\par \par \uc2 The euro has retreated from a 4 1/2-month high of $1.4249 hit last week, but has been supported on the view the ECB may raise interest rates at its next policy meeting on April 7.\par \par \uc2 The $1.40 level is supported by a trendline drawn from the low below $1.30 hit in January, while the euro's 21-day moving average stands just above that level, at around $1.4006.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, heavy options-related barriers around $1.4250 were expected to cap gains. A break of that level may see a test of the November high of $1.4283.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jessica Mortimer in London; Editing by Andrea Ricci)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 7)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110329e73t000pm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
47
