in the agricultural sector has limited the stimulatory impact of strong commodity prices." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise to 82.20 on overnight gains in U.S. Treasury yields on the back of expectations for an eventual end to the Fed's unconventional easy monetary policy, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. "The yield gap between U.S. and Japanese bonds has widened quite a bit lately," he says. "The pair may test the recent high" of 82.00 later in the global day, a level which was marked on March 18 following the G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yield spread between 10-year benchmark U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond stands at 219.6 bps vs 207.4 bps a week ago. Still, the pair's rise may be limited on selling from Japan exporters around 82.00, the dealer says. The USD/JPY is at 81.77. He tips support on any falls at 81.40. The EUR/USD may trade in a 1.4040-1.4130 band, he says; it is at 1.4078. The EUR/JPY is tipped to trade in a 114.80-115.50 range, it is at 115.15.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan calls a 2345 GMT press conference which may see him announce new appointments to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's policy-making board. Businessman \uc2 Donald McGauchie\uc2 's term ended this month. Newspaper speculation suggests Swan could also announce the replacement for \uc2 Warwick McKibbin\uc2  whose term ends midyear. Swan is set to appoint former \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist and Labor party veteran John Edwards and \uc2 BG Group\uc2  Managing Director \uc2 Catherine Tanna\uc2  to the board, according to the Australian Financial Review newspaper, which doesn't cite its sources. A spokesman for the Treasurer declines to comment. The RBA board meets next week and is widely expected to keep interest rates on hold. Appointing Edwards could be controversial. He is a former adviser to Labor Prime Minister Paul Keating, which raises questions about his objectivity. james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets may trade with a slight negative bias amid modest losses for U.S. stocks on Monday, and lack of clear positive leads; the unrest in Libya and painfully slow progress to bring Japan's nuclear crisis under control may continue to temper buying activity. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro looks set to trade in tight ranges against the dollar and the yen as renewed demand for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of expectations the ECB will hike next month is likely to be offset somewhat by Portugal's economic travails. ECB President Trichet on Monday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on track to hike rates in April, re-focussing some traders' attention on interest rate differentials which are in favor of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro is at 1.4080, from 1.4087 late Monday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 115.10, from 115.12, and the USD/JPY is at 81.74, compared with 81.67. On the data front, South Korea has February BOP data at 2300 GMT, Japan releases February household spending and February jobless reports at 2330 GMT, and February preliminary retail sales data at 2350 GMT. Later, St. Louis Fed President Bullard speaks in Prague, the U.S. releases January S&P/Case-Shiller Home Price Index, March consumer confidence and the weekly API gasoline and crude stocks data. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's February trade surplus of NZ$194 million shows high commodity prices continue to support export values although there is some evidence of moderation in world prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Philip Borkin. "Looking forward, the outlook for NZ's trade balance is naturally heavily dependent on the performance of soft commodity prices and global demand," he says. Exports totaled NZ$3.87 billion in February vs NZ$3.3 billion in January. Borkin adds imports are also likely to increase in the near term as businesses replace stock damaged in the February earthquake. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 03-28-11 1943ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110328e73s000ca\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
