\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: AUD/USD May Re-Test Friday's 1.0294 High - BNPP\b0\par\par\uc2 1,858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD reached a high of 1.0315 overnight, continuing its recent outperformance. Globally, the Aussie has outpaced all but the NZD over the past week, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note. It says the AUD/USD's rise is also due to general USD weakness, adding that there may be some risk of a turnaround for the USD if the non-farm payrolls numbers show solid gains Friday. The house says positive news on the Australian mining tax issue should continue to encourage investment that has seen corporate borrowing in Australia attract overseas capital at a rate of 8.0% of GDP. The AUD is also benefiting from expectations that Japan will add to demand for coal in the short term for power generation, and for iron ore in the longer term for reconstruction. BNPP says the way is now open for a re-test of Friday's 1.0294 high; the AUD/USD is at 1.0237. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] Following continued falls in U.S. Treasury prices Monday, the lead June JGB futures contract may fall to 139.44 from Monday's close at 139.64 amid concerns that the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 could force the government to increase JGB supply, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. "The risk of increased JGB issuance to secure funds for reconstruction has not been discounted," Miura says. "There is also the view among market participants that the worsening of Japan's fiscal balance will accelerate due to the disaster." He says the benchmark 10-year JGB yield may trade in a 1.230%-1.250% range compared with Monday's 1.245%.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may open lower today amid fresh concerns over the detection of plutonium at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 's (9501.TO) nuclear plant complex, as well as on overnight U.S. stock market weakness. "Although the plutonium news is undoubtedly bad, the known contamination points are still within (the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant) compound, so the market is unlikely to react dramatically," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager of equity marketing at Daiwa Securities. He pegs today's index in a 9350-9450 range vs Monday's close of 9478.53 (down 0.6%). Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9355 vs the Osaka close at 9410. Tepco shares' ask/bid levels are at Y567/Y566 vs Monday's limit-down close of Y696. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand exports at NZ$3.87 billion and imports at NZ$3.68 billion proved much stronger than expected in February, with little impact seen in export values due to the Christchurch earthquake, says ASB Bank economist Jane Turner. "However, there may be some disruption to exports over March and April, as some manufacturers in Christchurch were forced to shut down following the quake," Turner says. A Dow Jones Newswire poll of 10 economists had expected imports of NZ$3.30 billion and exports of NZ$3.55 billion. Turner adds that the February trade balance of NZ$194 million continues to highlight the export-led nature of New Zealand economic growth, "however, cautiousness in the agricultural sector has limited the stimulatory impact of strong commodity prices." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise to 82.20 on overnight gains in U.S. Treasury yields on the back of expectations for an eventual end to the Fed's unconventional easy monetary policy, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. "The yield gap between U.S. and Japanese bonds has widened quite a bit lately," he says. "The pair may test the recent high" of 82.00 later in the global day, a level which was marked on March 18 following the G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yield spread between 10-year benchmark U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond stands at 219.6 bps vs 207.4 bps a week ago. Still, the pair's rise may be limited on selling from Japan exporters around 82.00, the dealer says. The USD/JPY is at 81.77. He tips support on any falls at 81.40. The EUR/USD may trade in a 1.4040-1.4130 band, he says; it is at 1.4078. The EUR/JPY is tipped to trade in a 114.80-115.50 range, it is at 115.15.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan calls a 2345 GMT press conference which may see him announce new appointments to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's policy-making board. Businessman \uc2 Donald McGauchie\uc2 's term ended this month. Newspaper speculation suggests Swan could also announce the replacement for \uc2 Warwick McKibbin\uc2  whose term ends midyear. Swan is set to appoint former \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist and Labor party veteran John Edwards and \uc2 BG Group\uc2  Managing Director \uc2 Catherine Tanna\uc2  to the board, according to the Australian Financial Review newspaper, which doesn't cite its sources. A spokesman for the Treasurer declines to comment. The RBA board meets next week and is widely expected to keep interest rates on hold. Appointing Edwards could be controversial. He is a former adviser to Labor Prime Minister Paul Keating, which raises questions about his objectivity. james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets may trade with a slight negative bias amid modest losses for U.S. stocks on Monday, and lack of clear positive leads; the unrest in Libya and painfully slow progress to bring Japan's nuclear crisis under control may continue to temper buying activity. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro looks set to trade in tight ranges against the dollar and the yen as renewed demand for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of expectations the ECB will hike next month is likely to be offset somewhat by Portugal's economic travails. ECB President Trichet on Monday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on track to hike rates in April, re-focussing some traders' attention on interest rate differentials which are in favor of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro is at 1.4080, from 1.4087 late Monday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 115.10, from 115.12, and the USD/JPY is at 81.74, compared with 81.67. On the data front, South Korea has February BOP data at 2300 GMT, Japan releases February household spending and February jobless reports at 2330 GMT, and February preliminary retail sales data at 2350 GMT. Later, St. Louis Fed President Bullard speaks in Prague, the U.S. releases January S&P/Case-Shiller Home Price Index, March consumer confidence and the weekly API gasoline and crude stocks data. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's February trade surplus of NZ$194 million shows high commodity prices continue to support export values although there is some evidence of moderation in world prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Philip Borkin. "Loo