\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lead JGB Futures May Fall To 139.44 - Mizuho Sec\b0\par\par\uc2 1,930 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] Following continued falls in U.S. Treasury prices Monday, the lead June JGB futures contract may fall to 139.44 from Monday's close at 139.64 amid concerns that the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 could force the government to increase JGB supply, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. "The risk of increased JGB issuance to secure funds for reconstruction has not been discounted," Miura says. "There is also the view among market participants that the worsening of Japan's fiscal balance will accelerate due to the disaster." He says the benchmark 10-year JGB yield may trade in a 1.230%-1.250% range compared with Monday's 1.245%.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may open lower today amid fresh concerns over the detection of plutonium at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 's (9501.TO) nuclear plant complex, as well as on overnight U.S. stock market weakness. "Although the plutonium news is undoubtedly bad, the known contamination points are still within (the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant) compound, so the market is unlikely to react dramatically," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager of equity marketing at Daiwa Securities. He pegs today's index in a 9350-9450 range vs Monday's close of 9478.53 (down 0.6%). Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9355 vs the Osaka close at 9410. Tepco shares' ask/bid levels are at Y567/Y566 vs Monday's limit-down close of Y696. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand exports at NZ$3.87 billion and imports at NZ$3.68 billion proved much stronger than expected in February, with little impact seen in export values due to the Christchurch earthquake, says ASB Bank economist Jane Turner. "However, there may be some disruption to exports over March and April, as some manufacturers in Christchurch were forced to shut down following the quake," Turner says. A Dow Jones Newswire poll of 10 economists had expected imports of NZ$3.30 billion and exports of NZ$3.55 billion. Turner adds that the February trade balance of NZ$194 million continues to highlight the export-led nature of New Zealand economic growth, "however, cautiousness in the agricultural sector has limited the stimulatory impact of strong commodity prices." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may rise to 82.20 on overnight gains in U.S. Treasury yields on the back of expectations for an eventual end to the Fed's unconventional easy monetary policy, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank. "The yield gap between U.S. and Japanese bonds has widened quite a bit lately," he says. "The pair may test the recent high" of 82.00 later in the global day, a level which was marked on March 18 following the G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yield spread between 10-year benchmark U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond stands at 219.6 bps vs 207.4 bps a week ago. Still, the pair's rise may be limited on selling from Japan exporters around 82.00, the dealer says. The USD/JPY is at 81.77. He tips support on any falls at 81.40. The EUR/USD may trade in a 1.4040-1.4130 band, he says; it is at 1.4078. The EUR/JPY is tipped to trade in a 114.80-115.50 range, it is at 115.15.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian Treasurer Wayne Swan calls a 2345 GMT press conference which may see him announce new appointments to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 's policy-making board. Businessman \uc2 Donald McGauchie\uc2 's term ended this month. Newspaper speculation suggests Swan could also announce the replacement for \uc2 Warwick McKibbin\uc2  whose term ends midyear. Swan is set to appoint former \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist and Labor party veteran John Edwards and \uc2 BG Group\uc2  Managing Director \uc2 Catherine Tanna\uc2  to the board, according to the Australian Financial Review newspaper, which doesn't cite its sources. A spokesman for the Treasurer declines to comment. The RBA board meets next week and is widely expected to keep interest rates on hold. Appointing Edwards could be controversial. He is a former adviser to Labor Prime Minister Paul Keating, which raises questions about his objectivity. james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets may trade with a slight negative bias amid modest losses for U.S. stocks on Monday, and lack of clear positive leads; the unrest in Libya and painfully slow progress to bring Japan's nuclear crisis under control may continue to temper buying activity. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro looks set to trade in tight ranges against the dollar and the yen as renewed demand for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of expectations the ECB will hike next month is likely to be offset somewhat by Portugal's economic travails. ECB President Trichet on Monday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on track to hike rates in April, re-focussing some traders' attention on interest rate differentials which are in favor of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro is at 1.4080, from 1.4087 late Monday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 115.10, from 115.12, and the USD/JPY is at 81.74, compared with 81.67. On the data front, South Korea has February BOP data at 2300 GMT, Japan releases February household spending and February jobless reports at 2330 GMT, and February preliminary retail sales data at 2350 GMT. Later, St. Louis Fed President Bullard speaks in Prague, the U.S. releases January S&P/Case-Shiller Home Price Index, March consumer confidence and the weekly API gasoline and crude stocks data. (shri.navaratnam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's February trade surplus of NZ$194 million shows high commodity prices continue to support export values although there is some evidence of moderation in world prices, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Philip Borkin. "Looking forward, the outlook for NZ's trade balance is naturally heavily dependent on the performance of soft commodity prices and global demand," he says. Exports totaled NZ$3.87 billion in February vs NZ$3.3 billion in January. Borkin adds imports are also likely to increase in the near term as businesses replace stock damaged in the February earthquake. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold is marginally up after its informal open at 2200 GMT, last at $1,420.80/oz vs $1,419.50/oz open, following three consecutive down sessions on improving economic data. ANZ sees positive U.S. consumer spending and pending home sales data driving yesterday's fall, but sees ongoing strength for the metal as the sagas in Japan and the Middle East go on. Standard Bank points to continuing rising net length in CFTC data to suggest that the metal will be well-supported: "Should safe-haven demand remain intact, we could see gold recover from the liquidations seen in the aftermath of the earthquake in Japan." (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is likely to weaken slightly Tuesday after a late sell-off on Wall Street saw financials, materials and energy stocks fall. IG Markets institutional dealer Chris Weston tips a 7-point opening fall to 4726.0 and a further dip toward 4700.0. He expects Japan's Nikkei index to open down 1.3% after plutonium was discovered in soil at the Fukushima nuclear power plant. Uranium stocks could lead declines after their U.S. peers tumbled, due to Japan's nuclear crisis and a sharp increase in support for the Green Party in German state elections. Otherwise, the Woodside (WPL.AU) M&A rumors continue to swirl, with the Australian Financial Review saying Woodside is working with long-term advisers \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Grant Samuel to consider a potential deal with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU), and that BHP is evaluating a deal involving Shell's 24% stake. S&P/ASX 200 resistance from the downtrend line drawn from the February peak falls to 4740.0 today, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Potential support lies at the 20-day moving average near 4697.0. Index last 4733.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2238 GMT [Dow Jones] With the AUD/USD cross topping out at around 1.0314 overnight, it was more than 3% above \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Australia Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's fair value estimate of parity, says John Kyriakopoulos, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at the bank. He adds, generally speaking, AUD/USD trading 3% or more above "fair value" provides a warning of a correction. He says it's not clear where the catalyst for another climb in the AUD/USD will come in the near term, adds a ramping up in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate hike expectations is unlikely while another broad-based fall in the USD is also looking like a long shot. For the moment, Kyriakopoulos can't see a significant pull back in the AUD/USD as dips will be met by solid demand. He says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has proved to be pretty resilient to a European sovereign-debt crisis, Middle East unrest pushing up the crude oil price sharply and the Japanese earthquake, tsunami and nuclear crisis. The AUD/USD is at 1.0247. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 03-28-11 1925ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2237 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are mostly bearish, though overnight SPI 200 futures rose 0.1% to 4758.0. Wall Street's S&P 500 fell 0.3% to 1310.19, with Financials down 0.3%, Materials down 0.5% and Energy down 0.1%. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs fell to equivalent A$44.18 vs A$44.45 locally. In London, BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) rose 0.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RIO.AU) rose 0.8%. The S&P 500 hit a 13-day high of 1319.74 on stronger-than-expected economic data and deal flow, before turning down on profit-taking, although NYSE volume was very light. U.S. February consumer spending rose 0.7% vs 0.6% expected and pending home sales rose 2.1% vs 0.2% expected. Ebay agreed to buy GSI Commerce for US$2.4 billion. Uranium stocks fell 3.7%-6.0% after plutonium was detected in soil near Japan's Fukushima nuclear plant and Germany's Green Party won nearly a quarter of the votes in state elections in Baden-Wuerttemberg. Nymex May crude oil futures fell US$1.42 to US$103.98. LME copper fell 1.6%. Spot gold fell US$1.62 to US$1,429.70. AUD/USD hit a post-float high of 1.0294. S&P/ASX 200 last 4733.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-28-11 1925ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110328e73s000c6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
