\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro struggles to hold gains after Fed comments\b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Euro hits day's low, Fed comments clash with ECB view\par \par \uc2 * Support at $1.40, but seen in range below $1.4250\par \par \uc2 * Euro/yen at post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  high; Dlr/yen inches up\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Jessica Mortimer\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 29 (Reuters) - The euro struggled to hold gains against the dollar on Tuesday, as expectations for a euro zone interest rate rise were offset by a Federal Reserve warning about keeping U.S. monetary policy loose for too long.\par \par \uc2 The euro rose to around $1.4150 before retreating as traders took profits on its gains after Fed President James Bullard said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may normalise monetary policy before global uncertainties are resolved. [ID:nLDE72S0RJ]\par \par \uc2 Further comments about a possible cut to the Fed's second round of quantitative easing pushed the euro to a session low, although bids around $1.4050, including those from sovereign names, were seen limiting further near-term losses.\par \par \uc2 The euro has retreated from a 4 1/2-month high of $1.4249 hit last week, but has been supported on the view the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may raise interest rates as early as next month.\par \par \uc2 Comments from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet on Monday bolstered this view as he said inflation was "durably" above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target. [ID:nLDE72R20A]\par \par \uc2 "Euro/dollar is trading at the moment on two factors -- interest rate differentials and the technical configuration," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Nordea in Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 "The euro failed to break below $1.40 yesterday, and last week it disappointed on the upside, failing to test the November highs (at $1.4283), so it is likely to stay in a $1.40-$1.42 range".\par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> was a touch lower on the day at $1.4060. It pushed through bids seen around $1.4080, but hovered above a low of $1.4021 hit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The $1.40 level is supported by a trendline drawn from the low below $1.30 hit in January, while the euro's 21-day moving average stands just above that level, at around $1.4006.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, heavy options-related barriers around $1.4250 were expected to cap gains. A break of that level may see a test of the November high of $1.4283.\par \par \uc2 Many in the market argued the euro would likely stick to the $1.40-$1.4250 range ahead of U.S. payrolls data due on Friday, while low implied volatility levels suggested a break-out on either side was unlikely in the near term.\par \par \uc2 One-month euro/dollar implied vol <EUR1MO=> traded around 9.9 percent on Tuesday, near its lowest level in roughly a year.\par \par \uc2 "One-month (vol), which still covers the post-Easter lull, remains reasonably offered," Citi analysts said in a note. "We feel that it is hard to see it pop with everyone keen to jump on the selling bandwagon whenever it does start moving higher."\par \par \uc2 RATE OUTLOOK SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 Investors continued to focus on the prospect of higher rates and shrugged off the euro zone debt crisis, despite rising concerns about Portugal's ability to finance itself as the country prepares for a snap election. [ID:nLDE72R1RI]\par \par \uc2 The euro also rose 0.5 percent against the yen <EURJPY=R> to around 115.64 yen, its highest level since Group of Seven central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  jointly at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's request to curb the yen's appreciation following a devastating earthquake. It was on course to test 116.03, above which would mark a 10-month high.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.5 percent to 82.10 yen <JPY=>, with traders citing demand from UK and European banks. Gains were capped by offers, including those from Japanese exporters, around 82.00 yen. Further up, orders were seen around 82.50 and 83.00.\par \par \uc2 The rise in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the yen has quelled speculation of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Many in the market anticipate a fall to around 80 yen or lower will be required for Tokyo to re-enter the market to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar index <.DXY>, which tracks the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's performance against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , edged up 0.2 percent to 76.298.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 5)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110327e73r000r6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
