\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Singapore Govt Bonds Likely Down;Steeper Curve Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Singapore government bonds are likely to fall, steepening the yield curve, in line with Thursday's fall in U.S. Treasurys, a trader at a local bank says. "There are few domestic cues at the moment, and the Japan crisis has been priced in already; the market is no longer reacting much to it." She expects bond yields will remain flat at the front-end of the curve, while the 10-year yield may rise 3 bps. The two-year government bond yield was flat at 0.39% Thursday, while the 10-year yield eased 1 bp to 2.38%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may fall vs 6.5618 in late Asian trade Thursday, tracking the greenback's weakness vs the EUR overnight. A Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank expects the central parity to be set near 6.5600 vs Thursday's 6.5625. "If the fixing is lower than 6.5600, we could see more dollar demand because of month-end import settlement needs," says the trader. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares are likely to rise for the sixth straight session, but mounting profit-taking pressure near 8700 will likely keep any gains minimal, says Capital Securities analyst Diana Wu; the Taiex ends +0.4% at 8576.40 Thursday. She tips financial stocks will continue to outperform the broader market on higher local interest rates and hopes that more smaller and state-owned banks will merge to increase their scale and efficiencies; DRAM makers and companies in the supply chain of iPhones and iPads may also rise, but their upside is likely limited as Wu says their share prices have largely priced in those positive factors. She adds the stock market will continue to be rangebound in the medium term, with 8200-8800 being the broader range for the Taiex. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 1.1% at 9533.80, off its intraday high of 9569.71, as investors digest buoyant overnight U.S. earnings and jobs data. Beyond current trading levels, however, the Nikkei's upside will continue to be capped by lingering worries on the nuclear crisis in Japan, says Toshiyuki Kanayama, market analyst at Monex. Ahead of the weekend, investors are also wary of opening fresh positions as the situation at Tepco's (9501.TO) troubled Fukushima nuclear plant remains fluid, which carries the possibility of quickly worsening, he adds. He pegs today's Nikkei range at 9,500-9,600. 32/33 Topix subindexes are higher, with Tepco, now off 6.5% at Y843 dragging utilities down. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) is up 3.8% at Y2,647 after a Deutsche Securities upgrade to Buy from Hold. Auto shares are also rising on bargain-hunting after recent weakness and hopes for production resumptions. Toyota (7203.TO) is up 1.2% at Y3,255. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Foster's (FGL.AU) is up 2% at A$5.71 after hitting A$5.84 as talk of large AUD/EUR buying last night is fueling takeover speculation in Foster's as well as Woodside (WPL.AU), traders say. Foster's beer assets are certainly up for grabs and interest from overseas buyers has long been touted as a possibility. Reports last year tipped \uc2 SABMiller\uc2  (SBMRY) as a potential suitor with an offer of GBP7 billion for the Foster's beer assets. Foster's is demerging its beer and wine assets, a move that's been viewed as an invitation for takeover bids for the Australian brewer's beer unit, its crown jewel. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is down after it takes out the 1,120 support at the market's open amid Kospi strength (now up 0.9%), traders say; the USD/KRW is at 1,116.40 vs 1,121.00 late Thursday. "The pair started the day at a lower level than I'd expected, while a strong opening for the Kospi has also triggered some stop-loss sell orders on the dollar," says a local bank trader, who pegs support at 1,114. He adds, following the gap down earlier, "there's a lot more real dollar demand from dollar-buyers such as Korean corporates," while more fund repatriation related to dividend payouts to foreign investors is expected to provide support as well. Traders note offshore players aren't showing any strong bias on the KRW yet, with eyes also on any possible BOK action to curb KRW volatility. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones]The USD/MYR is a tad lower at 3.0260 from 3.0265 late Thursday as players turn ringgit bullish on growing expectations of a Malaysian policy rate hike in May amid worries over rising inflation. Three dealers say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been spotted at 3.0260 to ensure that the pair doesn't fall too sharply. "It looks like the pair will again be trapped in a tight range," says a dealer at a local bank; he tips the pair to trade in the 3.0240-3.0300 band for the day as investors await February CPI data due at 0900 GMT. A Dow Jones poll tips February CPI rises 2.6% on year, slightly higher than the 2.4% gain in January. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are likely to consolidate despite excess liquidity in the money market, as investors remain cautious about ongoing inflationary pressure, says Shenyin Wanguo Securities analyst Li Xiaoxuan. China's top economic planner, the National Development and Reform Commission, earlier said China's March CPI is likely to rise around 5.0% on-year, mainly boosted by higher food and residence prices; CPI is likely to grow around 4.9% in 1Q and increase 4.8%-5.0% in 1H11, the NDRC said in a report published in the China Securities Journal. The Shanghai Composite Index may face immediate resistance at 2950 after ending down 0.1% at 2946.71 Thursday. "The Shanghai Composite Index is likely to stay in a narrow 2900-2950 range in the coming sessions," Li says. The Shenzhen Composite Index ended down 0.04% at 1299.53 Thursday.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is trading around 1.2615, down from 1.2630 in late Asia trade Thursday. The pair touched a new overnight record low of 1.2600, as strong equity performance in the U.S. and continuing market focus on expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening in Singapore is lending the SGD strong upside. A local bank strategist says the market continues to be unperturbed about the burgeoning euro zone debt problems; he tips support at 1.2575 and resistance at 1.2640.(martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the JPY thus far is thought to be relatively small, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rest of the G-7 appear to be working. It says the G-7's commitment to JPY stability may be tested end-March, Japan's fiscal year-end. "There are mixed reports on the needs of exporters and corporates, with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges possibly knocked out in the sharp move lower last week. Equally, their needs have likely been diminished given the supply disruptions post the earthquake," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . It says there's a perception there may be fiscal-year-end JPY demand, "but the cross-currents mean such demand is not a certainty. The perception of the seasonal trend may see the market test the authorities' desire for a stable JPY in this case. If the JPY does strengthen, for the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be ultimately successful, we will need to see an increase in physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the coming week." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Slowing foreign fund inflows amid concerns over the Portugal's debt crisis will likely keep investors cautious and the USD/TWD is expected to remain in a tight 29.55-29.65 range vs 29.583 in late Asian trade Thursday, says a trader from a foreign bank. He says the pair finds "a quite comfortable support" at 29.50 for the moment, "as the problem in Portugal (sovereign debt) and Japan (nuclear crisis) have raised investors' appetite for the U.S. dollar lately," but the pair's upside is likely to be limited as local stocks are expected to rise following Wall Street's advance overnight. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may rise, taking strength from the gains on Wall Sreet and higher regional bourses. On Thursday, the STI index rises 0.7% to 3043.03, its fourth straight session in the green. Phillip Securities says "risk appetite is coming back...the STI is likely to continue higher today from the positive U.S. session. Yesterday's high for the STI tested a major resistance region at 3050...a push and close above 3050 would increase the odds of the intermediate-term trend shifting, especially as more resistance levels are turned into support." The house adds investors with a longer-term horizon could consider gradually scaling into long positions; "that said, our preferred indication of a shift in the intermediate-term trend continues to be higher highs and higher lows (which we have yet to see)." It tips resistance at 3050, then 3070 and 3100, while support is at 3025, then 3010 and 3000. \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG) may be in focus after it increased the size of its 2014 convertible bond offering by S$100 million to S$350 million. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 2050ET ]\par 