\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Shrs Likely To Consolidate; 2950 Cap Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are likely to consolidate despite excess liquidity in the money market, as investors remain cautious about ongoing inflationary pressure, says Shenyin Wanguo Securities analyst Li Xiaoxuan. China's top economic planner, the National Development and Reform Commission, earlier said China's March CPI is likely to rise around 5.0% on-year, mainly boosted by higher food and residence prices; CPI is likely to grow around 4.9% in 1Q and increase 4.8%-5.0% in 1H11, the NDRC said in a report published in the China Securities Journal. The Shanghai Composite Index may face immediate resistance at 2950 after ending down 0.1% at 2946.71 Thursday. "The Shanghai Composite Index is likely to stay in a narrow 2900-2950 range in the coming sessions," Li says. The Shenzhen Composite Index ended down 0.04% at 1299.53 Thursday.(ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is trading around 1.2615, down from 1.2630 in late Asia trade Thursday. The pair touched a new overnight record low of 1.2600, as strong equity performance in the U.S. and continuing market focus on expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening in Singapore is lending the SGD strong upside. A local bank strategist says the market continues to be unperturbed about the burgeoning euro zone debt problems; he tips support at 1.2575 and resistance at 1.2640.(martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the JPY thus far is thought to be relatively small, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rhetoric from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rest of the G-7 appear to be working. It says the G-7's commitment to JPY stability may be tested end-March, Japan's fiscal year-end. "There are mixed reports on the needs of exporters and corporates, with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges possibly knocked out in the sharp move lower last week. Equally, their needs have likely been diminished given the supply disruptions post the earthquake," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . It says there's a perception there may be fiscal-year-end JPY demand, "but the cross-currents mean such demand is not a certainty. The perception of the seasonal trend may see the market test the authorities' desire for a stable JPY in this case. If the JPY does strengthen, for the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be ultimately successful, we will need to see an increase in physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the coming week." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Slowing foreign fund inflows amid concerns over the Portugal's debt crisis will likely keep investors cautious and the USD/TWD is expected to remain in a tight 29.55-29.65 range vs 29.583 in late Asian trade Thursday, says a trader from a foreign bank. He says the pair finds "a quite comfortable support" at 29.50 for the moment, "as the problem in Portugal (sovereign debt) and Japan (nuclear crisis) have raised investors' appetite for the U.S. dollar lately," but the pair's upside is likely to be limited as local stocks are expected to rise following Wall Street's advance overnight. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may rise, taking strength from the gains on Wall Sreet and higher regional bourses. On Thursday, the STI index rises 0.7% to 3043.03, its fourth straight session in the green. Phillip Securities says "risk appetite is coming back...the STI is likely to continue higher today from the positive U.S. session. Yesterday's high for the STI tested a major resistance region at 3050...a push and close above 3050 would increase the odds of the intermediate-term trend shifting, especially as more resistance levels are turned into support." The house adds investors with a longer-term horizon could consider gradually scaling into long positions; "that said, our preferred indication of a shift in the intermediate-term trend continues to be higher highs and higher lows (which we have yet to see)." It tips resistance at 3050, then 3070 and 3100, while support is at 3025, then 3010 and 3000. \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG) may be in focus after it increased the size of its 2014 convertible bond offering by S$100 million to S$350 million. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0029 GMT [Dow Jones] Woodside (WPL.AU) shares jump over 5% before settling a little to be up 4% at A$47.39 as takeover speculation bubbles about a possible takeover bid, potentially from usual suspect BHP Billiton (BHP.AU). Broker chat has been triggered by a large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade this morning, which some brokers say involved a large U.K. bank buying A$9 billion using euros. One broker notes that the amount happens to equate to the market capitalization of Shell's remaining 24% stake in Woodside. "There is talk around the market that BHP will make a tilt at Woodside," the broker, from a large international investment bank says in a note to clients. Other traders, however, say that while there has been some offshore buying of Woodside, speculation is simply being fueled by a bullish broker report from Southern Cross Equities that recommended that investors buy Woodside before a corporate jumps in. Woodside could also be continuing to benefit from damage to a nuclear power plant in Japan, which is set to increase longer demand for LNG. Woodside up about 14% since March 14.(Ross.Kelly@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD recoups losses after it fell to an intraday low of 1.4150 on news S&P downgraded its credit rating on Portugal from A-to BBB. "There are plenty of investors who wish to buy on dips," says Satoshi Tate, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 , adding investors think the EUR's upward momentum remains intact amid growing expectations the ECB may hike rates in April. "The market has priced in bad news related to Portugal to some extent, so the impact is limited," another trader at a Japanese bank says. The EUR/USD is at 1.4170 and Tate tips it to trade a 1.4140-1.4220 range for the rest of the global day.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com, tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its policy rates by another 75 bps in the course of the year after an initial 25bp hike Thursday--the first since July 2009 and after a series of rate cuts totaling 200 bps from December 2008. "We remain constructive on Philippines' growth, and believe that rising inflationary pressures, especially from domestic sources, continue to warrant more tightening through policy rate hikes and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation...we continue to look for appreciation of the PHP," the house says. It projects USD/PHP at 41.30, 40.80 and 40.30 on a three-, six- and 12-month horizon respectively; the pair was at 43.42 late Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0016 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI may rise, taking the lead from firmer U.S. stocks Thursday, says a dealer with a local brokerage. "Sentiment is expected to improve as people are beginning to look past the Japan crisis and turmoil in Libya," says the dealer; he tips the index to break the 100-day moving average of 1515 and says it may test 1525, the 50% Fibonacci retracement (of the rise from 1474 to 1576), which also matches the 50-day moving average level. The dealer says Gamuda (5398.KU) may rise on better earnings while RHB Capital (1066.KU) may be in focus on local daily The New Straits Times report, citing unnamed sources, that \uc2 Abu Dhabi Commercial Bank\uc2  or ADCB, the United Arab Emirates' third largest bank by assets, is looking to sell its 25% stake RHB Capital in entirety to a strategic investor. RHB Capital could not be immediately reached for comment. The KLCI ended 0.1% higher at 1513.84 Thursday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open lower on weakness in U.S. Treasurys overnight and early gains in the Nikkei (now +1.2%); the contract is currently down 0.19 at 139.73. JGBs are likely to move in a narrow range, following movements in share prices, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii; if the Nikkei becomes top-heavy due to uncertainties over the economic outlook, JGBs are likely to trim some of their falls, he adds. 10-year cash bonds are yet to change hands; a 1.210%-1.230% yield band is tipped for the day, compared to 1.205% at Thursday's close. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is likely to open higher as foreigners are expected to continue to buy stocks and amid expectations of strong 1Q earnings at some companies; "foreigners have net bought Korean stocks for the past six sessions and market sentiment seems to be recovering because risks such as the aftermath of the Japan quake and tsunami as well as the turmoil in Libya are already reflected," says Oh On-su at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; he adds that unless there is a new explosion at the nuclear power plant in Fukushima, the Kospi may get a boost from solid 1Q expectations. Resistance is tipped at 2070 for the day. He adds shipbuilding, steelmaker and auto plays stand to gain on rising orders for ships, steel products and vehicles. "Shipbuilders have asked Posco to produce more steel to prepare for a possible lack of shipbuilding plates from Japan, as they expect an increase in new orders this year from overseas clients amid rising oil prices." He adds that investors may take profit on energy stocks such as \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) and \uc2 GS Holdings\uc2  (078930.SE) after their recent gains. The Kopsi ends up 1.2% at 2036.78 Thursday. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 2043ET ]\par \par \uc2 2347 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's core consumer price index falls 0.3% from a year earlier in February, marking the 24th-straight month of decline as deflation persists at the retail level despite the recent sharp rise in commodity prices. The figure matches economist forecasts and follows a 0.2% fall in the previous month. Further pressure on the CPI is expected following the March 11 earthquake, with a sharp downturn in consumer demand expected in the short-term. Tokyo area CPI for March, which offers a glimpse to the national figure released a month later, also showed a 0.3% drop in the core rate, but the data excludes the impact from the quake. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 2043ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110325e73p0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
