\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Weak Retail Sales To Impact UK GDP -Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1006 GMT [Dow Jones] Wednesday's UK February retail sales figures (-0.8% m/m vs -0.6% m/m consensus) highlight underlying high street conditions are weak as consumers are buffeted by falling real wages and higher unemployment. Based on these figures 'it is difficult to see how consumer spending will able to contribute positively to GDP growth in 1Q' according to Daiwa Capital Markets. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1004 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares trade higher led by oil stocks; the benchmark KSE-100 index is up 0.6% at 11,571.20. "Oil stocks are witnessing buying interest on expectations the government will raise prices in the upcoming review of domestic petroleum product prices", says a dealer. He adds, with global crude oil price above $100 a barrel - hopes are the government may rise product prices by 10%-15%; the oil price revision will likely be on March 31. The dealer says markets could remain in a 11,400-11,700 band near-term, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy outcome due Saturday dictating future trends. Most analysts expect interest rates to remain unchanged at 14%. \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  (PSO.KA) is up 0.6% at PKR275.75, and Attock Refinery (ATRL.KA) is up 1.1% at PKR355.60. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] Sweden's producer price index is unchanged on the month in February as a stronger krona and lower electricity prices offset rising international prices in the index, says SEB. "Currently there is thus no large upward pressure on CPI from producer prices," the bank adds. "Going forward PPI prices are expected to accelerate as international prices continue to rise while the impact from the stronger krona will dwindle," SEB adds. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0959 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is down at 8,720 from 8,725 late Wednesday, but off its intraday low of 8,710 as some investors sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take profit, traders say. "Foreign and local names are pretty much balanced, with locals mostly taking profit in the evening session," a local trader says. The market still expects the rupiah to maintain a broad upward trend against the USD, but worries relating to Japan, Libya and peripheral Europe continue to hurt the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they say. Foreigners were net buyers of IDR858 billion worth of stocks. The dealer tips the pair to track a 8,710-8,740 band Friday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end up 0.7% at 1034.39, the highest close since Jan. 19, in active trade. Banks are now being propelled higher as lending continues to be robust so far this year, say traders. "While its bullish, its certainly rotational, not across the board. People are looking for stocks that have underperformed and picking them up," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. Resistance is seen at 1040, with support at 1030, then 1025. Among the most actively traded stocks, Banpu (BANPU.TH) rises 2.4% to THB766, \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) is up 9.1% at THB2.88, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) climbs 4.4% to THB2.36, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) gains 0.5% to THB54.25, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) is up 3.4% at THB18.40, \uc2 Bank of Ayudhya\uc2  (BAY.TH) is 2.5% higher at THB24.50, and \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) rises 1.0% to THB104.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys closed near their low of the day in US trade before picking up slightly during Asian hours with better buying interest seen from Japan. In London trade Treasury prices are lower, driven more on events in Europe than fundamentals, while thin volumes are exaggerating any moves. The June 10-year contract is 2 ticks in negative territory at 119.290. In the cash market, 2-year paper yields 0.66% while the 10-year bonds yields 3.35%. Ahead we have durable goods and initial claims as well as an USD11 billion 10-year TIPS auction. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall to session lows with comments from \uc2 Moody's\uc2  being cited for the weakness. Although the weaker economic growth prospects in 2011 and 2012 do not directly cast doubt on the UK's sovereign rating level, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  believes that slower growth combined with weaker-than-expected fiscal consolidation could cause the UK's debt metrics to deteriorate to a point that would be inconsistent with a Aaa rating. June gilts are down 0.21 at 118.07. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia are lower as some traders cut positions after a rally in the previous session, says a trader at a state-run bank. He adds, "this is profit-taking only, as inflation is still at elevated levels and the cash crunch is persisting." The one-year premia are at 2.76 from 2.81 late Friday. He tips the one-year premia to remain in a 2.75-2.90 band near-term. For the week ending March 12, the year-on-year inflation rate for food articles edged up to 10.05% from 9.42%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is steady at 30.27-30.29 vs 30.28-30.29 late Wednesday. Trade is moderate as selling by gold traders offsets month-end USD demand for trade settlements and fund repatriation ahead of Japanese corporate book-closing, says a Bangkok-based dealer. "We see quite a number of sale orders from gold traders today," she says. The pair is tipped in a 30.25-30.35 range Friday, with traders widely expecting the BOT to continue supporting the USD/THB should it fall to the 30.25 level. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0942 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling has taken a sharp hit across the board after February's UK retail sales disappointed at -0.8%. GBP/USD sheds over half a cent to the day's low of 1.6150, EUR/GBP trades at the day's high of 0.8733 and the pound is at the day's lows vs pretty much across the board. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's core CPI likely fell 0.3% on-year in February, according to the median forecast in a poll of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. Any fall would mark the 24th straight month of declines as deflation persists due to sluggish domestic demand, despite upward price pressure from higher commodity costs. Analysts say it's uncertain how the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 will affect prices ahead. The Bank of Japan's decision to ease monetary policy further after the disaster, along with eventual reconstruction spending, should put upward pressure on prices. But consumer spending is likely to fall as shoppers curb non-essential purchases. Radioactive contamination amid ongoing problems with the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors will also depress consumer sentiment, analysts say. Meanwhile, the persistently strong yen, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a pledge on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G-7, could also keep prices depressed by lowering import costs. Data are due at 2330 GMT. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD's break back below 1.6280 combined with the Bearish Engulfing candle posted Wednesday signals the expected rise to 1.6460 will be more choppy than first thought says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 1.6205 the bank says near-term risks are for a deeper pullback toward 1.6140-1.6120 and then 1.5980-1.5960 from where a base should be seen. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 0606ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110324e73o0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
