he 10-year bonds yields 3.35%. Ahead we have durable goods and initial claims as well as an USD11 billion 10-year TIPS auction. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall to session lows with comments from \uc2 Moody's\uc2  being cited for the weakness. Although the weaker economic growth prospects in 2011 and 2012 do not directly cast doubt on the UK's sovereign rating level, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  believes that slower growth combined with weaker-than-expected fiscal consolidation could cause the UK's debt metrics to deteriorate to a point that would be inconsistent with a Aaa rating. June gilts are down 0.21 at 118.07. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0947 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia are lower as some traders cut positions after a rally in the previous session, says a trader at a state-run bank. He adds, "this is profit-taking only, as inflation is still at elevated levels and the cash crunch is persisting." The one-year premia are at 2.76 from 2.81 late Friday. He tips the one-year premia to remain in a 2.75-2.90 band near-term. For the week ending March 12, the year-on-year inflation rate for food articles edged up to 10.05% from 9.42%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is steady at 30.27-30.29 vs 30.28-30.29 late Wednesday. Trade is moderate as selling by gold traders offsets month-end USD demand for trade settlements and fund repatriation ahead of Japanese corporate book-closing, says a Bangkok-based dealer. "We see quite a number of sale orders from gold traders today," she says. The pair is tipped in a 30.25-30.35 range Friday, with traders widely expecting the BOT to continue supporting the USD/THB should it fall to the 30.25 level. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0942 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling has taken a sharp hit across the board after February's UK retail sales disappointed at -0.8%. GBP/USD sheds over half a cent to the day's low of 1.6150, EUR/GBP trades at the day's high of 0.8733 and the pound is at the day's lows vs pretty much across the board. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0936 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's core CPI likely fell 0.3% on-year in February, according to the median forecast in a poll of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. Any fall would mark the 24th straight month of declines as deflation persists due to sluggish domestic demand, despite upward price pressure from higher commodity costs. Analysts say it's uncertain how the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 will affect prices ahead. The Bank of Japan's decision to ease monetary policy further after the disaster, along with eventual reconstruction spending, should put upward pressure on prices. But consumer spending is likely to fall as shoppers curb non-essential purchases. Radioactive contamination amid ongoing problems with the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors will also depress consumer sentiment, analysts say. Meanwhile, the persistently strong yen, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a pledge on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G-7, could also keep prices depressed by lowering import costs. Data are due at 2330 GMT. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD's break back below 1.6280 combined with the Bearish Engulfing candle posted Wednesday signals the expected rise to 1.6460 will be more choppy than first thought says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 1.6205 the bank says near-term risks are for a deeper pullback toward 1.6140-1.6120 and then 1.5980-1.5960 from where a base should be seen. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are higher in moderate trade with sentiment bolstered by news of tighter supply ahead, says a bond trader. Public Debt Management Office head Chakkrit Parapuntakul tells Dow Jones Newswires that supply for the current fiscal year will be cut to under THB400 billion vs THB453.5 billion previously indicated due to the government's robust fiscal health. The trader adds, "we're seeing yields fall more explicitly in the far-end of the curve. Even with supportive supply news, front-end yields are not falling as much as most people still expect further rate hikes ahead." Yields are expected to stay rangebound Friday, with the focus on details of 3Q bond supply, due next week. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.27%/3.26% from 3.275%/3.265% late Wednesday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.51%/3.49% from 3.54%/3.52%, and the December 2021 yield is at 3.69%/3.68% from 3.73%/3.72%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 0604ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110324e73o0005x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
