\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Japan Economy To Improve In 2012, But Debt Will Also Rise-Experts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chana R. Schoenberger Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Japan's economy will pick up again by next year, but sovereign debt will balloon after the devastating earthquake and tsunami that triggered a nuclear-plant crisis two weeks ago, experts said Wednesday at a Japan Society panel on the quake's aftermath.\par \par \uc2 The country's gross domestic product is likely to grow 1% this year and 2.5% next year, said panelist Takashi Imamura, vice president and general manager of \uc2 Marubeni America Corp\uc2 ., the U.S. arm of Japanese trading house \uc2 Marubeni Corp\uc2 . (8002.TO, MARUY).\par \par \uc2 Citing Japanese government estimates that rebuilding after what is now the world's most expensive natural disaster could cost upwards of $300 billion, Imamura said the country will have to choose between issuing bonds and raising taxes. 'In Japan the quickest way is to use public funding,' rather than privatizing disaster-relief construction projects, he said.\par \par \uc2 Panelist Paul Scalise, an energy economist who studies Japan at Temple University's Tokyo campus, said in an interview with Dow Jones Tuesday ahead of the panel that he expects the Japanese to pour their vaunted household savings into reconstruction as the government sells bonds to finance the effort. 'I think demand for massive restructuring efforts alters the domestic balance between savings and investment,' he said.\par \par \uc2 As part of a concerted monetary-policy push to support the economy after the quake, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, as it did last week, but 'I suspect it will come to naught,' Scalise said. The yen hit record highs of Y76.25 to the dollar before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in conjunction with its counterparts from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations.\par \par \uc2 The yen's best-case scenario shows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back where it traded before the quake, in the low Y80-to-the-dollar range, panelist Robert Dujarric, director of the Institute of Contemporary Asian Studies at Temple University's Tokyo campus, said in an interview with Dow Jones Tuesday. In the worst case, falling exports will cause the yen to weaken as well, he said.\par \par \uc2 Dujarric discounted worries that manufacturing in the devastated Tohoku region would cause serious disruptions. He noted that major manufacturers would likely move affected production elsewhere while factories and distribution networks are repaired.\par \par \uc2 'If you're really a critical component manufacturer, you have such enormous pricing power,' Dujarric said in an interview with Dow Jones. 'If you say, 'I make a $5 piece for the iPad and I'm going to charge $10 for it,' Apple's going to go for it.' The larger concern is the power crisis, especially if rolling blackouts continue through April, he said. Although downtown Tokyo, home to most of the country's corporate headquarters, isn't likely to see power outages, workers will likely have trouble commuting and won't spend as much on dining out or entertainment, hurting the country's GDP, he said.\par \par \uc2 Scalise, who has done research on the Japanese electric-power industry, said the difficulties in reconstructing the shattered power plants and in starting to build new power stations will lead to widespread shortages in the affected area. In the most dire prediction, he estimates that rolling blackouts could plague the region for several years as utilities scramble to build new plants. -By Chana R. Schoenberger, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4803; chana.schoenberger@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110325e73o001h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
