\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Malaysia Feb Inflation Likely +2.6% On-Year -Poll\b0\par\par\uc2 2,369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's consumer price index likely rose 2.6% on-year in February, according to the median forecast from a Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. In January, the index rose 2.4% on-year. "The accelerating inflation environment, not just in Malaysia, but also the broader region, will keep the market focused on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies. Inflation will remain a key challenge for Asia, including Malaysia, in 2011 and 2012," Alvin Liew, an economist at Singapore-based Standard Chartered Bank says. He adds, the robust domestic demand and higher wage costs in Malaysia are also likely put upward price pressures on other major categories in the CPI basket. The data are due at 0900 GMT; they were previously scheduled for release Wednesday. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's manufacturing output likely grew 6.3% on year in February, slower than January's +10.5%, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. Economists expect the pace of growth to ease as benefits of a low base of comparison wear off and the Lunar New Year holidays cut working days. "We attribute this to the Chinese New Year effect as factories typically see a temporary fall in production during the week of the Chinese New Year," Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says in a note. The MAS is likely to consider the manufacturing data ahead of its review of monetary policy next month, though it is likely to remain focused on inflation for now. On a seasonally adjusted basis, eight analysts tip factory output to have contracted 1.1% in February compared with a surprise 15.4% expansion in January when manufacturers rushed to complete orders before the Lunar New Year. The February manufacturing data are due at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets are likely to be biased higher as risk aversion eases; there's a positive cue from Wall Street and fear surrounding Japan's nuclear crisis and concerns about allied air strikes in Libya appear to be fading a little, though the market could still be spooked by new developments on either front. In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro is likely to be supported by continued expectations of ECB rate hikes; yen bulls will still be deterred by the possibility of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities. The EUR/USD is at 1.4181 vs 1.4177 in late NY trade Thursday, the EUR/JPY is at 114.87 vs 114.76 and the USD/JPY is at 81.00 vs 80.97. On the data slate, there's Japan's February CPI and Tokyo's March CPI at 2330 GMT, Japan's February CSPI at 2350 GMT, Philippines January trade at 0100 GMT, Singapore's February industrial production at 0500 GMT, and Malaysia's February CPI at 0900 GMT. Later there's the German Ifo survey, the second day of the European Council meeting in Brussels, the U.S.'s 3rd estimate for 4Q GDP, also corporate profits, the final Thomson Reuters/University of Michigan consumer sentiment index, and expected comments from various Fed officials--Minneapolis Fed president Kocherlakota, Chicago Fed president Evans, Dallas Fed president Fisher, Atlanta Fed president Lockhart and Philadelphia Fed president Plosser, and Treasury Secretary Geithner meets with regional business leaders. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2251 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 may test a minor channel resistance line at 4740.0 before fading on weekend profit-taking to close up about 0.5%, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. With Wall Street S&P 500 futures up 0.3% after the close, IG Markets expects the S&P/ASX 200 to open up 0.8% at 4738.0. Certainly, the S&P/ASX 200 has continued to underperform and a break above the 50% Fibonacci retracement at 4710.0 could trigger buying. But the factors boosting Wall Street were U.S.-centric, with technology sector earnings, together with strength in industrials and consumer discretionaries, driving gains. U.S. economic data remain patchy, with durable goods missing expectations. Flows from U.S. bonds to equities before the end of QE2 could be a global theme, but there are also risks for equities and commodities, which have been pumped by QE. The S&P 500 closed on the downtrend line from the February peak, at 1309.66. Equivalent S&P/ASX 200 resistance lies at 4756.0 and the 100-day moving average lies at 4750.0. Premier Investments (PMV.AU) could jump after appointing Mark McInnes CEO of Retail. S&P/ASX 200 last 4699.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2245 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Nymex crude futures will average $106/bbl this year, up 16% on its previous forecast of $91, partly reflecting the likelihood of prolonged shutdown of Libyan production including permanent damage to some fields. It says Brent crude will average $112/bbl this year, up from an earlier forecast of $91. Oil prices are also supported by spare capacity squeezed to near 3 million bbl/day, as Saudi Arabia ups output to cover lost Libyan barrels. Libya was producing 1.65 million bbl/day prior to the popular uprising; oil exports are now crimped by U.N. sanctions. BarCap ups its 2011 oil demand growth forecast to 1.76 million bbls/day, from a previous estimate of 1.56 million bbl/day. "We expect a sharp compression in the WTI-Brent spread as the year progresses, with the spread remaining prone to dislocation but normally tending towards a small WTI premium," BarCap adds. Nymex crude for May delivery is down seven cents at $105.53/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals market sentiment turned a little bearish overnight after U.S. durable goods orders fell 0.9% following an upwardly revised 3.6% rise in January. This compounded the effect of sovereign debt problems persisting in Europe, says a major market participant requesting anonymity. "Base metals prices are holding surprisingly well considering the continued flow of poor economic data that seems to be hitting the market of late," it says, adding, though, that losses in base metals were only limited by a weaker U.S. dollar against the euro. Separately, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it's technically "broadly bullish" across the commodities complex and a trend U.S. dollar decline is supporting base metals prices. This view includes copper, and it says the red metal's strong underlying bull trend will force prices higher, pegging initial resistance at $9,800. LME 3-month copper settled $12 lower overnight at $9,714/metric ton. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2226 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: The EUR rose broadly, reversing the previous day's declines as expectations of an interest rate hike by the ECB overwhelmed persistent concerns about the euro-zone debt crisis. "The fact that Portugal needs [financial] assistance is not going to surprise many people in the market," says Kevin Hebner, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "But certainly it does focus attention on European Union leaders" who are meeting Friday to sketch the outlines of a bailout package. Late Thursday, the EUR/USD was at 1.4177 vs 1.4087 late Wednesday, the USD/JPY was at 80.97 vs 80.92, the EUR/JPY was at 114.76 vs 113.99, the GBP/USD was at 1.6110 vs 1.6238, and the USD/CHF was at 0.9088 vs 0.9085. The USD index was at 75.679 vs 75.894. Stocks gained as technology companies' encouraging earnings and outlooks helped investors look past the deepening European sovereign debt crisis. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Micron Technology\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 8.4% after reporting a better-than-expected 15% jump in fiscal 2Q revenue. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nvidia\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 8%. Dell was up 0.4% after a senior company executive said the company hasn't seen any material disruption to its supply chain because of the earthquake. The Dow rose 0.7%, while the Nasdaq gained 1.4%. Investors cashed in on record gold prices but losses were kept in check as participants still need a refuge amid a political crisis in Portugal, fighting in Libya and Japan's nuclear troubles; April Comex gold lost 0.2%, to settle at $1,434.90/oz. Crude futures settled nearly flat as traders weighed rising turmoil in the Middle East against renewed concerns about Europe's sovereign debt crisis; May Nymex crude closed down 15 cents at $105.60/bbl. Treasurys prices fell as market participants brushed off fresh signs of stress for fiscally strapped euro-zone nations; the two-year yield rose 3.3 bps to 0.686%, while the 10-year yield was 6.6 bps higher at 3.411%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2217 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads are bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.6% at 4750.0 after Wall Street rose on strong technology sector earnings reports and improving U.S. jobs data. The S&P 500 rose 0.9% to 1309.66, while the Nasdaq rose 1.4% to 2736.42. In Europe, Fitch cut Portugal to A-, citing increased risks after its parliament rejected austerity measures. Euro zone and German manufacturing PMI data missed expectations, as did U.K. retail sales. U.S. initial weekly jobless claims fell 5,000 versus expectations of a flat result, while February durable goods fell 0.9% vs a 1.2% rise expected. In the tech sector, Micron rose 8.4% and Red Hat rose 18%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Home Depot\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 2.0%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 1.2%. S&P 500 rose 0.3% to 1297.54, with Consumer Discretionary up 1.4%, Industrials up 1.0%, Financials up 0.5%, Materials up 0.4% and Energy up 0.3%. London-listed BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) rose 1.4% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RIO.AU) rose 2.5%. BHP ADRs were steady at an equivalent A$44.69 vs A$44.71 locally. Nymex May crude oil futures fell 15 cents to US$105.60 after hitting US$106.69 amid the most intense allied bombing in Libya since the campaign began. LME prices mostly fell. [ 03-24-11 1900ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold fell US$7.00 to US$1,431.08. S&P/ASX 200 last 4699.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2201 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold posted a record intraday high overnight at $1,447.70/oz, at which point it had added 4.8% from March 15 and 10.6% during what has been a powerful bounce off $1,308.45 on Jan. 28. After hitting a new high, gold pulled back and was last bid $1,430.80, down $7.80 on the day. Support for gold came from safe haven buying from a raft of sources, but investors cashed in after record highs were posted. "This is a buying opportunity if anything," says Bob Haberkorn, senior market strategist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lind-Waldock\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Chicago. "People want some peace of mind, and they want to be in gold." Triland Metals broadly agrees, saying in a note "this is a buy the dip market and will be until gold closes below $1,400." (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2119 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices will face upward pressure Friday from a positive overnight lead from the U.S., where wheat futures surged as weather conditions threatened wheat and corn crops. CBOT May wheat jumped 25.25 cents, or 3.5%, at US$7.3950/bushel. Forecasts for dry weather rallied wheat as meteorologists cut rainfall projections in the U.S. Plains, a key wheat-growing region already struggling with dryness. Traders are trying to assess the prospects for U.S. crops after poor weather slashed global wheat output last year. Futures are trading near multi-year highs on concerns grain supplies won't keep pace with global demand. The AUD/USD cross is at 1.0211, extending this week's rally and sharply higher than yesterday's low at 1.0085, which will limit price gains for Australian wheat. On Thursday, the most active ASX milling wheat futures deliverable in January next year in New South Wales fell A$3.50 to A$291.00/metric ton, while those in Western Australia lost A$1.50 at A$305.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0937 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's core CPI likely fell 0.3% on-year in February, according to the median forecast in a poll of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. Any fall would mark the 24th straight month of declines as deflation persists due to sluggish domestic demand, despite upward price pressure from higher commodity costs. Analysts say it's uncertain how the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 will affect prices ahead. The Bank of Japan's decision to ease monetary policy further after the disaster, along with eventual reconstruction spending, should put upward pressure on prices. But consumer spending is likely to fall as shoppers curb non-essential purchases. Radioactive contamination amid ongoing problems with the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors will also depress consumer sentiment, analysts say. Meanwhile, the persistently strong yen, despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a pledge on joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the G-7, could also keep prices depressed by lowering import costs. Data are due at 2330 GMT. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 1900ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110324e73o000bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
