\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Core CPI Tipped Down 0.3% On-Year In Feb -Poll\b0\par\par\uc2 1,524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 10:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0936 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's core CPI likely fell 0.3% on-year in February, according to the median forecast in a poll of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. Any fall would mark the 24th straight month of declines as deflation persists due to sluggish domestic demand, despite upward price pressure from higher commodity costs. Analysts say it's uncertain how the devastating earthquake and tsunami that struck northeastern Japan on March 11 will affect prices ahead. The Bank of Japan's decision to ease monetary policy further after the disaster, along with eventual reconstruction spending, should put upward pressure on prices. But consumer spending is likely to fall as shoppers curb non-essential purchases. Radioactive contamination amid ongoing problems with the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors will also depress consumer sentiment, analysts say. Meanwhile, the persistently strong yen, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a pledge on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G-7, could also keep prices depressed by lowering import costs. Data are due at 2330 GMT. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD's break back below 1.6280 combined with the Bearish Engulfing candle posted Wednesday signals the expected rise to 1.6460 will be more choppy than first thought says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 1.6205 the bank says near-term risks are for a deeper pullback toward 1.6140-1.6120 and then 1.5980-1.5960 from where a base should be seen. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are higher in moderate trade with sentiment bolstered by news of tighter supply ahead, says a bond trader. Public Debt Management Office head Chakkrit Parapuntakul tells Dow Jones Newswires that supply for the current fiscal year will be cut to under THB400 billion vs THB453.5 billion previously indicated due to the government's robust fiscal health. The trader adds, "we're seeing yields fall more explicitly in the far-end of the curve. Even with supportive supply news, front-end yields are not falling as much as most people still expect further rate hikes ahead." Yields are expected to stay rangebound Friday, with the focus on details of 3Q bond supply, due next week. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.27%/3.26% from 3.275%/3.265% late Wednesday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.51%/3.49% from 3.54%/3.52%, and the December 2021 yield is at 3.69%/3.68% from 3.73%/3.72%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is 0.7% higher at 18,322.32 and is trading in a narrow 18,270-18,340 range, drawing on positive cues from European markets. "The range bound nature of trade shows the market is stabilizing and we are likely to spend another session around these levels," says Prakash Diwan, head of institutional business at Networth Stock Broking. Another local analyst says markets are keeping a close eye on crude prices and events in the Middle East and North Africa. The BSE market breadth is positive with 13 gainers for very 10 losers. ICICI Bank (532174.BY) is 1.0% up at INR1,050.90 while \uc2 Tata Consultancy Services\uc2  (532540.BY) is up 2.4% at INR1,089.40. \uc2 Bharat Heavy Electricals\uc2  (500103.BY) is up 2.7% at INR2,048.00 on news the company will participate in a $900 million contract awarded to ABB (ABB). Among losers, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is down 0.7% at INR1,005.50. Dow Jones technical analysis tips the Sensex in a 18,210-18,450 range.(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] Research from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  suggests that gilt issuance is now likely to be higher by an additional GBP68B in the four years 2012/13 to 2015/16 due to new U.K. PSNB projections. With slower economic growth and higher inflation now forecast, the Office For Budget Responsibility was forced to raise its 2011/2012 PSNB deficit projection by 0.3% of GDP. Wednesday's DMO announcement of gilt issuance for 2011/2012 saw total planned sales slightly higher than consensus at GBP169B. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PKR is lower at 85.30 late Thursday from 85.40 on selling from exporters, says a dealer; "during the session, the dollar hit a high of 85.65, but selling from exporters dominated the market." He says speculative buying from banks was a major reason for the rising dollar earlier in the session, but as importers' demand failed to materialise, the pair gave up gains. The dealer tips the pair to trade in a 85.40-85.50 range Friday. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end up 1.6% at 3611.641 in moderate volume, led by foreign buying in most commodity-related, bank and telecommunication stocks on hopes March inflation figures will fall, traders say. They expect the market to continue to gain Friday on further foreign program buying; traders expect the main index to top 3650. "Gains in many Asian markets and hopes for higher dividend payouts also inspired buying," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. Foreigners were net buyers of IDR858 billion worth of shares. Among gainers, coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) rose 2.4% to IDR3,200, coking coal producer \uc2 Borneo Lumbung\uc2  (BORN.JK) gained 6.5% to IDR1,6650 after reporting strong 2010 earnings, while heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) added 5.2% to IDR7,150 on its plan for a IDR1.5 trillion share buyback. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is edging up against the dollar in European trading hours Thursday despite peripheral concerns rising ahead of a two-day European summit due to kick-off later Thursday. The dollar is moving higher against the yen as the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to quell yen buying. Meanwhile, sterling is trading in a tight range so far in Europe ahead of retail sales data due at 0930 GMT. EUR/USD is trading up at 1.4107, GBP/USD is steady at 1.6229, USD/JPY is up at 80.940.(clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD is well off the day's lows, with a little help likely coming from a strong run of flash PMI surveys. The dominant services PMIs beat expectations pretty much across the board, while manufacturing PMIs came in solid. EUR/USD currently trades at 1.4108, from the day's 1.4054 lows. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI ends 0.1% higher at 1513.84 after lackluster trading within a tight four point band all day. A local dealer says a lack of leads and nagging concerns over Japan's nuclear issues and Middle East unrest are keeping investors on the sidelines. "Market sentiment is positive, but there's really no news to drive the market up any higher," the dealer says. He tips the benchmark to continue moving sideways on Friday, in a 1500-1520 range. Market breadth ends positive with 444 advancers aganist 299 decliners. Among gainers, AirAsia (5099.KU) ends up 5.1% at MYR2.70, Genting Malaysia (3182.KU) ends 2.0% higher at MYR3.55 and \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) ends up 0.4% at MYR6.76. Among decliners, Hai-O Enterprise (7668.KU) ends down 4.4% at MYR2.17 after weak 3Q results, and \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) ends down 0.8% at MYR6.13. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT [Dow Jones] Russia's ruble will likely stay strong in the short term, buoyed by high oil prices and expected further rate hikes at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. Still, the ruble could start weakening against the Bank of Russia's dollar-euro basket in the second half because of a declining current account surplus, uncertainty related to elections, and higher budget spending. Ruble weakens to 28.4016 against the dollar on the Micex exchange, but is little changed at 33.6269 against the basket. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP was lower late as the market awaited the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy action due after spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading ended at 0800 GMT Thursday. The pair was at 43.42 after settling at 43.47 late Wednesday; volume was relatively thin. The BSP raised both its borrowing and lending rates by 25 bps each to 4.25% and 6.25%, respectively, in line with most economists' expectations; this marks the BSP's first rate hike since July 2009. Traders say the USD/PHP may lose more ground Friday, adding that the pair could slide to 43.20 following the BSP's rate hike. They tip resistance for the pair at 43.50. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-24-11 0536ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110324e73o0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
