\par\par \b \uc2 5th UPDATE: BOJ Signals Comfort With Liquidity, On Board With Yen-Weakening Efforts\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- BOJ refrains from providing overnight funds to the banking system for the first time since the March 11 earthquake\par \par \uc2 -- Suggests BOJ feels there's enough liquidity in banking system now, on board with G-7 efforts to weaken yen\par \par \uc2 -- Current account balance set to hit record high again Wednesday\par \par \uc2 -- Overnight call rate declines, within BOJ's target range\par \par \uc2 (Updates the BOJ's latest current account balance figures in paragraph eight.)\par \par \uc2 By Hiroshi Miwa and Arran Scott\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday refrained from pumping extra overnight funds into the banking system for the first time since the devastating earthquake on March 11, and left the yen from the coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday in the market.\par \par \uc2 The move suggests the Bank of Japan feels there was sufficient liquidity in the market to meet demand for short-term funds from financial institutions. These institutions scrambled for liquidity after corporate and household demand for cash spiked following the 9.0-magnitude quake and ensuing tsunami, which caused widespread damage on Japan's northeast coast and crippled a nuclear power facility that later leaked radiation.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ's decision also indicates the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is playing its part to increase the effectiveness of the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its Group of Seven partners aimed at weakening the yen.\par \par \uc2 Market participants were watching the BOJ's money-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, when transactions from Friday's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled, to see if the BOJ left the additional yen in the market. By not "sterilizing" the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by removing the extra yen from the system, the supply of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is higher than it would have been without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , giving the G-7 action more punch.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has discretion over how to manage liquidity in the domestic money market. To sterilize Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ would have drained funds from the money market Wednesday--something it didn't do.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ "appears to have practically entered the realm of unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it denies that," said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed-income strategist Eiji Dohke.\par \par \uc2 The yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely amounted to about Y530 billion, according to BOJ data released Tuesday and market estimates of fund flows, a far smaller figure than the roughly Y2 trillion some traders had previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash since the March 11 quake to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity. It said its current-account balance--the funds that financial institutions in Japan keep at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they can use for lending or other needs--increased to its highest ever at Y41.83 trillion Wednesday, from Y41.62 trillion on Tuesday. The BOJ forecast the balance will rise to Y42.60 trillion Thursday, up sharply from Y32.70 trillion Friday.\par \par \uc2 BOJ policy board member Ryuzo Miyao said Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will do its utmost to ensure stability in the settlement system and financial markets" amid high uncertainty following the earthquake, the largest ever to hit Japan.\par \par \uc2 Given it will take a while to restore social infrastructure damaged by the disaster, "the degree of economic downside risks from the supply side is likely to be high--compared, for example, with the Hanshin Awaji earthquake 16 years ago---and it is necessary to pay careful attention to the possibility that such impact could be prolonged," Miyao told business leaders in Oita, southern Japan.\par \par \uc2 The recent fund provisions brought the BOJ's quantitative easing into uncharted territory for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The highest level the current-account balance reached before this week was Y36.36 trillion on March 31, 2004, when the BOJ was ramping up a previous quantitative easing regime to combat deflation and support a rickety banking system at the time.\par \par \uc2 Conditions in Japan's money market appeared to support the BOJ's decision to forgo injections of same-day funds Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The overnight call rate--the BOJ's policy target rate--ended trading at 0.06%, well below its Tuesday weighted-average of 0.084%, suggesting there was sufficient liquidity in the market. The BOJ's target is 0% to 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 A Tokyo-based money broker said he thinks the BOJ can't ease its accommodative stance yet since "the financial system is still so vulnerable against another unexpected blow."\par \par \uc2 The broker said he expects the BOJ to continue to add liquidity to the money market, probably at least beyond the Golden Week holiday, which starts at the end of April.\par \par \uc2 Japanese banks were closed Monday for a national holiday.\par \par \uc2 -By Hiroshi Miwa and Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; +813 6269 2800; hiroshi.miwa@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Megumi Fujikawa and Kazuhiro Shimamura contributed to this report. [ 23-03-11 1034GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110323e73n0001q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
