\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Safe-Haven Yen Gains As Radiation Concern Mounts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Monahan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen strengthened slightly against the dollar and euro in Asia Wednesday as the broadening fallout from Japan's nuclear reactor crisis weighed on share prices, increasing demand for the country's low-yielding unit, considered a safe-haven.\par \par \uc2 But dealers said sharper rises would continued to be limited by expectations that any such gains could prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to order \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar would likely hold in a relatively narrow band around Y81.00 for the rest of the global day, dealers said, as investors hesitate to make major bets amid the continued uncertainty over the situation in Japan as well as the military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya.\par \par \uc2 The Tokyo Metropolitan Government said Wednesday that radioactive iodine exceeding permissible levels for infants has been detected in tap water in Tokyo, adding to concerns over the broadening scope of contamination from the earthquake and tsunami-stricken Fukushima Daiichi reactors about 150 miles north of the capital.\par \par \uc2 "People want to see how things go with the nuclear reactors, and there is also the uncertainty with the geopolitical situation in northern Africa and the Middle East," said Shuichi Kanehira, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the yen added slightly to its gains after the news about the tainted water in Tokyo encouraged further safe-haven bids, dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should stay above Y80.00 in coming sessions.\par \par \uc2 "Perceptions about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase with any sharp falls in the dollar against the yen, particularly if we see any renewed plunge in equities," Mizuho's Kanehira said.\par \par \uc2 Motonari Ogawa, a senior dealer at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , said that speculation about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise on any falls in the dollar toward Y80.\par \par \uc2 Another reason the yen has not posted sharper gains on safe-haven flows is that investors have become somewhat inured to negative news on the reactor situation in Japan, Ogawa said.\par \par \uc2 "Like with the constant aftershocks, people are getting more used to the ongoing reactor issues, so it would take a clearer deterioration in the situation to move the market a lot," he said.\par \par \uc2 At 0450 GMT, the dollar was at Y80.90, down from Y80.95 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at Y114.70 from Y114.95 and at $1.4175 from $1.4195. The ICE Dollar Index, which measures the greenback against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 75.55 from 75.50.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                                     2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par Dollar Rates \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              80.90-91   80.91-94   -0.01  81.07   80.81    -0.39 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.4174-78  1.4196-98  -0.15  1.4202  1.4150   +6.01 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.6370-74  1.6371-76  -0.01  1.6382  1.6340   +5.00 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc    