\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Likely Mixed; Japan Markets Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 5,253 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourse, govt debt seen mixed; euro, spot gold lower, oil higher\f28 \par \par \uc2 -US Secretary Of State Clinton Says Heard Gadhafi Son May Be Dead - ABC\par \par \uc2 -German Warships Won't Take Part In Libya Embargo Drive - Defense Ministry\par \par \uc2 -Iran OPEC Governor Concerned Output Rise Leads To Glut\par \par \uc2 -Japan Nuclear Agency Says Nuclear Reactor Cooling Units Close To Coming Online\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Shirakawa: Quake Damage Creating Severe Conditions For Short-Term Production\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Gov: Want To Achieve Financial Mkt Stability By Aggressive Fund Supply To Money Mkt\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Shirakawa: Low JGB Yield Reflects Market Confidence In Japan Fiscal Discipline\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Fisher Eyes Exit From Easy-Money Stance\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Munich Re\uc2 : EUR1.5B Claim From Japan; Profit Goal Out Of Reach\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  CEO: Company Remains Cautious On M&A Activity\par \par \uc2 -Spain's Bankia Plans To Raise EUR3B From IPO -Newspaper\par \par \uc2 -Wendel, KKR: Sign New Shareholder Agreement Regarding Legrand\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0930 UK Mar Agents' Summary of Business Conditions; 0930 UK Feb BBA main high street banking groups statistics; 0930 UK Mar Bank of England - Bank of England MPC meeting minutes; 1100 US Dallas Fed Pres Fisher speaks; 1430 US Mar 18 EIA Weekly Petroleum Status Report\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mixed Wednesday, but Japanese shares succumbed to profit-taking after their post-earthquake bounce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With investors still focusing on Japan and the conflict in Libya, European stock markets are likely to start mixed or a bit lower, with government bonds also mixed. The euro and spot gold are lower, while oil futures are higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses, having to cope with euro-zone issues and the Libyan conflict, are seen starting mixed Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Wednesday's opening, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BGC Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  is calling the FTSE down 14 points at 5748, the DAX up 6 at 6786 and the CAC off 8 at 3884.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Uncertainty from the Middle East, Northern Africa and Japan weighed on sentiment," IG Markets noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday's losses came as Portugal's main opposition party signaled it won't support the government's call for additional austerity measures. Portugal is widely seen as one of the euro-zone nations that may eventually need external financial aid in order to meet its obligations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A vote on the measures is expected Wednesday, and defeat could trigger the government's resignation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Libya remains a volatile concern.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is beginning to show concerns as the international community is not managing to speak with one voice on Libya," said Frederic Aubel, a French trader at Global Equities, in comments which could equally apply to bourses around the world.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Angus Campbell, head of sales at Capital Spreads, said Tuesday's moves represented some risk reversal following the gains over the previous three days, and also reflected disappointing inflation data in the U.K., where February consumer inflation rose 4.4% on an annual basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ian Scott, a strategist at Nomura, said that even after stocks' broad gains over the past few days, the market has overestimated the impact of recent negative news events outside Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a note to clients, Scott said the recent earthquake and tsunami in Japan should have a minimal impact on Europe, because Japan accounts for only 3.6% of European exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the case of the Middle East, there's been no impact on implied volatility and the decline in oil stocks suggests an element of indiscriminate selling, he noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Positive news has largely been ignored. Record high levels of business confidence in Germany have been reflected in tighter credit spreads, but not in higher equity valuations," Scott said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are lower Wednesday, after markets faltered slightly Tuesday, ending a three-day rally during which investors showed signs of looking past recent concerns over unrest in the Middle East and Japan's earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Continued progress by Japan to control a nuclear reactor on the brink of a meltdown is being offset by festering Middle East uncertainty," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Schwab & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pause comes after three days that saw the Dow gain 3.5% to trade above 12000 for the first time since the March 11 Japan earthquake, helped by encouraging geopolitical developments and corporate mergers such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 's agreement to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We just glided into 2011, and after two months we were up 6%," said Joe Quinlan, chief market strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  U.S. Trust Private Wealth Management. After the turbulence of the past few weeks, "we've rattled some complacency out of the markets, and that's good in the sense that it's not a glide," he said. "That first 6% felt easy--almost too easy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors were cheered by the strength of the rebound that began last Thursday, and saw Tuesday's light action as a day of "consolidation" for the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market swing over the last three trading days was really surprising," said Larry Glazer, managing partner at Mayflower Advisors in Boston. "The market really needs to digest what's been happening and investors need to decide how daring they want to be."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks Wednesday took a breather after the Nikkei's 7.2% rise over the previous two sessions, but foreign investor buying limited the market's losses. Signs of gradual progress at the troubled nuclear plants in northeastern Japan--which roiled global markets last week--lifted shares off their early lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Until month-end the index will likely hover around 9,500, reflecting both optimism on the nuclear resolution and pessimism towards future corporate earnings," said Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. He added that external factors such as the ongoing unrest in Libya, as well as fluctuating crude prices, will once again dominate themes when the index recovers above the 10,000 mark to pre-quake levels.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar traded in a range against the yen as many players avoided making major bets amid continued uncertainty over the problems at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors and the coalition's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Libya.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People want to see how things go with the nuclear reactors, and there is also the uncertainty with the geopolitical situation in Northern Africa and the Middle East," said Shuichi Kanehira, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are also cautious amid the threat of more yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Perceptions about the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will increase with any sharp falls in the dollar against the yen, particularly if we see any renewed plunge in equities," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our interpretation is that the signaling effect from 'coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' did most of the job for the [Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] in the short run," said Nomura Securities analysts. "Nevertheless, we judge that the BOJ is likely to be ready for more persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , should they be needed."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was a little lower against the dollar and the yen, as it consolidated its recent gains. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to be supported by expectations of interest rate hikes from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after recent hawkish comments from ECB officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you look at the major central banks of the world, the ECB [European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] has signaled that it's about to tighten imminently," said Constantine Ponticos, managing director of investment management research in London for Pareto, which manages more than $50 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "The BOE is, I would say, in the eyes of the market losing credibility unless it signals a hike," Ponticos said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials, meanwhile, continue to hint that the euro zone could see higher rates as soon as next month, helping the euro move Tuesday above $1.42. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encountered technical resistance at those levels and fell slightly against the dollar in late trading, but was still considered to be on an upward path.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some members of the U.S. Federal Reserve, meanwhile, are signaling that they are becoming less comfortable with policy meant to prop a U.S. economy whose recovery may no longer need the stimulus of loose policy, "although they haven't signaled any imminent" moves to tighten, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prospects of higher interest rates in the euro zone and the U.K. boost the prospects of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , making them more attractive to hold than the dollar, which is hamstrung by near-zero rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts are likely to start mixed as investors weigh the need for a safe haven against prospects for rate increases by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of England.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you look at the major central banks of the world, the ECB [European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] has signaled that it's about to tighten imminently," said Constantine Ponticos, managing director of investment management research in London for Pareto. "The BOE is, I would say, in the eyes of the market losing credibility unless it signals a hike," Ponticos said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A surge in bond yields and a sharp economic downturn would weaken the creditworthiness of western European countries and have "severe social costs" in Greece, Ireland, and Portugal, with nearly one-quarter of borrowers in these countries and Spain defaulting, although western Europe as a whole could withstand the shock, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.K., "February's public finances and consumer prices numbers presented a distinctly unfavourable backdrop to tomorrow's budget," noted Capital Economics analyst Jonathan Loynes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On top of that the cost of Britain's role in Libya, even if it is less than the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan, is a fresh headache for the cash-strapped government as it tries to tackle a record deficit. [ 23-03-11 0602GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. reported inflation, as measured by the consumer price index, increased 4.4% in the 12 months to February after rising 4% in the year to January. That is a bigger rise than most economists were forecasting and well above the BOE's 2% annual target. Inflation has been more than one percentage point above the bank's target for more than a year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The case for a gradual U.K. rate increase was underscored Tuesday by Andrew Sentance, a long-time policy hawk on the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With inflation running very considerably above its target level and set to rise further, failure to take timely monetary-policy action risks a more abrupt and destabilizing rise in interest rates in the future," Sentance said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. benchmark Treasury prices are little changed Wednesday, after securities posted small losses Tuesday when progress in containing Japan's nuclear crisis lessened the demand for safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. inflation report added to concern about global inflation, fueling speculation that the Bank of England will raise interest rates in the coming months. The data spurred a selloff in the U.K. gilts market which spilled over to Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anxiety about inflation has been boosted by higher prices for basic commodities and food. Political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa has pushed crude-oil prices above $100 a barrel. U.S. consumer prices have inched up in recent months, even though price pressures are relatively contained compared with other economies. Inflation is the main threat to bonds' fixed returns over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are in a semi-normalcy. Japan and the Middle East remain a great concern, but lack of new news over the past 48 hours has resulted in a pullback in Treasurys," said John Canavan, market analyst at Stone and McCarthy Research Associates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher said in an interview Tuesday with Dow Jones Newswires that he is likely to be among the first Fed officials to press for tighter monetary policy when the time comes, warning that inflationary pressures are building.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fisher, known as one of the Fed's most strident anti-inflation hawks, signaled he is weary of the Fed's crisis programs, which have pushed the size of the Fed's balance sheet to well in excess of $2 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now, though, Fisher is among the lone voices within voting members of the Fed's policymaking committee that is warning about continued loose policy. The euro-zone and U.K., on the other hand, are likely to raise rates soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo on Wednesday, lead Japanese government bond futures were up 0.26 at 139.66 points, lifted by the Nikkei's losses, while the yield on the 10-year cash JGB was down two basis points at 1.225%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude rose in Asian trade Wednesday as unrest in Libya and the region continued to stoke supply fears, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light, sweet crude for delivery in May, gained 26 cents to $105.23 per barrel while Brent North Sea crude for May was up 51 cents to $116.21.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The issues of Libya and Yemen have been driving prices of crude oil up, although Yemen is a small exporter and Libya has been more significant," said John Vautrain, vice president for Purvin and Gertz energy consultancy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The situation is probably going to last for a while," Vautrain, based in Singapore, told AFP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The conflict in Libya continued to press markets, after forcing the shutdown of more than three-quarters of the country's oil production.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil-rich Libya was producing 1.69 million barrels a day before the unrest, according to the International Energy Agency. It is now producing 400,000 barrels a day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Yemen, President Ali Abdullah Saleh's offer to quit by January failed to appease escalating opposition to his 32-year rule, as Washington warned that Yemen's crisis could serve to boost Al-Qaeda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If crude oil gets to $110 a barrel, I think that's a yellow light, and at $120 a barrel it's really a red light where it will be bearish for stocks and have a lot of people thinking about this double dip notion we had last year," said Jerry Harris, president of asset management at Sterne Agee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME metals are mostly edging higher with Chinese bids coming in for copper and zinc, but the market could be hit if the euro weakens further ahead of Portugal's budget debate later in the global day, says a senior Hong Kong based trader. "The Portugal thing is an overhang so the euro is holding us back and we could see some selling when London opens," he says. Portuguese Prime Minister Jose Socrates could resign if an austerity vote later is rejected by opposition parties, the Wall Street Journal says. That result would trouble bond markets and put the euro under pressure, potentially hurting copper, which often tracks the euro's moves against the dollar. LME 3-month copper is at $9,516/ton, up $31 with a thin 797 lots done.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold and silver are still close to the top of their recent ranges, but haven't managed to break out to new highs, despite rallying oil prices, with regional stock markets struggling and the Japanese yen gaining early. "We expect gold to stay locked in a range for the immediate term while keeping a close look at the situation in Japan and Libya," says MKS Finance. Silver did manage a 31-year high New York close at $36.38/oz, but has slipped back slightly this morning to trade down 9 cents at $36.29/oz. Spot gold was at $1,426.60 per troy ounce, down $2.20 from its New York settlement Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 23-03-11 0602GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12018.63  -17.90  -0.15% \par Nasdaq     2683.87   -8.22  -0.31% \par S&P 500    1293.77   -4.61  -0.36% \par FTSE 100   5762.71  -23.38  -0.40% \par Xetra DAX  6780.97  -35.15  -0.52% \par CAC40      3892.71  -11.74  -0.30% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9533.92  -74.40 -0.77% \par Hang Seng    22819.83 -38.07 -0.17% \par S&P/ASX 200   4653.50 +10.06 +0.22% \par Taiwan Index  8545.61 +37.57 +0.44% \par S.Korea Kospi 2011.13 -2.53  -0.13% \par Shanghai Comp 2942.66 +23.52 +0.81% \par Bombay SenSex 18168.76 +180.46 +1% \par Dow Future    11935.00 -20.00 -0.2% \par NASDAQ Future 2251.75  -5.50  -0.2% \par S&P Future    1286.20  -2.10  -0.2% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      80.90-91      -0.01% \par Range        81.07   80.81 \par EUR/USD      1.4174-78    -0.15% \par Range        1.4202  1.4150 \par AUD/USD      1.0098-00    -0.06% \par Range        1.0113  1.0062 \par GBP/USD      1.6370-74    -0.01% \par Range        1.6382  1.6340 \par USD/CHF      0.9033-35     0.00% \par Range        0.9044  0.9027 \par EUR/GBP      0.8658-60    -0.12% \par Range        0.8674  0.8650 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2807-09    -0.14% \par Range        1.2832  1.2787 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 11.04%/11.72% \par Day earlier: 12.09%/12.77% \par EUR/U