\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Pound Falters As BOE Signals Wait-And-See Stance\b0\par\par\uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Pound weakens as investors trim UK rate expectations\par \par \uc2 --UK Budget speech awaited\par \par \uc2 --Euro treads water amid worries on Portugal, Ireland\par \par \uc2 --Middle East tensions, Japan helping safe haven Swiss franc\par \par \uc2 By Siva Sithraputhran Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LONDON(Dow Jones)--The pound lost its footing above $1.63 against the dollar Wednesday after the minutes of the Bank of England's meeting earlier this month disappointed market expectations by failing to ramp up the aggressive tone on rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tone was a little more tentative, as it signalled a wait-and-see approach given the uncertainties for economic growth and the forces driving inflation. That led some investors to scale back expectations that the BOE will lift interest rates soon, and in turn weighed on the pound.\par \par \uc2 News of spiralling inflation, with the key annual inflation rate hitting its highest level since October 2008 in data released Tuesday, had triggered fresh support for the pound, taking it close to $1.64 against the dollar\par \par \uc2 'The problem is that the Monetary Policy Committee is driven not by inflation in itself, but by what the inflation means,' said Daragh Maher, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 This would mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on the watch for knock-on effects from increases in headline inflation, particularly wages, rather than just responding to rising price trends.\par \par \uc2 That said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned that the headline annual inflation rate may well top 5% in the coming months. It tempered that assessment by saying that it will learn more about the forces driving inflation over the coming months and that it remains too early to gauge the strength of economic recovery in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 At 1134 GMT, the pound traded at $1.6281 against the dollar from the $1.6325 area immediately before the minutes were released. Sterling had already fallen heavily in earlier trading. The euro rose to GBP0.8724 from GBP0.8676.\par \par \uc2 The next big event of the day for sterling is the U.K. finance minister's budget speech due from 1230 GMT. This will outline the government's spending, and cutting, plans for the year ahead. The set-piece event is often a big factor for the pound, but this time around, most elements have been flagged in advance.\par \par \uc2 The government's forecasts for public finances may have an impact, though. 'Smaller deficits would be a short-term pound positive,' said \uc2 Adam Cole\uc2 , an analyst at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Any confirmation that the government is set to sell the stakes it bought in banks at the height of the financial crisis would likely support sterling, as it would signal that the UK financial services industry was in recovery mode, Cole added.\par \par \uc2 The euro was stuck in a range against the dollar, as it took in stride concerns that Portugal may soon have to ask for European Union help to shore up its finances. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been well supported this week after the progress achieved in setting up a permanent mechanism to help fiscally-challenged member states. But the rest of this week could be critical for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 'Ahead of the upcoming EU Summit on Thursday and Friday we see little chance of a euro rally. Germany's position has hardened, as Chancellor Merkel is put under pressure by her own law makers,' said \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Also today, Portugal's budget vote at 1800 GMT may have some implications for the euro.\par \par \uc2 If Portugal fails to agree on austerity measures, this will force the country into asking for a emergency bailout from the EU's emergency funds, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analysts said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the Portuguese vote, the euro was under pressure, reflecting stresses in the Irish government debt market.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the yen was stuck in a narrow range after last week's concerted G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The nuclear crisis in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still not resolved and continues to be monitored closely by markets worldwide.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    That, and the continued tensions in the Middle East as coalition forces get tough on Libya are keeping safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  well supported. The Swiss franc, in particular, notched up hefty gains. \par    The euro was at $1.4155 from $1.4195 late Tuesday in New York, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.91 from Y81.01, while the euro was at Y114.57 from Y114.95. The U.K. pound was at $1.6285 from $1.6378. The dollar was at CHF 0.9015 from CHF0.9046 . \par   The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 75.644 from 75.496. \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/USD    USD/JPY    GBP/USD    USD/CHF \par Spot 1135 GMT     1.4158     80.91      1.6281     0.9018 \par 3 Day Trend       Bullish    Bearish    Bullish    Bearish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish    Range      Bullish    Bearish \par 200 day ma        1.3496     84.22      1.5807     0.9832 \par 3rd Resistance    1.4283     81.76      1.6458     0.9090 \par 2nd Resistance    1.4249     81.32      1.6401     0.9076 \par 1st Resistance    1.4215     81.07      1.6380     0.9032 \par Pivot*            1.4208     81.04      1.6358     0.9035 \par 1st Support       1.4149     80.70      1.6283     0.8978 \par 2nd Support       1.4080     80.52      1.6250     0.8958 \par 3rd Support       1.4050     80.00      1.6203     0.8852 \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/GBP \par Spot 1135 GMT     0.8694 \par 3 Day Trend       Bearish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish \par 200 day ma        0.8537 \par 3rd Resistance    0.8759 \par 2nd Resistance    0.8744 \par 1st Resistance    0.8729 \par Pivot*            0.8688 \par 1st Support       0.8655 \par 2nd Support       0.8630 \par 3rd Support       0.8592 \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Siva Sithraputhran, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0)7842 9462; siva.sithraputhran@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copyright (c) 2011 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110324e73n00dnr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
