\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Equities Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par FED REJECTS BANK OF AMERICA DIVIDEND, CAPITAL PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve rejects a plan submitted by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  in the latest round of Fed stress tests that would have 'modestly' increased its dividend this year. The bank says it intends to resubmit a comprehensive plan in the summer. Shares dip more than 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ALLIES STRAIN TO MEND SPLIT OVER LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A coalition of military powers pound the Gadhafi regime's military installations for a fifth day and solidified their control over Libya's skies -- even as they continued to struggle to resolve an internal conflict over how to lead the campaign.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS FALL ON MIDDLE EAST VIOLENCE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks drop as violence continues in the Middle East, where a bus in Israel was hit by a massive explosion, and North Africa, and amid fears of a collapse of Portugal's government mount.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERNATIONAL MARKETS POWER \f28 \uc2 \f2 GENERAL MILLS\f28 \uc2 \f2  PROFIT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Mills\f28 \uc2 \f2  fiscal 3Q earnings rise 18% of $392.1 million, or 59c a share, as the maker of cereals, Progresso soups and Hamburger Helper says that profit soared at its international business. Shares fall 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COLGATE TO BUY UNILEVER UNIT FOR $940M \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 World's largest toothpaste maker agrees to buy the Sanex personal-care brand from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $940 million, or EUR672 million, strengthening its personal-care business in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SIGNALS COMFORT WITH CURRENT LIQUIDITY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  refrains from pumping extra overnight funds into the banking system for the first time since the devastating earthquake on March 11, and left the yen from the coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday in the market.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAKES PROGRESS ON NUCLEAR PLANT CONTROLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vital control and cooling systems are expected to come back online in the next few days at some of the most damaged reactors of the quake-hit Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex, Japan's nuclear safety agency says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ACTRESS ELIZABETH TAYLOR DIES AT 79 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hollywood actress Elizabeth Taylor, famed for her striking beauty and a film career that spanned five decades, has died in a hospital in Los Angeles, according to a statement by her publicist. She was 79 years old.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SYRIAN PROTESTS PRESSURE ASSAD REGIME \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Protests in the south of Syria continue for the fifth consecutive day, posing an unprecedented challenge to the regime of President Bashar al-Assad, The Wall Street Journal reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AIG'S BENMOSCHE SAYS BID FOR MBS IS 'FAIR' \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 American International Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's bid of $15.7 billion to the Federal Reserve for a portfolio of mortgage-backed securities is "fair," and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in effect is conducting an auction for the bonds, says CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Robert Benmosche\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US BANKS BLAMED FOR CREDIT UNION ILLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal regulators are blaming Wall Street's biggest firms for the collapse of five institutions at the heart of the nation's credit-union industry and are seeking to recoup tens of billions of dollars in losses on securities that doomed the five.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par   ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  \par THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par Japan Ignored Warning Of Nuclear Vulnerability \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese regulators discussed in recent months the use of new cooling technologies at nuclear plants that could have lessened or prevented the disaster at the Fukushima Daiichi power facility, The Wall Street Journal reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HEARD ON THE STREET \par Singapore Exchange Should Look At "Plan B" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With his proposed $8.3 billion tieup with the Australian exchange facing increasingly stiff political opposition the Singapore Exchange's chief executive, Magnus Bocker, should probably look more closely at his "plan B," Sam Holmes writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   ============ U.S. MARKETS ACTION =========== \par DJIA              down 24.41 points to 11993.16 \par NASDAQ            down 15.19 points to 2668.46 \par S&P 500           down  6.48 points to 1287.29 \par 10-year T-note    102 26/32 at 3.2893 yield \par NYMEX Crude       up $0.54 at $105.48/bbl \par Dollar/Euro       down 0.0076 at 1.4108 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-23-11 1000ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5030 55101   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110323e73n00082\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
