\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Equities Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 BANK OF AMERICA\f28 \uc2 \f2  NOT ALLOWED TO RAISE DIVIDEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that after completing the latest round of government stress testing, the Federal Reserve won't allow the giant bank to boost its dividend but will let it submit a revised capital plan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ALLIES STRAIN TO MEND SPLIT OVER LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A coalition of military powers pound the Gadhafi regime's military installations for a fifth day and solidified their control over Libya's skies -- even as they continued to struggle to resolve an internal conflict over how to lead the campaign.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES TURN LOWER; OIL TOPS $105 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock futures move lower as oil prices rise to more than $105 a barrel, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adobe Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2  in focus after the software group reports a jump in profit, but cuts its revenue outlook following the Japanese earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERNATIONAL MARKETS POWER \f28 \uc2 \f2 GENERAL MILLS\f28 \uc2 \f2  PROFIT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Mills\f28 \uc2 \f2  fiscal 3Q earnings rise 18% of $392.1 million, or 59c a share, as the maker of cereals, Progresso soups and Hamburger Helper says that profit soared at its international business. Shares are down 2% pre-market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par COLGATE TO BUY UNILEVER UNIT FOR $940M \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 World's largest toothpaste maker agrees to buy the Sanex personal-care brand from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $940 million, or EUR672 million, strengthening its personal-care business in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SIGNALS COMFORT WITH CURRENT LIQUIDITY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  refrains from pumping extra overnight funds into the banking system for the first time since the devastating earthquake on March 11, and left the yen from the coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday in the market.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAKES PROGRESS ON NUCLEAR PLANT CONTROLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vital control and cooling systems are expected to come back online in the next few days at some of the most damaged reactors of the quake-hit Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex, Japan's nuclear safety agency says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TELECOM MERGER SETS OFF DEBATE IN WASHINGTON \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 's proposed $39 billion acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  hits a lot of hot buttons in Washington: worries about U.S. competitiveness, the Obama administration's push to expand broadband Internet access and the desire of elected officials to show voters they are fighting to limit monthly phone bills.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US BANKS BLAMED FOR CREDIT UNION ILLS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal regulators are blaming Wall Street's biggest firms for the collapse of five institutions at the heart of the nation's credit-union industry and are seeking to recoup tens of billions of dollars in losses on securities that doomed the five.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S FISHER WOULD VOTE AGAINST FURTHER EASING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\f28 \uc2 \f2  President Richard Fisher says the Fed has provided "plenty of fuel" to the private sector to ramp up business and hiring, and he wouldn't support any move by the bank to continue quantitative easing measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 4 GOP OFFICIALS OBJECT TO MORTGAGE CUTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Four Republican state attorneys general say they oppose forcing banks to reduce the value of loans for troubled homeowners as part of a state-led mortgage-servicing settlement proposal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par   ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  \par THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par Japan Ignored Warning Of Nuclear Vulnerability \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese regulators discussed in recent months the use of new cooling technologies at nuclear plants that could have lessened or prevented the disaster at the Fukushima Daiichi power facility, The Wall Street Journal reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HEARD ON THE STREET \par Singapore Exchange Should Look At "Plan B" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With his proposed $8.3 billion tieup with the Australian exchange facing increasingly stiff political opposition the Singapore Exchange's chief executive, Magnus Bocker, should probably look more closely at his "plan B," Sam Holmes writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   ============ U.S. MARKETS ACTION =========== \par DJIA              12018.63 from Tuesday \par NASDAQ             2683.87 from Tuesday \par S&P 500            1293.77 from Tuesday \par 10-year T-note    102 27/32 at 3.2866 yield \par NYMEX Crude       up $0.27 at $105.26/bbl \par Dollar/Euro       down 0.0049 at 1.4134 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-23-11 0900ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5030 55101   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110323e73n0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
