\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: ING Less Confident Of Singapore Tightening In April\b0\par\par\uc2 1,686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] ING Bank maintains its forecast that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore will tighten policy at its twice-yearly meeting in April, but says it is less confident in that forecast following the Japan earthquake. February inflation data is due at 0500 GMT and the consensus forecast is for the CPI to rise 5.4% on-year, at roughly the same pace as a month ago. The widely-held view that the MAS will tighten has helped boost the SGD to record highs, but some analysts, including ING, are now hedging those predictions, in light of the possible dampening of global economic growth due to Japan's quake. The USD/SGD is at 1.2650 vs 1.2635 late Tuesday. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are higher amid strong demand from cash-rich banks and insurers, as they need the low-risk debt to meet their asset allocation purposes, says a Shenzhen-based trader with a local broker. "The market has not been affected much by the latest RRR hike," he adds. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 127.57, up from Tuesday's close of 127.54, while the interbank seven-day weighted average repo falls 12 bps to 2.72%. The trader tips the bond index in a 127.55-127.65 range. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are likely to fall, tracking Wall Street's decline and losses in most Asian markets (Japan's Nikkei, Hong Kong's HSI and South Korea's Kospi are all down), traders say. They expect the main index to trade in a 3500-3550 range after it ended flat at 3517.721 Tuesday. "I think the market may continue to consolidate as sentiment remains cautious amid ongoing concerns over high oil prices and rising inflationary pressures," says a trader with a foreign securities firm. Heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may lead broader market losses after its ADRs fell 1.0% in New York, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may fall on profit taking after Tuesday's 2.0% gain. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is down 0.4% at 22,756.33, snapping its three-session 2.6% winning streak; KGI's chief operating offer Ben Kwong says after accumulating more than 700 points in its rebound from its year-to-date low of 22,123 (March 17), the HSI is witnessing some profit-taking pressure today. "Investors are taking a step back and consider lingering problems in Japan and across the Arab world." Concerns on Libya's crisis lead to higher oil prices, Kwong says; April crude futures settled up 1.6% at US$104/bbl on Nymex Tuesday. After weak results, China Coal (1898.HK) is down 7.3% at HK$10.96 and China Life (2628.HK) is down 2.9% at HK$28.35. Henderson Land (0012.HK) is up 0.5% at HK$48.95 on an exchange disclosure that shows its chairman Lee Shau Kee bought 5.952 million shares on March 18. Kwong tips the index in a 22,600-23,000 range near term. Volume is modest at HK$5.95 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY rises on a higher central parity, set at 6.5601 from Tuesday's 6.5592, following a rebound by the USD index overnight. In OTC trade, the USD/CNY is at 6.5601 from late Tuesday's record-low of 6.5561; a Shanghai-based local bank trader tips the pair in a 6.5570-6.5610 range for the session. "I think the PBOC could let the yuan rise again ahead of the G-20 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seminar in Nanjing at the end of the month, as it usually does when the international spotlight is on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.4350/6.4370 from 6.4290/6.4330 late Tuesday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is a tad higher on fund outflows but thin volume suggests that selling interest is not strong; the pair is at 29.588 vs 29.542 in late Asian trade Tuesday. A trader from a local bank says the pair is tracking the sluggish Taiex, which is flat; he expects the pair to stay in a narrow 29.55-29.60 range for the rest of the day. Exporters aren't willing to sell aggressively, as the pair was rather steady over the past month, he adds. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is higher at 8,725 vs 8,710 in late Asian trade Tuesday; players are selling the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , taking profit after its recent gains, traders say. "But dollar-selling by several foreign banks has put a brake on the dollar's rally," says a trader with a local bank. She expects the pair to trade in a 8,710-8,730 range for the rest of the session. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones]The USD/JPY will likely continue to trade in a relatively narrow band around 81.00 as uncertainty over the problems at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors and the coalition's military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya discourage market participants from making major bets, says Shuichi Kanehira, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 ; "People want to see how things go with the nuclear reactors, and there is also the uncertainty with the geopolitical situation in northern Africa and the Middle East." Kanehira adds that investors are also cautious amid the threat of more JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Perceptions about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase with any sharp falls in the dollar against the yen, particularly if we see any renewed plunge in equities," he says. The USD/JPY is at 80.95, while Japan's benchmark Nikkei Stock Average is down 1.3%.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects CNY-settled cross-border trade will continue to grow rapidly, and will comprise 15%-20% of China's total trade volume in three to five years. Last year, 2.5% of China's trade was settled in CNY, according to the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "China's trade with Asean will grow much faster than the OECD. I expect a lot of the trade flows will be re-denominated in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Neil Daswani, Standard Chartered transaction banking managing director, tells reporters at a briefing. Together with more non-trade related outflows of CNY, he estimates offshore CNY deposits in Hong Kong will mushroom to CNY4.5 trillion by the end of 2015 from CNY370.6 billion at end-January. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open flat with gains in oil companies offsetting declines in banking stocks. The Shanghai Composite Index is flat at 2919.02 and analysts tip consolidation around 2900. "The recent slow trading pace indicates investors remain cautious amid strong inflationary pressures, and the spike in oil prices is also a major concern," says Capital Securities Jacky Zhang; noting that the impact of Japan's nuclear risks on the A-share market is fading. Oil firms rise following an increase in oil prices; \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is up 0.3% at CNY11.80 while Sinopec (600028.SH) adds 0.2% to CNY8.59. Banks fall on modest profit- taking after Tuesday's gains; Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) sheds 0.4% to CNY2.65 and \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) eases 0.3% to CNY13.81. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.1% lower at 1284.16. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.2% at 3007.61, extending Tuesday's gains which saw the index reclaim the psychological 3000 mark, and brushing off the weak lead from Wall Street. Commodities plays Olam (O32.SG) and Golden Agri (E5H.SG) are supporting the index, +3.4% at S$2.74 and +2.3% at S$0.980, respectively. Phillip Securities says the STI's marginal close above 3000 Tuesday "is somewhat positive." However, while the short term has turned bullish, it says "the intermediate term still has a downside bias" with 2965 as strong support. "We ideally want to see price dip, put in a swing low above 2965 and continue trading higher from there. In such a case we might be looking at the beginning of an intermediate term uptrend." The house tips resistance at 3010, 3020, then 3045/50. Support is at 2988/2980, then 2965. Market volume stands at 173 million shares worth S$200 million, with gainers and fallers evenly matched. HPH Trust (NS8U.SG) is most active, flat at US$0.970. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan's move to flood the banking system with cash via same-day market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at maximizing the effect of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed-income strategist Eiji Dohke. With the BOJ's current account balance at a record Y41.6 trillion and the MOF still left with over Y30 trillion for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the March fiscal year-end, the BOJ "appears to have practically stepped into unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it denies that," Dohke says. In a sign that the BOJ sees enough liquidity in the market, it skipped the money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  today for the first time since the March 11 earthquake. The overnight call rate is tradi