\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Tipped Around 81.00 Amid Unease-Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones]The USD/JPY will likely continue to trade in a relatively narrow band around 81.00 as uncertainty over the problems at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors and the coalition's military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Libya discourage market participants from making major bets, says Shuichi Kanehira, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; "People want to see how things go with the nuclear reactors, and there is also the uncertainty with the geopolitical situation in northern Africa and the Middle East." Kanehira adds that investors are also cautious amid the threat of more JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Perceptions about the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will increase with any sharp falls in the dollar against the yen, particularly if we see any renewed plunge in equities," he says. The USD/JPY is at 80.95, while Japan's benchmark Nikkei Stock Average is down 1.3%.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0148 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered expects CNY-settled cross-border trade will continue to grow rapidly, and will comprise 15%-20% of China's total trade volume in three to five years. Last year, 2.5% of China's trade was settled in CNY, according to the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "China's trade with Asean will grow much faster than the OECD. I expect a lot of the trade flows will be re-denominated in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Neil Daswani, Standard Chartered transaction banking managing director, tells reporters at a briefing. Together with more non-trade related outflows of CNY, he estimates offshore CNY deposits in Hong Kong will mushroom to CNY4.5 trillion by the end of 2015 from CNY370.6 billion at end-January. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open flat with gains in oil companies offsetting declines in banking stocks. The Shanghai Composite Index is flat at 2919.02 and analysts tip consolidation around 2900. "The recent slow trading pace indicates investors remain cautious amid strong inflationary pressures, and the spike in oil prices is also a major concern," says Capital Securities Jacky Zhang; noting that the impact of Japan's nuclear risks on the A-share market is fading. Oil firms rise following an increase in oil prices; \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is up 0.3% at CNY11.80 while Sinopec (600028.SH) adds 0.2% to CNY8.59. Banks fall on modest profit- taking after Tuesday's gains; Agricultural Bank of China (601288.SH) sheds 0.4% to CNY2.65 and \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) eases 0.3% to CNY13.81. The Shenzhen Composite Index is 0.1% lower at 1284.16. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.2% at 3007.61, extending Tuesday's gains which saw the index reclaim the psychological 3000 mark, and brushing off the weak lead from Wall Street. Commodities plays Olam (O32.SG) and Golden Agri (E5H.SG) are supporting the index, +3.4% at S$2.74 and +2.3% at S$0.980, respectively. Phillip Securities says the STI's marginal close above 3000 Tuesday "is somewhat positive." However, while the short term has turned bullish, it says "the intermediate term still has a downside bias" with 2965 as strong support. "We ideally want to see price dip, put in a swing low above 2965 and continue trading higher from there. In such a case we might be looking at the beginning of an intermediate term uptrend." The house tips resistance at 3010, 3020, then 3045/50. Support is at 2988/2980, then 2965. Market volume stands at 173 million shares worth S$200 million, with gainers and fallers evenly matched. HPH Trust (NS8U.SG) is most active, flat at US$0.970. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan's move to flood the banking system with cash via same-day market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at maximizing the effect of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed-income strategist Eiji Dohke. With the BOJ's current account balance at a record Y41.6 trillion and the MOF still left with over Y30 trillion for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the March fiscal year-end, the BOJ "appears to have practically stepped into unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it denies that," Dohke says. In a sign that the BOJ sees enough liquidity in the market, it skipped the money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  today for the first time since the March 11 earthquake. The overnight call rate is trading at 0.07%-0.08% this morning, down a touch from Tuesday's weighted average of 0.084%, with nobody seen trying to bid it higher, suggesting the BOJ's efforts to keeping its target rate stable are paying off. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are likely to fall in tandem with weakness in regional bourses. "The (Thai) bourse lacks any fresh leads to drive the market, while eased concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis are already priced in," says an analyst with Far East Securities, Parin Kitchatornpitak. Investors are keeping a watch on military action against Libya and political tensions in other countries in the Middle East region. He tips support at 1015 and resistance at 1025. The SET index ends 0.1% lower at 1019.14 Tuesday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is off 0.2% at 1505.95, dragged by selling in select blue chips by institutional funds, says a dealer with a local brokerage. "The 1500 support should hold as the selling is mild," says the dealer; he notes that the market breadth is mixed with 82 gainers, 77 losers and 110 unchanged. "Given the slow newsflow, the index will probably continue to swing sideways," says another dealer, tipping immediate resistance at 1513, the 61.8% Fibonnaci Retracement of 1474 to 1576. She notes that Sarawak state stocks remain in focus ahead of the state polls (expected next month); Suria (6521.KU) is +1.5% at MYR2.08, Zecon (7028.KU) is up 4.7% at MYR0.485 and Cahya Mata (2852.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR2.72. Among losers, Genting (3182.KU) is down 0.9% at MYR10.30, \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is down 0.8% at MYR9.05 and Maybank (1155.KU) is down 0.2% at MYR8.70. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are biased slightly lower after Tuesday's losses in Wall Street and a dearth of local market-moving news. "We will continue to see the market drift sideways. There's no (local) news to trade on," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. The benchmark PSEi may slide to 3820 support after closing 0.3% higher Tuesday at 3854.14, with resistance pegged at 3880. Major blue chips such as Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), PLDT (TEL.PH) and geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) are likely to follow the broader market cue. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is steady at 30.22-30.24 vs 30.23 in late Asian trade Tuesday but the pair is biased up as losses in most regional stock markets support the greenback. Still, "the upside is likely to be limited" as exporters are likely to continue selling, says a dealer at a local bank. She tips resistance at 30.25. Traders think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in (buy USD) to prevent the pair from falling below 30.20.(oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is down, tracking offshore USD weakness. The pair is at 7.7953 from 7.7980 late Tuesday. "The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supported by the recent rise in local stocks and expectations of yuan appreciation," says a Singapore bank trader. He expects the pair will edge down further if local stocks continue to perform well. He tips the pair in a 7.7930-7.7980 range. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0047 GMT incorrectly stated when the USD/SGD touched its record lows. The correct version follows:) The USD/SGD is rebounding slightly in early Asia trade after touching record lows late Tuesday. The pair was trading at 1.2654 after being quoted as low as 1.2620 Tuesday. "I think we'll see some consolidation in dollar-Asia today," says a trader at a foreign bank, who tips USD/SGD resistance at 1.2680. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were buoyed by the coordinated G-7 yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, which lent "massive support to risk appetite," the trader adds. But that may cool off a bit as the market returns its focus to developments in Europe with respect to talks over the European Union bailout facility. The trader tips support for USD/SGD around yesterday's lows. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds open slightly higher, but are likely to remain little changed with few fresh cues driving the market, a trader at an international bank says. Yield on the two-year Singapore government bond is down 1 bp at 0.38%, and the 10-year is down 1 bp at 2.41%. "Nothing much has changed overnight, and the (U.S.) Treasurys ended the day with small losses after continued progress in containing Japan's nuclear crisis lessened the demand for safe assets," the trader says, tipping yields to stay flat across the curve. On Tuesday, bond yields rose a tad across the curve, both two-year and 10-year yields added 1 bp. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 2154ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110323e73n00019\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
