\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: BOJ Ops Aimed At Supporting  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  - Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan's move to flood the banking system with cash via same-day market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at maximizing the effect of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed-income strategist Eiji Dohke. With the BOJ's current account balance at a record Y41.6 trillion and the MOF still left with over Y30 trillion for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the March fiscal year-end, the BOJ "appears to have practically stepped into unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if it denies that," Dohke says. In a sign that the BOJ sees enough liquidity in the market, it skipped the money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  today for the first time since the March 11 earthquake. The overnight call rate is trading at 0.07%-0.08% this morning, down a touch from Tuesday's weighted average of 0.084%, with nobody seen trying to bid it higher, suggesting the BOJ's efforts to keeping its target rate stable are paying off. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are likely to fall in tandem with weakness in regional bourses. "The (Thai) bourse lacks any fresh leads to drive the market, while eased concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis are already priced in," says an analyst with Far East Securities, Parin Kitchatornpitak. Investors are keeping a watch on military action against Libya and political tensions in other countries in the Middle East region. He tips support at 1015 and resistance at 1025. The SET index ends 0.1% lower at 1019.14 Tuesday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is off 0.2% at 1505.95, dragged by selling in select blue chips by institutional funds, says a dealer with a local brokerage. "The 1500 support should hold as the selling is mild," says the dealer; he notes that the market breadth is mixed with 82 gainers, 77 losers and 110 unchanged. "Given the slow newsflow, the index will probably continue to swing sideways," says another dealer, tipping immediate resistance at 1513, the 61.8% Fibonnaci Retracement of 1474 to 1576. She notes that Sarawak state stocks remain in focus ahead of the state polls (expected next month); Suria (6521.KU) is +1.5% at MYR2.08, Zecon (7028.KU) is up 4.7% at MYR0.485 and Cahya Mata (2852.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR2.72. Among losers, Genting (3182.KU) is down 0.9% at MYR10.30, \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is down 0.8% at MYR9.05 and Maybank (1155.KU) is down 0.2% at MYR8.70. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are biased slightly lower after Tuesday's losses in Wall Street and a dearth of local market-moving news. "We will continue to see the market drift sideways. There's no (local) news to trade on," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. The benchmark PSEi may slide to 3820 support after closing 0.3% higher Tuesday at 3854.14, with resistance pegged at 3880. Major blue chips such as Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), PLDT (TEL.PH) and geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) are likely to follow the broader market cue. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is steady at 30.22-30.24 vs 30.23 in late Asian trade Tuesday but the pair is biased up as losses in most regional stock markets support the greenback. Still, "the upside is likely to be limited" as exporters are likely to continue selling, says a dealer at a local bank. She tips resistance at 30.25. Traders think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in (buy USD) to prevent the pair from falling below 30.20.(oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is down, tracking offshore USD weakness. The pair is at 7.7953 from 7.7980 late Tuesday. "The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supported by the recent rise in local stocks and expectations of yuan appreciation," says a Singapore bank trader. He expects the pair will edge down further if local stocks continue to perform well. He tips the pair in a 7.7930-7.7980 range. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0047 GMT incorrectly stated when the USD/SGD touched its record lows. The correct version follows:) The USD/SGD is rebounding slightly in early Asia trade after touching record lows late Tuesday. The pair was trading at 1.2654 after being quoted as low as 1.2620 Tuesday. "I think we'll see some consolidation in dollar-Asia today," says a trader at a foreign bank, who tips USD/SGD resistance at 1.2680. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were buoyed by the coordinated G-7 yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, which lent "massive support to risk appetite," the trader adds. But that may cool off a bit as the market returns its focus to developments in Europe with respect to talks over the European Union bailout facility. The trader tips support for USD/SGD around yesterday's lows. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds open slightly higher, but are likely to remain little changed with few fresh cues driving the market, a trader at an international bank says. Yield on the two-year Singapore government bond is down 1 bp at 0.38%, and the 10-year is down 1 bp at 2.41%. "Nothing much has changed overnight, and the (U.S.) Treasurys ended the day with small losses after continued progress in containing Japan's nuclear crisis lessened the demand for safe assets," the trader says, tipping yields to stay flat across the curve. On Tuesday, bond yields rose a tad across the curve, both two-year and 10-year yields added 1 bp. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD has risen into its previous range and is likely to encounter resistance up to 1.02, says Greg Gibbs, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at RBS. But while it is back on track against the USD, it is developing a downward trend against European and some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , consistent with a narrowing interest rate differential, he adds. The AUD's upside may lag as policy tightening in Europe and Asia continues and as Japan economic disruption and Middle East concerns build. But he adds, the recent correction in the AUD has cleared out some longs, while the RBA will be hiking rates more than markets have priced in later this year, and global commodity prices remain buoyant. Overall, the outlook is mixed, he says. The AUD/USD is at 1.0067, steady with its level in late Sydney trade Tuesday. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is up at 1,124.80 from 1,120.90 late Tuesday in Seoul tracking the EUR/USD fall and rising oil prices, participants say; a weaker Kospi (down 0.2%) is also supporting the pair's rise, they add. "The pair fell in the last few sessions, reducing its volatility, and now it's time to take a breather," says a local bank trader, who tips a 1,120-1,127 band for the session. Traders don't expect sharp gains from current levels due to anticipated USD selling by exporters. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] A formal downgrade of New Zealand's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit rating is a "reasonably high probability event," says First NZ Capital economist Chris Green. He points to the underlying trend decline in the current account position, weak near-term activity profile, and a sharp deterioration in the government's fiscal position on the back of the Canterbury reconstruction costs. Green says New Zealand's current account deficit will likely move into a modest surplus over the March 2011 quarter but will slip back into deficit "driven by a rise in the investment income deficit as foreign-owned NZ companies earn higher profits on the back of a modest improvement in activity over the second half of this year, together with the higher cost of financing NZ's large net foreign liability position." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI may fall following Wall Street's decline overnight, with high oil prices hurting sentiment amid ongoing unrest in the Middle East and North Africa, while Japan nuclear crisis remains resolved, says Mark To, head of research at Wing Fung Financial. The market's outlook is unclear "due to the geopolitical tensions in the Middle East and the follow-up development in Japanese disaster." He expects the HSI to fall to 22,600 today. China Life (2628.HK) and China Coal (1898.HK) may fall after their results came in below market expectations, while Henderson Land (0012.HK) may rise on an exchange disclosure that shows its chairman Lee Shau Kee bought 5.952 million shares on March 18. The index ended up 0.8% at 22,857.90 Tuesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 2138ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110323e73n00015\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
