\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Warns Of Further  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Weaken Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Hiroshi Miwa  \par \uc2 OF THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese authorities pumped cash into the banking system up to record levels and warned of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen in their latest efforts to ease financial strains that could hamper recovery from natural disaster.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, officials in China and Australia said the earthquake and tsunami would have a short-term impact on trade and their economies but reconstruction efforts would later make up for some of the losses.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com).\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average jumped 4.4% Tuesday as fears receded further about the threat of a widening crisis at damaged nuclear power plants near the stricken areas.\par \par \uc2 In Asian trading Tuesday, the yen remained largely unchanged at around 81.10 to the dollar amid the possibility that authorities could jump into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets once more after a rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven industrialized nations Friday. The G-7's move pushed the yen to its current level around 81, well below its recent high of Y76.25 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan data and market estimates of fund flows indicated Friday's yen sales by Japan and its G-7 partners amounted to about Y530 billion ($6.55 billion), much less than the Y2 trillion some traders originally estimated.\par \par \uc2 While traders wondered whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited to last Friday, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Tuesday noted that the G-7 statement said the group would monitor exchange rates and "cooperate as appropriate," adding: "That tells you everything."\par \par \uc2 A Japanese government bureaucrat familiar with the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy added more explicitly that no decision was made to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to one day.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen harms large Japanese exporters by making their goods more expensive aboard, cutting into sales and profits.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's cash injections into money markets Tuesday pushed its current account balance--the funds that financial institutions in Japan keep at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which they can use for lending or other needs--to a record high of Y41.72 trillion, or $514.42 billion. The flood of liquidity pushed down overnight interest rates near to zero.\par \par \uc2 In Beijing, China commerce ministry spokesman Yao Jian said Tuesday Japan's exports to China of certain electronics, chemical and steel products as well as spare parts will decline as many of the manufacturers of these goods are located in the northeastern areas of Japan most affected by the earthquake and tsunami. The region's shipping and port \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also damaged, he said at a regular news conference.\par \par \uc2 China's exports of garments and agricultural products to Japan will also fall in the near term, Yao said. Japan was China's third-largest trade partner, its largest source of imports and the fifth-largest destination of its exports as of the end of last year, he said.\par \par \uc2 Australia's economy would likely take a hit from the events in the Japan, \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  board member Graham Kraehe said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "It will slow us down for six months probably," he said. "Long term, it shouldn't be major."\par \par \uc2 Japan is Australia's second largest export market behind China and economists estimate the disaster there could shave around 0.2 percentage points from Australia's growth in the second quarter. Hardest hit will be steel related exports such as coal, iron ore and natural gas.\par \par \uc2 But Kraehe, who is also chairman of steel maker \uc2 Bluescope Steel Ltd\uc2 . (BSLAU), said Australia should recover once the rebuilding process begins and demand for materials ranging from natural gas to steel picks up.\par \par \uc2 -Yajun Zhang and Enda Curran contributed to this article. [ 03-22-11 0838ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110322e73m0007v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
