\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Lower As Markets Pause\b0\par\par\uc2 4,218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen lower, govt debt mixed; euro lower, with spot gold up, oil futures mixed\f28 \par \par \uc2 -US Says Goal Is To Get Gadhafi To Step Down\par \par \uc2 -Allies Spar but Renew Airstrikes on Libya\par \par \uc2 -Generals Revolt in Yemen\par \par \uc2 -Syrian Protesters Defy Crackdown\par \par \uc2 -Mixed Signals At Japan's Fukushima Nuclear Plant\par \par \uc2 -Tokyo Shares Up As Nuclear Fears Subside, Banks Rise\par \par \uc2 -German Fin Min: ESM Deal 'Reasonable, Sustainable'\par \par \uc2 -Ireland Insists On Keeping Corporate Tax As Is -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  Iron Ore CEO: Expects 'Some Strengthening' In Iron Ore Prices This Year\par \par \uc2 -Glencore Senior Management Courts US Investors: Sources - Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  Gets $600M Offshore Platform Order From BP\par \par \uc2 -AMP Launches A$600 Million Note Issue To Fund AXA APH Acquisition\par \par \uc2 -Heathrow's Owner BAA Outsources IT to \uc2 Capgemini\par \par \uc2 -UK Media Alliance Urges Govt To Block \uc2 News Corp\uc2 /Sky Deal\par \par \uc2 -UK Lawmakers Vote To Support Libya Military Action\par \par \uc2 -UK Budget: Tax Avoidance Measures To Raise GBP1 Billion - Source\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Telekom Austria\uc2  Bids Up To EUR950M For \uc2 Telekom Srbija\uc2  Stake\par \par \uc2 -Belgium Prices EUR5 bln 2017 3.5% Bond\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  Grabs German Cable Operator KBW for EUR3.2 billion\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0930 UK Feb Public sector finances; 0930 UK Feb UK monthly inflation figures; 1100 UK Mar CBI Monthly Industrial Trends Survey; 1130 GER Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas - Dallas Fed President; 1200 US Cleveland Fed Pres Pianalto; 1400 US Jan U.S. Monthly House Price Index\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mostly higher Tuesday, led by a surge in Japanese stocks on signs of progress in containing the country's nuclear crisis, but markets remained watchful of fresh developments in Libya.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and its allies worked to expand the protective shield in the skies over Libya, while skirmishing broke out over who would take command of the continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Work to restore power to reactors at the quake-hit Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power station resumed on Tuesday morning, despite signs of smoke rising from reactor No. 2 and 3. Japan's nuclear safety agency said Tuesday that it detected higher-than-permitted levels of radioactive materials in seawater near the plant, raising the possibility that contamination from the power station could spread to marine life.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European markets are likely to see a lower opening, with government bonds mixed. The euro is slightly lower while spot gold is higher and oil futures mixed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses are seen starting a bit lower Tuesday, consolidating recent gains as investors take a breather to assess strategies based on developments in Libya and Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Tuesday's opening, the FTSE is seen down 10 points at 5776, the DAX off 27 at 6789, and the CAC off 4 at 3900, according to David Buik of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BGC Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Justin Urquhart Stewart, co-founder of Seven Investment Management, took a cautious stance on the recent rally. "Any of the risks you thought were there on Friday, are still there on Monday," he said. "The Japanese issues may have become slightly less immediately dangerous."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tensions in Libya and the Middle East remained a concern, as the U.S. and its allies launched air strikes against Libya. "This is another episode that could possibly be translated into risk-off trades and market nervousness," Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is noteworthy that the U.N.'s reaction in support of the Libyan antigovernment protesters could have wider implications. Similar antigovernment sentiments could be possibly expressed more openly now, especially at bigger oil-producing states in the region, with significant implications on oil prices, inflation and possibly global growth," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are a bit lower Tuesday, after the Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed back above 12000, boosted by a major telecommunications deal and a jump in energy stocks as oil prices leapt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the second-largest U.S. mobile carrier, added 1.1% after saying it was buying \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $39 billion in cash and stock, a move that would create the nation's largest wireless carrier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It never hurts to have merger news and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  merger announcement with T-Mobile is definitely something that's got people feeling that business is getting done," said William Vogel, vice president at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merlin Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , noting \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's aid in providing $20 billion in financing--the bank's biggest loan ever. "It's clear that banks are playing a big role," he said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are narrowly mixed Tuesday as traders play it safe with more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  talk in the wind.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials on Tuesday maintained the threat of further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, saying joint action is not limited to last week's coordinated market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unfolding disaster, "the dollar's appeal as a refuge of safety was not as pre-eminent as it normally is," said Andrew Wilkinson, senior market analyst at Interactive Brokers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As some of these geopolitical concerns subside, the focus on interest-rate differentials is going to be a euro-positive story," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Washington, D.C. "Central banks are expected to raise interest rates before the Fed, and that's why the dollar is going to remain under pressure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Signs of nervousness over the euro's outlook may be emerging in the form of options positions. Even as the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has rallied, investors were snatching up bearish "put" options, or bets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will decline, according to a trader. That could signal escalating nerves ahead of this week's key European Union summit, or possibly a feeling the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent rally has gone too far. "People are thinking one day we'll drop heavily," the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling, now little changed, is likely to consolidate ahead of Wednesday's U.K. Budget. Finance Minister Osborne will set out his commitment to the austerity plan, which has shaved growth and could add to already high unemployment in months ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed as investors pause after a busy day in the markets on Monday, with stocks now looking a bit fragile while euro-zone issues play out and the U.K. braces for inflation data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The June bund contract was under pressure Monday as stronger equity markets and weaker U.S. Treasurys combined to drag German bund futures 46 ticks lower to 122.17. A confirmed break of 122.19 would open the way to a fall to 121.70 according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the agreement struck Monday by the euro zone's 17 finance ministers is "a reasonable, sustainable solution" to the debt problems of its members, German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many market participants still fear that Ireland could walk away from the deal it negotiated with its creditors last year, unable to service what appears to be an unsustainable debt load.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Schaeuble acknowledged that there are still "concerns" over the sustainability of Ireland's banks, which are only staying afloat thanks to total liquidity assistance of nearly EUR200 billion from the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Irish government insisted late Monday that it would not capitulate to pressure to increase the corporate tax rate and that a reduction in the interest rate on bailout loans remains its objective, The Irish Times reported Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.K., the latest inflation figures will help investors divine when the first interest-rate increase since the financial crisis could come from the Bank of England.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne will use Wednesday's budget to announce a series of measures to combat tax avoidance, aimed at raising around GBP1 billion in extra revenue each year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The crackdown on tax avoidance will address cases of disguised remuneration--where pay is rooted through trusts or other vehicles to avoid tax, a person familiar with the matter told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are mixed Tuesday, after prices dropped Monday for a third straight session as investors took heart from some encouraging pieces of news on the Japan nuclear crisis, and after the U.S. Treasury surprised the market with plans to start selling mortgage-backed securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The situation is slowly getting better" in Japan, said Adam Brown, managing director of U.S. government bond trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York, though developments in the country remain a primary focus for the market. "Right now, this market is being driven by geopolitical events and first and foremost is Japan."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo Tuesday, lead Japanese government bond futures were down 0.25 at 139.49 points on the strength in the Tokyo stock market and weakness in U.S. Treasurys Monday. The 10-year cash JGB yield was recently up 3.5 basis points at 1.240%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil was mixed early in the Asian session, with April Nymex crude oil futures up seven cents at $102.40 per barrel on Globex while the May contract was up 19 cents at $103.28 per barrel. Brent North Sea crude for May delivery edged down 15 cents to $114.81.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Currently, we still have tensions in the Middle East underpinning oil prices. Investors are expecting that Libyan oil is not returning to the markets any time soon," said Ong Yi Ling, investment analyst for Phillip Futures in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil-rich Libya was producing 1.69 million barrels a day before the unrest, according to the International Energy Agency. It is now producing 400,000 barrels a day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi's Tripoli compound was rocked by blasts late Monday, his southern strongholds targeted and a navy base bombed as the US-European coalition sought to pin down Gadhafi's military and protect Libya's civilians and opposition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by French, American and British forces in Libya has hobbled loyalist forces but offers little hint of how the situation will be resolved. [ 22-03-11 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A stalemate at current positions leaves most of the oil export ports in the hands of the Gadhafi regime," noted PetroMatrix analyst Olivier Jakob in Zurich.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange metals are mostly higher but the market is lacking momentum, says a Singapore-based trader. However, the trader says dip buyers are about after the market fell Monday on poor U.S. housing data. "It's been a dull start to the week (compared to last week), but we are actually seeing some good flow this morning." LME three-month copper is at $9,475/ton, up $80 with 964 lots done. LME three-month aluminum is at $2,572/ton, up $2 with 167 lots done. The unresolved situation at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power station remains key to sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold is up $2.60 at $1,429.70/oz, taking its directional cues from crude prices and the U.S. dollar. Gluskin Sheff says a key driver remains the fact that real interest rates in the U.S. are negative with commodity price inflation starting to exacerbate the situation. "Gold will remain a safe haven and will benefit from a further move by real short-term U.S. interest rates into negative terrain," says the bank's chief economist David Rosenberg. Gold is also supported by reports that Middle Eastern countries, including Iran and Jordan, are buying gold to diversify away from the U.S. dollar. The technical bias remains bullish, with resistance at $1,435 on a closing basis, and then the March 2 record high of $1,445.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 22-03-11 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12036.53  +178.01  +1.50% \par Nasdaq     2692.09   +48.42  +1.83% \par S&P 500    1298.38   +19.18  +1.50% \par FTSE 100   5786.09   +67.96  +1.19% \par Xetra DAX  6816.12  +151.72  +2.28% \par CAC40      3904.45  +94.23   +2.47% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9570.37  +363.62 +3.95% \par Hang Seng    22737.88 + 52.66 +0.23% \par S&P/ASX 200   4638.30  -4.51  -0.10% \par Taiwan Index  8535.52 + 67.81 +0.80% \par S.Korea Kospi 2013.08 +  9.66 +0.48% \par Shanghai Comp 2913.52  + 4.39 +0.15% \par Bombay SenSex 18015.54 +176.49 +0.99 \par Dow Future    11943.00 -20.00  -0.2% \par NASDAQ Future 2251.25  -3.25   -0.1% \par S&P Future    1290.40  -2.70   -0.2% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      80.96-98      -0.05% \par Range        81.30   80.88 \par EUR/USD      1.4215-17    -0.07% \par Range        1.4231  1.4201 \par AUD/USD      1.0056-59    -0.06% \par Range        1.0067  1.0035 \par GBP/USD      1.6294-97    -0.08% \par Range        1.6316  1.6290 \par USD/CHF      0.9043-46     0.00% \par Range        0.9058  0.9034 \par EUR/GBP      0.8721-27     0.00% \par Range        0.8725  0.8709 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2852-55    -0.09% \par Range        1.2880  1.2846 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract  12.09%/12.77% \par Day earlier: 12.86%/13.54% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract  9.84%/10.46% \par Day earlier: 9.99%/10.61% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Contract   10.7%/11.2% \par Day earlier: 11.0%/11.5% \par GBP/USD Vol Option Contract  8.45%/8.85