\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Shares down, oil up amid Japan, Middle East worries\b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 17:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, March 23 (Reuters) - Asian shares fell on Wednesday, with Tokyo's Nikkei shedding nearly 2 percent, and oil ticked up as investors fretted about the possible repercussions of a nuclear crisis in Japan, violence in Libya and unrest in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 The euro hovered below a 4-1/2-month high as worries about debt problems in Portugal and Ireland halted its recent gains, while the yen hugged a tight range around 81 to the dollar amid caution about further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei fell 1.7 percent as investors took profits from a two-session bounce. Japanese stocks remain around 8 percent below their close on March 11, the day northeast Japan was struck by a 9.0 magnitude earthquake and 10-metre tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The disaster left more than 21,000 people dead or missing, crippled a nuclear power plant that has been leaking radiation and hit production at major companies such as \uc2 Sony\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "It's not that the market is ignoring fundamentals," said Takashi Hiroki, chief strategist at \uc2 Monex Inc\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We just simply don't know them yet, as many companies are still gathering information about damage sustained during the disasters."\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan\par \par \uc2 fell 0.2 percent, mirroring falls on Wall Street, where the Dow Jones industrial average lost 0.2 percent and the S&P 500 fell 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The euro traded around $1.4165 , off a four-month high near $1.4250 reached on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The yen remained little moved over the past 24 hours, with traders wary the Bank of Japan might step in below 80.50 after Friday's rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G7 central banks -- the first such move since 2000 -- to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 Oil crept higher, with U.S. crude up 0.1 percent at $105.08 a barrel and Brent crude gaining 0.4 percent to $116.17. Unrest in the Middle East and Libya has been pushing oil prices higher on concerns about supply disruptions. (Writing by Alex Richarson; Editing by Sugita Katyal)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110325e73m0017v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
