\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Stark: Japan woes haven't changed ECB inflation view\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Reasons to hike ECB rates unchanged by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  disaster\par \par \uc2 * Further G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at call of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * ECB Q3 money market support depends on government moves\par \par \uc2 (adds quotes, detail, background)\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, March 22 (Reuters) - The euro zone's near-term inflation outlook has not been changed by the disasters in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and G7 members would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in yen \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided it was necessary, a top ECB policymaker was quoted as saying on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ECB board member Juergen Stark, who heads the bank's influential economics unit, said the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disasters in Japan had increased economic uncertainty in recent weeks, as had increased tensions in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, he bolstered the view that the ECB will follow through with its plans to hike interest rates next month.\par \par \uc2 "For me, the situation in the euro area, with the ongoing economic growth and ongoing threat to price stability, in the short term has not changed," Stark said in an interview with Japan's Nikkei newspaper carried out on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "However, the uncertainty has increased, in particular due to the events in Japan and due to the geopolitical situation in other regions."\par \par \uc2 Stark also said G7 members were willing to repeat last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided it was necessary.\par \par \uc2 "This (further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is up to the Japanese authorities to consider this possibility," he said. "The G7 finance ministers and central banks expressed their readiness to provide any needed cooperation."\par \par \uc2 Stark, one of the ECB's six-strong board, gave further hints that he saw the need for the ECB to raise rates -- frozen at a record low of 1 percent since May 2009 -- next month to prevent a wage-price spiral.\par \par \uc2 "There is a risk of second-round effects. And it is the task of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent these effects from materialising."\par \par \uc2 "The monetary policy around the globe in advanced economies and in emerging economies is very accommodative," he said. "So what is considered by the ECB's Governing Council is also a step into (the) direction of normalising the monetary policy stance."\par \par \uc2 Stark added in the interview, a transcript of which was provided by the ECB, that the bank would stop buying government bonds as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 The ECB introduced the controversial tactic at the beginning of the euro zone debt crisis last May. Last week it bought no bonds for the third week in a row but it has so far failed to convince euro zone leaders to allow the region's fiscal bailout fund to take over the role.\par \par \uc2 "The Securities Market Programme (SMP) is a temporary measure, but still in place in order to ensure the functioning of the transmission mechanism for the monetary policy in some regions of the euro area," Stark said.\par \par \uc2 "It is a temporary measure and it will be phased out as quickly as possible."\par \par \uc2 Stark also touched on the ECB's money market support measures, saying its ability to phase out the aid depended on decisions made by governments this week on the euro zone's future debt rules and bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 "Banks are less dependent to our \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and this is a clear sign of normalisation in the interbank market. We have phased out already many measures. Some measures are still needed."\par \par \uc2 "It will also depend on the measures taken by governments and the decisions by the Heads of State or Government this week," Stark said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Marc Jones; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 ECB/STARK (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110325e73m000n5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
