\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Close Slightly Lower, Ending Three-Day Rally\b0\par\par\uc2 3,264 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12018.63  -17.90  -0.15% \par Nasdaq     2683.87   -8.22  -0.31% \par S&P 500    1293.77   -4.61  -0.36% \par FTSE 100   5762.71  -23.38  -0.40% \par Xetra DAX  6780.97  -35.15  -0.52% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 30/32 -1/32 0.653% +1.6BP \par 5Y Tsy  100 13/32 -2/32 2.035% +1.5BP \par 10Y Tsy 102 16/32 -1/32 3.326% +0.5BP \par  \par USD/JPY       80.91-94     -0.11% \par Range         81.30-80.82 \par EUR/USD       1.4194-96    -0.22% \par Range         1.4249-1.4172 \par AUD/USD       1.0102-05    +0.40% \par Range         1.0128-1.0035 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   10.80/11.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    9.85/10.15% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   10.75/11.15% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,427.60    +$1.20 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,419.50-$1,432.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $104.00    +$1.67 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks faltered slightly Tuesday, ending a three-day rally. The small decline, which came on lower-than-average volumes, saw stocks move in a narrow band of just 48 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished down 17.90 points, or 0.15%, at 12018.63, while the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index fell 4.61 points, or 0.36%, to 1293.77, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 8.22 points, or 0.31%, to 2683.87. Leading the declines were industrial and consumer-discretionary stocks.\par \par \uc2 Walgreen\uc2  fell $2.76, or 6.6%, to $39.21 a share to lead the S&P 500's decliners after the drugstore operator's second-quarter earnings fell short of some analysts' expectations, with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  saying the quarter would be viewed as "a setback" after two strong quarters.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were also a concern for some investors, as crude-oil futures rose to $104 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 The pause comes after three days that saw the Dow gain 3.5% to trade above 12000 for the first time since the March 11 Japan earthquake, helped by encouraging geopolitical developments and corporate mergers such as \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's agreement to buy \uc2 T-Mobile USA\uc2  from \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We just glided into 2011, and after two months we were up 6%," said Joe Quinlan, chief market strategist for \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  U.S. Trust Private Wealth Management. After the turbulence of the past few weeks, "we've rattled some complacency out of the markets, and that's good in the sense that it's not a glide," he said. "That first 6% felt easy--almost too easy."\par \par \uc2 Investors were cheered by the strength of the rebound that began last Thursday, and saw Tuesday's light action as a day of "consolidation" for the market.\par \par \uc2 Some were concerned about rising oil prices, with crude pushing higher. "If crude oil gets to $110 a barrel, I think that's a yellow light, and at $120 a barrel it's really a red light where it will be bearish for stocks and have a lot of people thinking about this double dip notion we had last year," said Jerry Harris, president of asset management at Sterne Agee.\par \par \uc2 Volumes were light, with just 3.7 billion shares changing hands in New York Stock Exchange composite volume, well below the daily average this year of about 4.5 billion shares.\par \par \uc2 The biggest decliners among Dow components were \uc2 General Electric\uc2  and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , off 1.2% each, while \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  continued to benefit from the prospect of consolidation in the telecommunications industry. Verizon was the strongest performer among the Dow components, gaining 49 cents, or 1.3%, to 36.95, while \uc2 AT&T\uc2  slipped 16 cents, or 0.6%, to 28.10.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks closed sharply higher Tuesday, as hopes for reconstruction in the wake of Japan's devastating earthquake and tsunami overcame receding concerns over its troubled nuclear reactors.\par \par \uc2 While the Nikkei may recover to the 10,000-mark as early as this week, various risk factors--including higher oil prices--will cap its recovery beyond that level, said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. "There are medium-term risks, including deterioration in corporate earnings and the impact on the economy due to the planned rolling blackouts," he added.\par \par \uc2 Toshiyuki Kanayama, a market analyst at Monex, noted that futures surpassed their March 14 opening price (the first business day after the earthquake) and said they will likely maintain their upward momentum.\par \par \uc2 But he cautioned that as markets continue to be very sensitive to new information on the nuclear situation, any negative developments can easily influence their direction.\par \par \uc2 He pegged resistance for the June contract at 9,800 for this week.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly higher for the third consecutive session Tuesday, led by banks on hopes of strong full-year results and gold miners on rising gold prices.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the index to continue to hover around 2900 this week, as strong inflationary pressures driven by high global oil prices will temper any gains from hopes of strong results from blue-chip banks.\par \par \uc2 "The market is likely to stay in a tight 2850-2950 band as inflation concerns will cap upside," said Zhou Lin, an analyst from Huatai Securities.\par \par \uc2 "If high global oil prices, caused by unabated political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa, lead to hike in domestic oil product prices, that would add to (already-strong) inflationary pressures, just like adding fuel to the flames," said \uc2 Guodu Securities\uc2  analyst Zhang Xiang.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher Tuesday, tracking Wall Street's gains overnight, as bargain hunting more than offset worries over geopolitical tensions in North Africa, with oil producers leading the rise.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the city's benchmark index will likely face resistance at 23,000 in the near term as Tuesday's gains could easily be wiped out if uncertainties about the nuclear crisis in Japan resurface. They expect the blue-chip index to trade between 22,200 to 23,000 over the next few sessions.\par \par \uc2 "The market's focus is shifting back to corporate earnings as worries over Japan's nuclear crisis continue to recede. But in the absence of catalysts, there is limited upside for the benchmark index," said Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oil futures rose to their highest level since the March 11 Japanese earthquake in U.S. trading Tuesday, as fighting in Libya and tensions in the Middle East renewed worries about supplies.\par \par \uc2 Gold and silver futures rose as a bounce in oil prices sent investors into the metals as an inflation hedge.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mostly higher on the London Metal Exchange Tuesday after keeping to recent ranges amid fairly benign trading.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. pound hit its strongest level against the dollar Tuesday since January 2010 after rising inflation in that country led investors to bet that the Bank of England would soon be forced to increase key rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro also pushed farther above the psychologically key $1.42 level to its highest since November, as tough-on-inflation comments from the region's central bankers also hinted at an impending rate rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both left the dollar sidelined, as the easy-money Federal Reserve is increasingly seen as likely to lag other major central banks that are mapping their way to normalizing monetary policy, while the Fed continues down the road of ultralow rates and large-scale asset purchases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you look at the major central banks of the world, the ECB [European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] has signaled that it's about to tighten imminently," said Constantine Ponticos, managing director of investment management research in London for Pareto, which manages more than $50 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "The BOE is, I would say, in the eyes of the market losing credibility unless it signals a hike," Ponticos said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some members of the Fed, meanwhile, are signaling that they are becoming less comfortable with policy meant to prop a U.S. economy whose recovery may no longer need the stimulus of loose policy, "although they haven't signaled any imminent" moves to tighten, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prospects of higher interest rates in the euro zone and the U.K. boost the prospects of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , making them more attractive to hold than the dollar, which is hamstrung by near-zero rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dollar Index traded near a 15-month low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher in an interview Tuesday with Dow Jones Newswires said he is likely to be among the first Fed officials to press for tighter monetary policy when the time comes, warning that inflationary pressures are building.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fisher, known as one of the Fed's most strident anti-inflation hawks, signaled he is weary of the Fed's crisis programs, which have pushed the size of the Fed's balance sheet to well in excess of $2 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now, though, Fisher is among the lone voices within voting members of the Fed's policymaking committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s consumer price index increased 4.4% in the 12 months to February after rising 4% in the year to January. That is a bigger rise than most economists were forecasting and well above the BOE's 2% annual target. Inflation has been more than one percentage point above the bank's target for more than a year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The case for a gradual U.K. rate increase was underscored Tuesday by Andrew Sentance, a long-time policy hawk on the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With inflation running very considerably above its target level and set to rise further, failure to take timely monetary-policy action risks a more abrupt and destabilizing rise in interest rates in the future," Sentance said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. pound gained nearly 0.5% on the dollar on rate-increase expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials, meanwhile, continue to hint that the euro zone could see higher rates as soon as next month, helping the euro move above $1.42; the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encountered technical resistance at those levels and fell slightly against the dollar by late afternoon trading, but was still considered to be on an upward path. [ 22-03-11 2214GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar stayed near the Y81 level as traders remained reluctant to provoke more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets from a Group of Seven nations-led coalition of central banks. With the estimated size of last Friday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's rise being well below the size of last September's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , more efforts can't and shouldn't be ruled out, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our interpretation is that the signaling effect from 'coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' did most of the job for the [Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] in the short run," said Nomura Securities analysts. "Nevertheless, we judge that the BOJ is likely to be ready for more-persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , should they be needed."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.4195 from $1.4226 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.95 from Y81.01, while the euro was at Y114.95 from Y115.25. The U.K. pound was at $1.6378 from $1.6311. The dollar was at CHF0.9036 from CHF0.9046.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 75.496 from 75.401.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. benchmark Treasury securities posted small losses Tuesday as progress in containing Japan's nuclear crisis lessened the demand for safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong report from the U.K. on its consumer price index added to concern about global inflation, fueling speculation that the Bank of England will raise interest rates in the coming months. The data spurred a selloff in the U.K. gilts market which spilled over to Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anxiety about inflation has been boosted by higher prices for basic commodities and food. Political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa has pushed crude-oil prices above $100 a barrel. U.S. consumer prices have inched up in recent months, even though price pressures are relatively contained compared with other economies. Inflation is the main threat to bonds' fixed returns over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are in a semi-normalcy. Japan and the Middle East remain a great concern, but lack of new news over the past 48 hours has resulted in a pullback in Treasurys," said John Canavan, market analyst at Stone and McCarthy Research Associates in Skillman, N.J.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Tuesday afternoon trade in New York, the benchmark 10-year note was 1/32 lower in price to yield 3.326%. Bond prices and yields move inversely to each other. The two-year note was 1/32 lower to yield 0.653%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the fourth straight decline for benchmark Treasury bonds, but the bond market recouped most losses in the afternoon session as some investors bought safe-haven assets at cheaper levels. The buying reflected continued uncertainty about the aftermath of Japan's earthquake and tsunami, and the broad geopolitical risks in the Middle East and North Africa.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries about sovereign-debt problems in the euro zone also supported demand for Treasurys, traders said. Yields on government bonds in Ireland and Portugal continued to surge Tuesday ahead of the a key vote by Portugal's parliament on spending cuts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Andrew Brenner, head of emerging markets at Guggenheim Securities, said that if the budget isn't passed, Portugal could be forced to ask for a bailout from the European Union, just as Greece and Ireland had. Despite a recent EU agreement to help assuage the euro-zone debt crisis, bond market participants fear it may flare again at any time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended lower Tuesday as investors took their cues from higher Tokyo shares and a weaker yen compared with last week to lock in profits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns about the economic health of the nation will likely keep JGBs well-supported in the near term in the absence of any government funding plan for post-quake reconstruction, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since market activities were dominated by position unwinding of leveraged investors and worries about a possible financial crisis-like shock in the first week after the quake, trading hasn't yet fully priced in negative effects of damage on the economy or expectations for monetary policies," RBS Securities Chief Strategist Akito Fukunaga said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Increased funds in the money market also benefit JGBs, analysts said. Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake and counter a yen rise that threatens the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts also agree that JGBs aren't likely to regain a sharp upward momentum once the current round of selling runs its course.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mitsubishi UFJ \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief strategist Jun Ishii said downside support for the 10-year issue's yield seen last week around 1.2% is a good indication of how expected inflation and risk premiums could make JGB trading complicated despite the cloudy economic outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expected inflation may start to edge up due to shortages of various resources and supplies for the quake damage restoration, and concerns about the higher fiscal burden. That, along with a possible increase in new JGB issues, could lift risk premiums, Ishii said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Tuesday amid increasing expectations that the PBOC has no plans to raise interest rates immediately, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left the yield on one-year bills sold in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unchanged from the week before, a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prefers quantitative measures, such as selling bills in open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and raising reserve requirement ratios, rather than interest rate hikes in containing inflation at the current stage," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is likely to extend gains near term as liquidity in the money market remains in excess despite the latest RRR hike.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Wednesday, Taiwan's industrial output likely grew 17.98% in February from a year earlier, slightly faster than January's 17.19% increase, according to the median forecast of nine economists in a Dow Jones survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Singapore likely continued to rise briskly in February, after increasing at their fastest pace in more than two years in January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consumer price index probably rose 5.5% in February compared with a year earlier, the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. January's inflation rate was the highest since December 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Compared with the previous month, the February index likely rose 0.4% in seasonally adjusted terms, slower than the preceding month's 1.3% increase, according to the median forecast of five economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in Malaysia likely accelerated slightly in February from the previous month due to higher food and transport costs as well as rising global commodity prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consumer price index likely increased 2.6% in February compared with the year-earlier month, according to the median forecast of a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 11 economists. In January, the index rose 2.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Jet Crashes In Libya As Air Strikes Slow \par Gadhafi Makes Public Appearance At Tripoli Residence -TV \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Warns Of Further Yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par 