\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Japan Makes Further Progress On Nuclear Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THE NEWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a sign of further progress at the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, authorities said that external power has been reconnected to all six reactors at the nuclear power plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 An official at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . (9501.TO), known as Tepco, said the next challenge is to check whether cooling systems including pumps and other equipment at each reactor will work properly. He said lighting at central control rooms of the No. 3 and No. 4 reactors will be back again soon.\par \par \uc2 He said the utility has already confirmed the pumps inside the No. 5 and No. 6 reactors can go ahead with cooling process there.\par \par \uc2 The spraying missions by fire departments from Tokyo and Osaka have helped in lowering temperatures in the spent fuel pools to keep them from catching on fire.\par \par \uc2 The Japan Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency said it detected higher-than-permitted levels of radioactive materials in seawater near the complex, raising the possibility that contamination from the plant could spread to marine life.\par \par \uc2 NISA said that sampling conducted in waters about 330 meters south of the plant showed the amount of iodine-131 exceeded the allowable limit by more than 29.8 times, compared with 126.7 times on Monday afternoon\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MARKET REACTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The progress in the recovery work has helped to stabilize nervous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. The dollar is at Y80.98 at 1152 GMT compared with Y81.01 late Monday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks surged on reconstruction hopes following the devastation wrought by the earthquake and tsunami, with the Nikkei 225 stock average closing up 4.4% at 9608.32.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CASUALTIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's national police agency said the number of people killed by the earthquake and tsunami as of 0300 GMT Tuesday was 9,079, with 12,645 missing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event and related news can be found by searching the code R/JA.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LATEST STORIES: \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: TROUBLED JAPANESE NUCLEAR PLANT RECONNECTED TO GRID \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (9501.TO) said Tuesday that external power has been reconnected to all of six reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, in modest gains by Japanese authorities to contain a crisis at a heavily damaged facilities that has become a matter of international concern. (published 1230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: ADB KURODA: YEN SPIKE WAS NOT DRIVEN BY ECONOMIC FACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's spike following the earthquake and tsunami in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wasn't driven by economic factors, and recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by major economies is likely to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from rising again, Asian Development Bank President Haruhiko Kuroda said Tuesday. (published 1109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN NUCLEAR AGENCY: FOUND RADIOACTIVE MATERIAL IN SEAWATER NEAR DAIICHI PLANT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency said Tuesday it has again detected radioactive materials in seawater near an outlet at the quake-damaged Fukushima nuclear power plant. (published 0704 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: BOJ FLOODS BANKING SYSTEM WITH RECORD LIQUIDITY TO SUPPORT ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake and counter a yen rise that threatens the economy. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FOOD IMPORTS MAY RISE ON QUAKE, NUCLEAR CONTAMINATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan may import more processed foods and finished products in the second quarter as the country grapples with the devastating earthquake and tsunami and a nuclear crisis that has raised fears of food contamination within the country. (published 0854 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN MAINTAINS THREAT OF FURTHER G-7 ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials on Tuesday maintained the threat of further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, saying joint action is not limited to last week's coordinated market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0210 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SHELL MADE 8 EXTRA SHIPMENTS LNG TO JAPAN LAST WEEK -EXECUTIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Royal Dutch Shell PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RDSA) made eight extra shipments of liquefied natural gas to Japan last week, a company executive said Tuesday. (published 1056 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECONOMY MINISTER: NUCLEAR ENERGY KEY TO JAPANESE ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy minister said Tuesday that nuclear power would remain the primary source of energy for the Japanese economy despite concerns over safety after problems at the quake-hit Fukushima Daiichi power plant. (published 0056 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par QUAKE COULD SPARK BIG CHANGES IN DPJ MANIFESTO - NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan is stepping up moves to drastically change his ruling Democratic Party of Japan's manifesto, or a set of pledges, in a bid to secure funding for reconstruction work after the devastating March 11 earthquake and tsunami, the Nikkei reported in its Tuesday morning edition. (published 0421 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN RELEASES MORE OIL FROM STOCKPILES TO MEET URGENT DEMAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry said Tuesday it has approved the release of a further 22 days worth of oil reserves in an effort to boost shipments to the quake- and tsunami-stricken northern region. (published 0520 GMT) [ 22-03-11 1249GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110322e73m0011p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
