\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par BOJ DATA SUGGESTS Y530B \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its Group of Seven partners likely amounted to about Y530 billion, according to Bank of Japan data and market estimates of fund flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ALLIES SPAR AS LIBYA AIR STRIKES RAGE ON \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and its allies are working to expand the protective shield in the skies over Libya, while political skirmishing broke out over who would take command of the continuing international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NUCLEAR PLANT EFFORTS FOCUS ON RESTORING POWER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Workers at Japan's stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear complex continue efforts to restore power to its most troubled reactors, while authorities say they'll probe higher-than-permitted levels of radioactivity in nearby seawater.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER ON TAIL OF MONDAY GAINS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures were pointing to a slightly higher start for Wall Street, but also indicated some consolidation as well after the prior session's lofty gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK INFLATION RISES TO 4.4% IN FEBRUARY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer prices rose 4.4% in the 12 months to February, the highest annual rise in more than two years and above analysts expectations polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB STARK: MAY "NORMALIZE" MONETARY POLICY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The outlook for euro zone inflation has "not changed" since the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's last meeting when it said an April rate rise was "possible," ECB Executive Board Member Juergen Stark says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC LIKELY TO HIKE INTEREST RATES, RESERVE REQUIREMENT Q2 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely raise benchmark lending and deposit rates by 25 basis points each in the second quarter, with the hike coming as early as April, a survey of 10 economists by Dow Jones Newswires shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PORTUGAL OPPOSITION CHIEF SAYS EARLY ELECTION INEVITABLE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pedro Passos Coelho, the leader of Portugal's biggest opposition party, says early elections are "inevitable" and that he isn't worried that he might lose them, Jornal de Negocios reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRISH BANK REGULATION HEAD: STRESS TESTS TO BE RIGOROUS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Head of Financial Regulation at the Irish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says that looming stress tests on Irish lenders will be "rigorous' to help lift deep market scepticism surrounding the Irish banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRANCE SAYS G20 CAN'T DEAL WITH NEW BANKING CRISIS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Well-balanced regulation of the banking sector is essential to prevent the repeat of a 2008-style financial meltdown, which public finances of G-20 nations couldn't take, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Focus \par WATCHING THE DOLLAR FALL BEHIND THE CURVE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Events in Japan and the Middle East have dominated markets over the past few weeks. But as these immediate fears begin to subside, global inflation concerns are returning and the dollar has been found wanting, Nicholas Hastings writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  DIGS IN FOR A FIGHT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 's deal to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $39 billion is as much a wager on the political environment in Washington as it is a bet on the future of the wireless market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par US BANKS OPPOSE TIGHTER MONEY RULES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even as governments freeze assets tied to regimes in Libya, Egypt and Tunisia, U.S. banks are resisting efforts to tighten international rules to prevent the flow of money from corrupt politicians.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par PROMISE ON US TAXES SPARKS GOP RIFT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two decades after U.S. President George H.W. Bush abandoned his "read my lips" promise, some Republicans are chafing at their party's stand against new taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US TREASURYS DRIFT LOWER IN LONDON WITH BUNDS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasurys are lower after Treasury Departments planned MBS sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER ON TAIL OF MONDAY GAINS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures look to edge up after yesterday's lofty gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STERLING HIGHER AFTER INFLATION DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling gains against the dollar, and euro after inflation data raises rate increase expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE FUTURES STABLE, LIBYA CONCERNS REMAIN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude futures are stable on reports that Japan is making progress, concerns on Libya continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPOT GOLD FLAT, INVESTORS REMAIN WARY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold flat as crude futures stabilize; investors eye developments in Japan, Middle East. [ 22-03-11 1200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110322e73m000z4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
