\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Up As Japan Exporters Buy, G-7 Threat Limits Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("WORLD \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Yen Up As Japan Exporters Buy, G-7 Threat Limits Gains," at 0541 GMT, misstated the expected size of the current account in the 5th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Monahan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Buying by Japanese exporters pushed the yen up slightly against the dollar and the euro in Asia Tuesday, though gains were limited as short-term investors refrained from getting onboard amid concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Tuesday that the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations will continue to cooperate after pledging joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the yen last week. A person familiar with international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy told Dow Jones Newswires that Friday's G-7 pledge should not be considered limited only to that day.\par \par \uc2 "It's now a lot harder for speculators to buy the yen, which means for the time being those kinds of speculative moves have likely been suppressed," says Hideaki Inoue, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking.\par \par \uc2 "I think the trend should be toward a weaker yen for now given the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the moves by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease further, which make it harder for speculators to buy the yen," said Inoue.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped a record amount of cash into the banking system Tuesday to counter the yen's strength and ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with the aftermath of a massive earthquake that struck the northeast of the country on March 11. The BOJ's current-account balance will rise to Y41.72 trillion Tuesday as a result of its liquidity injections, the highest since March 31, 2004.\par \par \uc2 For these reasons, traders said that the dollar will likely remain above Y80.00 in coming sessions, while possibly rising as high as Y82.00. At 0450 GMT, the dollar was at Y80.97 compared with Y81.01 late Monday in New York. The euro was at Y115.12 compared with Y115.23.\par \par \uc2 Still, dealers said that the yen could begin strengthening again on any flare-up in risk aversion. That could come if the problems with Japan's Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant intensify or problems arise with coalition air strikes against forces loyal to Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy minister said Tuesday that nuclear power would remain the primary source of energy for the Japanese economy despite concerns over safety after problems at the quake-hit Daiichi plant.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.4216 at 0450 GMT from $1.4225 late Monday in New York. The ICE Dollar Index, which compared the greenback to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the yen and euro, was at 75.440 compared with 75.398.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                                     2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par Dollar Rates \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              80.96-98   81.00-02   -0.05  81.30   80.88    -0.31 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.4215-17  1.4225-27  -0.07  1.4231  1.4201   +6.32 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.6294-97  1.6307-11  -0.08  1.6316  1.6290   +4.52 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      0.9043-46  0.9043-47   0.00  0.9058  0.9034  -15.49 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     0.9782-88  0.9779-83  +0.03  0.9798  0.9769   -2.47 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   1.0056-59  1.0062-64  -0.06  1.0067  1.0035   +2.76 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.7380-84  0.7352-62  +0.38  0.7383  0.7334   -5.23 \par  \par Euro Rate \par  \par EUR/JPY Yen              115.09-13  115.22-27  -0.11  115.52  115.03   +5.99 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Nakamichi, Hiroshi Miwa and Arran Scott contributed to this article. [ 22-03-11 0752GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110322e73m000jb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
