\par\par \b \uc2 DJ WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : UK Pound, Euro Boosted By Rate Expectations -2-\b0\par\par\uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Bradley Davis and Andrew J. Johnson Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The U.K. pound hit its strongest level against the dollar  Tuesday since January 2010 after rising inflation in that country led investors to bet that the Bank of England would soon be forced to increase key rates.\par   The euro also pushed farther above the psychologically key $1.42 level to its highest since November, as tough-on-inflation comments from the region's central bankers also hinted at an impending rate rise.\par   Both left the dollar sidelined, as the easy-money Federal Reserve is increasingly seen as likely to lag other major central banks that are mapping their way to normalizing monetary policy, while the Fed continues down the road of ultralow rates and large-scale asset purchases.\par   "If you look at the major central banks of the world, the ECB [European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] has signaled that it's about to tighten imminently," said Constantine Ponticos, managing director of investment management research in London for Pareto, which manages more than $50 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "The BOE is, I would say, in the eyes of the market losing credibility unless it signals a hike," Ponticos said.\par   Some members of the Fed, meanwhile, are signaling that they are becoming less comfortable with policy meant to prop a U.S. economy whose recovery may no longer need the stimulus of loose policy, "although they haven't signaled any imminent" moves to tighten, he said.\par   The prospects of higher interest rates in the euro zone and the U.K. boost the prospects of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , making them more attractive to hold than the dollar, which is hamstrung by near-zero rates.\par   The Dollar Index traded near a 15-month low.\par   Dallas Fed President Richard Fisher in an interview Tuesday with Dow Jones Newswires said he is likely to be among the first Fed officials to press for tighter monetary policy when the time comes, warning that inflationary pressures are building.\par   Fisher, known as one of the Fed's most strident anti-inflation hawks, signaled he is weary of the Fed's crisis programs, which have pushed the size of the Fed's balance sheet to well in excess of $2 trillion.\par   For now, though, Fisher is among the lone voices within voting members of the Fed's policymaking committee that is warning about continued loose policy; the euro-zone and U.K., on the other hand, are likely to raise rates soon.\par   The U.K. reported inflation, as measured by the consumer price index, increased 4.4% in the 12 months to February after rising 4% in the year to January. That is a bigger rise than most economists were forecasting and well above the BOE's 2% annual target. Inflation has been more than one percentage point above the bank's target for more than a year.\par   The case for a gradual U.K. rate increase was underscored Tuesday by Andrew Sentance, a long-time policy hawk on the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee.\par   "With inflation running very considerably above its target level and set to rise further, failure to take timely monetary-policy action risks a more abrupt and destabilizing rise in interest rates in the future," Sentance said.\par   The U.K. pound gained nearly 0.5% on the dollar on rate-increase expectations.\par   European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials, meanwhile, continue to hint that the euro zone could see higher rates as soon as next month, helping the euro move above $1.42; the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encountered technical resistance at those levels and fell slightly against the dollar by late afternoon trading, but was still considered to be on an upward path.\par   The dollar stayed near the Y81 level as traders remained reluctant to provoke more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets from a Group of Seven nations-led coalition of central banks. With the estimated size of last Friday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's rise being well below the size of last September's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , more efforts can't and shouldn't be ruled out, analysts said.\par   "Our interpretation is that the signaling effect from 'coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' did most of the job for the [Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ] in the short run," said Nomura Securities analysts. "Nevertheless, we judge that the BOJ is likely to be ready for more-persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , should they be needed."\par   Late Tuesday, the euro was at $1.4195 from $1.4226 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.95 from Y81.01, while the euro was at Y114.95  from Y115.25. The U.K. pound was at $1.6378 from $1.6311. The dollar was at CHF0.9036 from CHF0.9046.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 75.496 from about 75.401. -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Brian Blackstone and Nina Koeppen in Frankfurt contributed to this article.\