\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Equities Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par ALLIES SPAR AS LIBYA AIR STRIKES RAGE ON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and its allies are working to expand the protective shield in the skies over Libya, while political skirmishing broke out over who would take command of the continuing international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS RISE SLIGHTLY AS INVESTORS REASSESS RALLY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks edge higher as bullish investors pause to reassess a three-day rally that's helped the stock market recoup some of the losses triggered in recent weeks by unrest in the Middle East and Japan's earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TROUBLED JAPAN NUCLEAR PLANT RECONNECTED TO GRID \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . says that external power has been reconnected to all of six reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, in modest gains by Japanese authorities to contain a crisis at heavily damaged facilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GENERALS REVOLT IN YEMEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A contingent of Yemen's key military commanders defect to the political opposition, the most significant challenge yet to the ability of the country's president, a U.S. ally against al Qaeda, to hold on to power.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WARNS OF FURTHER YEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities pumped cash into the banking system up to record levels and are now warning of more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen in their efforts to ease financial strains that could hamper recovery from natural disaster.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL FALLS AS JAPAN APPROVES RESERVE RELEASE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures slip after Japan approved the release of oil reserves, though tensions in the Arab world kept markets on edge, as some fear unrest could spread to major oil producers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WALGREEN PROFIT UP 10% AS SALES RISE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fiscal 2Q profit at the largest U.S. drugstore chain rises 10% to $739 million, or 80c a share, as core earnings climb more than analysts expected due to higher front-end and prescription sales at comparable stores. Shares fall 5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  DIGS IN FOR A FIGHT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2 's deal to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $39 billion is as much a wager on the political environment in Washington as it is a bet on the future of the wireless market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC LIKELY TO HIKE INTEREST RATES, RESERVE REQUIREMENT Q2 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely raise benchmark lending and deposit rates by 25 basis points each in the second quarter, with the hike coming as early as April, a survey of 10 economists by Dow Jones Newswires shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR GENERAL 4Q NET DOUBLES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The retailers of low-priced goods sees higher traffic and transactions lead to profit more than doubling to $222.5 million, or 64c a share, topping analysts' estimates 59c a share, as sales climb 9.4% to $3.49 billion. Shares rise 4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S FISHER OPPOSES EXTENDING QUANTITATIVE EASING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\f28 \uc2 \f2  President Richard Fisher opposes any extension of the Fed's asset purchase program after June, saying inflationary pressures are building and no added accommodation is needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par   ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS \par Watching The Dollar Fall Behind The Curve \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Events in Japan and the Middle East have dominated markets over the past few weeks. But as these immediate fears begin to subside, global inflation concerns are returning and the dollar has been found wanting, Nicholas Hastings writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par US Banks Oppose Tighter Money Rules \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even as governments freeze assets tied to regimes in Libya, Egypt and Tunisia, U.S. banks are resisting efforts to tighten international rules to prevent the flow of money from corrupt politicians.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   ============ U.S. MARKETS ACTION =========== \par DJIA              down 8.82 points to 12038.05 \par NASDAQ            down 2.66 points to 2689.79 \par S&P 500           down 1.59 points to 1296.82 \par 10-year T-note    102 10/32 at 3.3482 yield \par NYMEX Crude       down $0.39 at $101.94/bbl \par Dollar/Euro       down 0.0019 at 1.4186 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-22-11 1000ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5030 55101   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110322e73m000bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
