\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi +0.3%; Foreigners Buy For Fourth Straight Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is up 0.3% at 2009.82, rising modestly in early trade and tracking strength in U.S. shares, while foreigners extend their buying for a fourth straight session. But Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo expects the Kospi to move sideways from here amid a lack of further momentum, with a focus on foreigners' moves and tech shares' performance; "the index was around the 2000 mark when it started pricing in the Middle East (and North Africa) tensions, so the Kospi is pretty much done recouping recent losses." Tech plays are mixed so far, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 0.2% at KRW888,000 and \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) up 2.0% at KRW104,000. Stocks of securities firms are up on bargain hunting, while airline company stocks underperform, hurt by higher oil prices. \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2  (005940.SE) rises 1.2% to KRW21,100, \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE) gains 1.3% to KRW23,000, while \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) slips 0.5% to KRW60,100 and \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) is flat at KRW9,430. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is lower at 1,121.50 from 1,124.90 late Monday in Seoul on the back of the Kospi's 0.4% rise. Strong gains in Japan stocks (the Nikkei is now up 3.7%) suggest easing fears over the nuclear issues at Fukushima and improved risk appetite, which is a positive for KRW, traders say. There's some USD demand from importers, but "dollar sellers are firmly capping the pair's attempt to rise above 1,122," says a local bank trader. He tips a 1,117-1,124 range for the day with the pair keeping its downward bias. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP may fall, tracking the USD's weakness vs the EUR overnight, while expectations of an interest rate hike support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a local bank trader. The pair may test 43.40 support after settling at 43.55 in late Asian trade Monday; next resistance is eyed at 43.70. The median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones projects the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas lifting overnight rates by 25 bps Thursday. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is lower at 3.0310 from 3.0330 late Monday on USD weakness, says a local brokerage dealer. "The tensions in Libya don't seem to have much impact on the ringgit and chances are pretty good that the pair may test the recent low of 3.0280," says the dealer; he tips the pair's upside to be capped at 3.0450 as "people are keeping an eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to see if the BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb the yen's rise, which could lead to a stronger USD." (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is likely to edge lower on recovering risk appetite, largely because the nuclear crisis in Japan appears to be under control; a trader from a foreign bank tips the pair to hover in a narrow 29.45-29.60 band, compared to 29.572 late Monday. But he adds "since the pair didn't react much during the height of the nuclear crisis last week, I don't think it's going to fall a lot." He expects 29.30 is likely to be very strong support for this week at least. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0026 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may rise after the Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed 1.5% Monday, also as Japan's Nikkei rises 3.5% in early trade, in a sign that fears over a catastrophe at the Fukushima power plant are receding. Monday, the STI closed up 1.6% to 2983.51. AmFraser technical analyst Najeeb Jarhom notes Monday's "spirited recovery" in late afternoon trade, following diminishing Japanese nuclear threat and amid Allied air strikes on Libya, "has to be sustained to around 3040-50 in the next week or two to confirm a significant floor at Friday's late 2920 intraday low." He notes the market rallied strongly when the U.S. launched air strikes on Iraq in 1991 and again in 2003, marking major bottoms, "and investors may be counting on a repeat of this pattern." Watch for gains in rigbuilders after \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) says one of its units secured a US$450 million contract from Seadrill to build a harsh-environment jack-up rig. SIA (C6L.SG) may be in focus after it suspends some Tokyo flights. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is likely to open higher, extending Monday's gains, taking a cue from firmer U.S. stocks overnight, says a local brokerage dealer. "With concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis subsiding, sentiment is expected to continue to improve," says the dealer; she tips the index to test immediate resistance at 1515, which is the 100-day moving average, followed by 1525, the 50% Fibonacci Retracement of 1474 to 1576. The dealer says Sarawak stocks such as \uc2 Hock Seng Lee\uc2  (6238.KU), Cahya Mata Sarawak (2852.KU), \uc2 Naim Holdings\uc2  (5073.KU) and Dayang (5141.KU) are likely to continue to attract interest in the run-up to the Sarawak state polls, likely to be held next month. The KLCI ended up 0.3% at 1508.88 Monday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is lower in early Asian trade as encouraging news on Japan's fight to contain its nuclear crisis boosts sentiment on Asian growth as well as demand for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a trader with a foreign bank says. "There's a general improvement in risk appetite on news from Japan, and this has a positive effect on the Singapore dollar and across Asia. I think Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are going to trade heavy today." The USD/SGD may breach its all-time low at 1.2637; the trader tips support at 1.2630, with resistance pegged at 1.2700. The pair is last at 1.2661, after reaching an offshore low of 1.2655, compared to 1.2680 late Asia Monday. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead June JGB futures open lower, following weakness in U.S. Treasurys and gains in the Nikkei (+3.7%); the contract is currently down 0.24 at 139.50. While "unwinding of flight-to-quality" is seen in the JGB market, concerns over economic impact of the earthquake and a subsequent nuclear crisis, as well as the BOJ's ample fund provision are likely to limit downside in JGBs, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. 10-year cash bonds are yet to change hands; a 1.215%-1.240% range is tipped vs Friday's 1.210%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is 0.6% higher and is likely to maintain the rise as bargain hunters move in on bluechips, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson. "The market is recovering very solidly on the back of overseas markets," he says. Bluechip Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) is up 0.6% at NZ$8.78, with Telecom (TEL.NZ) up 1.3% at NZ$2.01 and \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ) up 0.9% at NZ$5.79 as they move off recent lows due to bargain hunting. Williamson adds there is "some cash coming out of the banks" as interest rates are expected to remain low and this is supporting high-yielding stocks such as \uc2 Goodman Property Trust\uc2  (GMT.NZ), up 1.1% at NZ$0.93. The NZX-50 is at 3368.69. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.6% at 4669.9 after hitting a 7-day high of 4677.0 on broad-based gains following a 1.5% rise in Wall Street's S&P 500. Financials are generating most of the strength, with major banks up 0.8%-1.1%. Materials and industrials are also mostly positive, with \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 0.5%, Fortescue (FMG.AU) up 2.0% and Leighton (LEI.AU) up 2.1%. Oil and gas producers are underperforming, with Woodside (WPL.AU) down 0.6% after oil prices cooled slightly from Asian highs reached Monday. However, uranium stocks have continued to recover, with Paladin (PDN.AU) up 2.2% and \uc2 Extract Resources\uc2  (EXT.AU) up 7.5%. The focus now turns to the Japanese share market opening, where strong gains are possible following progress toward stabilizing Japan's nuclear crisis while markets there were closed for a long weekend. "The response to Wall Street's rise has been muted, but corporate activity has accounted for some of the U.S. strength," says Macquarie Private Wealth investment adviser James Rosenberg. "The risk of a worst-case scenario in Japan has receded. There's a lot of focus on the Japanese share market opening today." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW may fall from 1,124.90 late Monday in Seoul following the rise in the EUR/USD overnight amid anticipation for an interest rate hike by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With worries over the damaged nuclear facilities in Japan subsiding, a recovery in investors' risk appetite may continue to help raise demand for the KRW, participants say. A local bank trader tips a 1,117-1,125 range for the session; "if the Kospi shows a sound performance, the pair is expected to fall below the 1,120 level." But uncertainties persist relating to the political unrest in the Middle East and north Africa and its impact on oil prices, so this may limit any fall in the pair, participants say. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-11 2044ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
