\par\par \b \uc2 DJ News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2   DEAL IS KEY TEST FOR REGULATORS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  's proposed $39 billion acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  is as much a wager on the political environment in Washington as it is a bet on the future of the U.S. wireless market. \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares rise 1%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  ADS's jump 11%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCKS CLOSE HIGHER, LIFTED BY DEAL ACTIVITY, ENERGY SECTOR \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average climbs back above 12000, boosted by \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  's deal to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  for $39 billion and a jump in energy stocks as oil prices leap. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US EXISTING-HOME SALES FALL 9.6 % IN FEBRUARY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Existing home sales in the U.S. decline by 9.6% in February to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 4.88 million, and prices fall to the lowest level in nearly nine years, indications that the market remains depressed. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ATTACKS AS ALLIES REMAIN DIVIDED ON LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and its allies launch new missile strikes against Col. Moammar Gadhafi's forces and say they'll expand the no-fly zone over Libya, as coalition officials work to ease divisions about the course of the military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  there. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PROGRESS AT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NUCLEAR PLANT IS SLOW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  continues to report progress in restoring order at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear reactors, but finishing the job is turning out to be a painstaking process plagued by damaged equipment and unexpected incidents. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TREASURY\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO START WINDING DOWN  MBS PORTFOLIO \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department plans to start selling off a $142 billion portfolio of mortgage-backed securities purchased at the height of the financial crisis, winding down a program credited with helping the housing market to stabilize. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL RISES AS AIR STRIKES SPARK SUPPLY WORRIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures settle higher as air strikes by the U.S. and its allies in Libya raise concerns about extended oil-supply disruptions amid growing regional tensions. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  April crude settles $1.26, or 1.3%, higher at $102.33 a barrel. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ANADARKO, \f28 \uc2 \f2 KOREA NATIONAL OIL\f28 \uc2 \f2  IN JOINT VENTURE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. oil company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anadarko Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it has signed a $1.55 billion joint-venture agreement with state-run \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korea National Oil Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  ., or KNOC, for one-third of its Maverick Basin assets in southwest Texas. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CITIGROUP\f28 \uc2 \f2  SETS 1-FOR-10 REVERSE SPLIT, 1c DIVIDEND \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banking giant will execute a 1-for-10 reverse stock split in May and reinstate its dividend with a one-cent quarterly payment in the second quarter, its first payout to shareholders since 2009. Shares fall 1%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KEYCORP\f28 \uc2 \f2  , SUNTRUST BORROW TO REPAY TARP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A firmer tone across credit markets is allowing U.S. companies to borrow nearly $6 billion in new deals, including two banks seeking financing to pay back government-bailout funds: \f28 \uc2 \f2 KeyCorp\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SunTrust Banks\f28 \uc2 \f2  . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Analysis \par YEN COULD OFFER VALUE, BUT CAUTION WARRANTED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, there are still some opportunities -- albeit rife with risk -- to profit from a run-up in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Bradley Davis writes. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Big Picture \par HIGHER INFLATION EXPECTATIONS ARE SPREADING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects higher price pressures to be "transitory," a new survey of finance professionals by \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  shows core inflation expectations are rising around the world, writes Kathleen Madigan. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Heard on the Street \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2   DEAL COULD BOOST \f28 \uc2 \f2 VERIZON\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Verizon CEO Ivan Seidenberg may feel a bit better about stepping down at the end of the year in the wake of \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  's deal to buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile\f28 \uc2 \f2 , writes Martin Peers, as the deal arguably makes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Verizon Wireless\f28 \uc2 \f2  the winner in the near term. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Taking Stock \par JUMP NOT SUPPORTED BY STRONG VOLUME DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even the rosiest days on Wall Street have their thorns. A high \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Arms Index reading was a subtle sign suggesting the stock market's rally today wasn't quite as strong as it appeared on the surface. Tomi Kilgore explains. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCKS CLOSE HIGHER FOR 3RD STRAIGHT SESSION, AIDED BY DEAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares end higher, lifted by \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  deal and energy companies. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TREASURY\f28 \uc2 \f2  PRICES FALL FOR THIRD STRAIGHT DAY; FEARS ON JAPAN CALM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasurys drop as Japan worries ease a bit and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  announces MBS sales. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DOLLAR FALLS TO 15-MONTH LOW AS RISK APPETITE RECOVERS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar falls to its lowest level in 15 months against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE GAINS AS AIRSTRIKES RAISE SUPPLY WORRIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures rise as air strikes against forces in Libya raise supply worries. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GOLD GAINS AMID LIBYA, JAPAN UNCERTAINTY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comex gold rises amid continued uncertainty in Libya and Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 21, 2011 18:00 ET (22:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110321e73l005jz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
