\par\par \b \uc2 RCM Global CIO Andreas Utermann; Human tragedy in Japan more grave than foreseeable economic consequences\b0\par\par\uc2 1,250 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par \uc2 ENP Newswire\par \uc2 ENPNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011, Electronic News Publishing. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Release date - 18032011\par \par \uc2 Unless the situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant escalates, RCM is not expecting any long-term influence on global markets .\par \par \uc2 The earthquake and tsunami with their direct and long-term consequences are an immense tragedy for the people of Japan.\par \par \uc2 As to the economic consequences which, according to RCM, a subsidiary of Allianz Global Investors, cannot yet be fully estimated, Global CIO Andreas Utermann has this to say: \u8216\'20\'18Even if the economy of the area affected by the earthquake is considerably smaller than that in Kobe in 1995, the damage caused to the road and rail network, power plants and the entire infrastructure seems to be much more extensive this time.\u8217\'20\'19 Above all, forecasts are made more difficult as a result of the situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant, which could change at any time. However, in the current baseline scenario, RCM continues to expect the effects of the earthquake and the disaster affecting the power plant to be kept within limits, and moderate economic growth is anticipated in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Consequences for economic growth in Japan\par \par \uc2 If a further escalation of the situation at Fukushima can be avoided, Utermann expects the negative consequences of the closure of facilities and supply disruptions to have only a short-term effect on economic growth: \u8216\'20\'18In the second half of 2011, the rebuilding of infrastructure could have a balancing positive effect on Japanese growth\u8217\'20\'19, he says. Also, the temporary disruptions in energy supply and the reduction of base load capability of up to 10 per cent are not insurmountable for a country like Japan, because liquefied natural gas and coal transported into the country by sea would fill this gap in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 Political decision-makers are already at work on a diversity of measures, including an economic package, an increase in liquidity effected by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ), and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from being revalued. In RCM\u8217\'20\'19s overall assessment, these measures are likely to advance reconstruction and recovery.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Because of a strong cyclical dynamism in many parts of the world, additionally supported by a globally expansive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, we expect the effect on growth prospects worldwide to be moderate, and to decrease due to Japan\u8217\'20\'19s geographical distance.\u8217\'20\'19 For the Asian region, to which 55 per cent of Japanese exports go and from which 45 per cent of Japanese imports come, Utermann expects growth to be curbed moderately in the short term, providing the Tokyo region is not hit by more intensive radiation exposure or extensive power cuts.\par \par \uc2 Consequences for monetary policy\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The Bank of Japan reacted quickly and doubled the maximum volume of the asset purchase programme to 10 billion yen (approx. 123 billion US-dollars),\u8217\'20\'19 says Utermann. \u8216\'20\'18It has also appreciably increased the inflow of liquidity into the economy\u8217\'20\'19. As a result of this move, the already supportive monetary policy in Japan will become even more expansive, which should positively affect the recovery after the shock of the earthquake. As for the world\u8217\'20\'19s other central banks, he is not expecting any effects on current policy: \u8216\'20\'18We expect global monetary policy to keep to its basic expansive direction to begin with, despite the restrictive measures recently announced by various central banks.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Consequences for capital markets\par \par \uc2 In retrospect, after similar natural disasters in recent decades, capital markets generally reacted positively to anticipated investments for reconstruction after an initial slump lasting a few months. Good entry possibilities may therefore be bound up with the current lows. Utermann compares the situation with that in 1995: \u8216\'20\'18In contrast to the previous great earthquake in Kobe, when Japan still had to digest the after-effects of the property and capital market bubble, in global share portfolios Japan is currently underrepresented and assessed extremely favourably in relation to its own history.\u8217\'20\'19 In addition, the current price/book ratio in the global context is also low. He does not therefore expect longer-term effects on other stock markets, provided the situation at Fukushima does not escalate and cause any permanent impediments to growth.\par \par \uc2 Inflation to remain under control\par \par \uc2 In the current global economic situation, the pendulum has swung from the deflation to the inflation side, as Utermann had anticipated. The capital markets are continuing to assume gradual monetary streamlining in the emerging markets, and are expecting a corresponding development in Europe, and later also in the USA in the course of the year. The situation in Japan does not change this assessment of monetary policy in Europe and the USA. Utermann believes the situation will remain under control: \u8216\'20\'18Despite strong price pressure and the departure of imported deflation, we are not expecting any runaway inflation in the OECD countries. The continuing slack labour markets and limited wage negotiation potential as a result of globalisation do not suggest this.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The EMU will remain\par \par \uc2 Utermann emphasises his belief that he regards a collapse of the EMU as extremely unlikely. Europe\u8217\'20\'19s politicians realised that if the EMU failed the cost would be greater than the rescue packages for the peripheral countries. The RCM head is therefore convinced that \u8216\'20\'18the most important challenge consists in a long-term solution for reducing debt, which must be supported by the private sector as well.\u8217\'20\'19 This is a complicated process, which was why he was still expecting a difficult period for the euro zone. Until a sustainable solution is found, confidence in the monetary union could still be put to the test a few more times.\par \par \uc2 US debts with the focus on markets\par \par \uc2 Utermann is concerned that political blockades could delay necessary reforms in the USA and therefore move the focus from the European debt crisis to the difficult budget situation in the USA. \u8216\'20\'18With a better demographic starting position and a debt ratio relative to GDP which will still be less than 100 per cent until 2015, the United States still has room to manoeuvre - but that won\u8217\'20\'19t be the case for ever\u8217\'20\'19, he explains.\par \par \uc2 Challenges for emerging economies\par \par \uc2 In the global growth environment, Utermann\u8217\'20\'19s main concern is focused on a possible overheating in the emerging economies. He believes the combination of growth and inflation is a great monetary challenge. Because of relatively high inflation rates, real exchange rates are currently rising rapidly, which according to Utermann furthers the reduction of global imbalances: \u8216\'20\'18Through the regulating effect of exchange rates, imports are becoming more favourable for emerging economies, while import prices rise in industrial countries.\u8217\'20\'19 The experts at RCM see the long-term growth trend as being essentially unbroken. However, various levels of acceleration are expected in individual countries. At the moment, investments in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed income bonds with short-to-medium maturities, and companies in industrial countries whose business is firmly anchored in emerging countries are preferred in terms of participating in the long-term trend.\par \par \uc2 Share selection remains demanding\par \par \uc2 Against the background of various factors of uncertainty in other investment classes, Utermann believes that investors should examine market reversals in the stock markets as entry opportunities with a view to long-term asset growth and from the perspective of old-age provision. Nevertheless, share selection remains challenging, because the markets are accompanied by high volatility in uncertain times. RCM currently prefers dividend-bearing securities above all, and is looking at companies with particular growth opportunities.\par \par \uc2 [Editorial queries for this story should be sent to newswire@enpublishing.co.uk]\par \par \uc2 Dokument ENPNEW0020110321e73l000b8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
